# A Gazetteer of Dongguan

## Section One: Dragon Boat

In 2003, I had just quit my job in Guangzhou. I slung a backpack over my shoulders and jolted my way by bus to the Chuangye Road coach station.

The moment the bus pulled in, I regretted it. Not because it was dirty, but because of that enormous, gray-white, endless flatness. Guangzhou at least had arcades to block the sky for you. Dongguan blocked nothing—the sky pressed straight down, white and glaring, plastered over your face like a dishcloth that hadn’t been wrung dry.

I stood at the station entrance. The wheel of my suitcase caught in a crack between paving stones. I tugged once and couldn’t pull it free. Something seemed stuck in my chest. Not sadness—more the stifling feeling that comes when you arrive in a completely strange new place and find it different from what you imagined, yet feel you have no way back.

Only later did I learn that this stifling feeling was the first embrace Dongguan gave me.

I rented a place in Xinji, Nancheng: one bedroom, one living room, 350 yuan a month. The landlady was a local old woman surnamed Chen. When we signed the contract she said a pile of things in Dongguan dialect. I understood about a third. She was saying “deposit plus two months’ rent,” “utilities separate,” and so on, but the pronunciation was different from Cantonese. Some syllables were especially hard, like pebbles striking sheet metal.

At the time I didn’t know this was Guancheng speech. I only felt that the old woman spoke as if she were quarreling. I was a little nervous—did she dislike me as an outsider? Was she going to cheat me?

Only after living there a while did I understand: that was just how she was. She spoke as if quarreling with everyone. She quarreled with the fish seller at the market, with the air-conditioner repairman, even with her own son. But after the quarreling, if she was supposed to leave the door open for you, she left it open; if she was supposed to bring in your clothes, she brought them in.

For the first week after I moved in, I hardly spoke to anyone. Every day I just walked around Nancheng Pedestrian Street, or went down to the convenience store to buy instant noodles, nodded at the clerk, and left.

The clerk was a girl in her early twenties, very pale, with big eyes, but not much expression on her face. She was from Hunan, surnamed Yang, and had worked in that shop for a year, standing from eight in the morning until eleven at night every day. She didn’t smile not because she was cold, but because she was tired.

One night at half past ten I went downstairs to buy water. I saw her asleep with her head pillowed on the checkout counter, tilted to one side, an old Nokia phone beside her. I stood at the door and hesitated, then didn’t go in. I gently pulled the door shut and left.

That night, lying on the bed in my rented room, I stared at the ceiling. There was a crack running from the light fixture all the way to the corner. I looked at it for a long time and thought the shape of the crack looked like a river.

I thought of my mother. The morning I left, she cooked me a bowl of noodles with two eggs. The yolks she fried were always overdone, hard as stones. She pushed the bowl in front of me and said, “Eat it while it’s hot,” then turned to wash the dishes, her back to me. I knew she was holding back tears. I didn’t dare turn back either. I wolfed down the noodles in three or four mouthfuls, picked up my bag, and left.

Back then I was just over twenty, thought I understood everything, when in fact I understood nothing. I only knew I had to go; I didn’t know where I was going. First I spent a few years doing odd jobs in the secondhand computer market on Shipai West in Guangzhou. Now Dongguan was the endpoint of that “not knowing where to go.”

***

My first face-to-face encounter with Dongguan folk custom came when I was woken up.

It was before the Dragon Boat Festival in 2004.

What woke me was not an alarm clock but a sound from the river. That day I woke at 5:40 and hazily heard a rhythmic “hey—yo—hey—yo—” outside the window.

I thought construction had started on a site. I cursed, turned over, and tried to go back to sleep, but the sound wouldn’t disperse. It came in waves like water, lapping at me again and again. Irritated, I got up and leaned out the window.

On the canal was a dragon boat. A dozen or so people on board, bare-chested, pulling their oars to the drumbeat. The drummer sat at the bow, bald, with a slightly protruding belly, wielding the drumsticks with great vigor.

It was just barely light. A thin mist floated over the river. The drumbeats came through the mist, muffled, like someone beating a cloth-covered drum very far away.

Standing at the window, I suddenly wasn’t angry anymore.

Not because I was moved, but because the scene was too concrete—a dozen people on the river at six or seven in the morning, sweat running down their backs. The oar blades went into the water, came out, went in again. No spectators, no applause, only the drum and their calls to one another.

There were actually a few people standing on the bank, but they were too far away to see clearly.

After that I leaned out the window at that hour every day and watched for about two weeks before I finally worked up the courage to go downstairs and walk to the river.

An old man stood beside me. I tried asking in Mandarin: “Is this training?”

He glanced at me and said nothing. I thought he didn’t understand and felt a little awkward. I was about to leave when he suddenly said, in heavily accented Mandarin, “Dragon boat. Training starts at 6:30 every day.”

Just that one sentence. But for some reason my nose prickled.

Not because it was sentimental. Because it reminded me of the activities I had joined when I was in school—feverish recruitment at the start, and two weeks later no one came anymore. Yet here were these dozen people, at 6:30, before daylight, with no prize money and hardly any spectators, and they came.

Then again, that made sense. In Dongguan, dragon boat paddling is not an “activity”; it is a “duty.” If you belong to this village, you get on this boat. If you don’t, your father will scold you, your grandfather will scold you even more, and the villagers will look at you differently.

I stood by the river for a long time. The drumming stopped. The paddlers brought the boat ashore, clapping one another on the shoulders and laughing. I couldn’t understand what they were saying, but I could understand that relaxation—bodies taut for more than an hour suddenly loosening, like a string given a gentle pluck.

I turned and walked back. Morning light had already spread across the road. The breakfast shop downstairs from my building had just opened, white steam rising from the steamer. I bought a basket of char siu bao, so hot I had to switch them from left hand to right. At the first bite the sugary juice flowed out and dripped onto the back of my hand. It hurt a little, but it was very sweet.

Back downstairs, Chen Ah Po was sitting on a low stool picking vegetables, a bunch of water spinach spread across her knees, tearing off the leaves one by one. She saw me, looked up, and said something. I didn’t understand, but her expression was a little gentler than when we signed the contract—if “gentle” is a word that can be used for someone who talks like she’s quarreling.

I smiled and nodded, said, “Morning, Ah Po.” She gave an “mm” and went on picking vegetables.

I went upstairs, opened the door, and tossed my keys on the table.

The crack in the ceiling was still there. I stared at it for a few seconds, then went to wash my face. I turned on the tap; the water was cold. In the mirror my hair was a mess, and there were faint dark circles under my eyes. I stared at that face for a long time—I had seen this face in Guangzhou too, in that rented room on Shipai West, but back then the person in the mirror had looked ready to run at any moment.

Now this face didn’t seem very different.

Not long afterward, a friend introduced me to a job as a network administrator at a property management company near the entrance to the pedestrian street.

In the days that followed, I was like a wound-up pendulum, swinging between the company and my rented room. Apart from work, I almost never went out. The days passed quickly, so quickly that you couldn’t tell whether you were repeating one day or repeating three hundred.

After the dragon boat drumming sank away, my days in Nancheng were filled with work. By day I fixed network cables and installed systems; at night I went back to the rented room and listened to the occasional boat passing on the canal. Xie Ge was a local friend I made around that time. He was from Chashan, spoke slowly, and every time he invited me out he would say, “Come on, come on, it’s great.”

One weekend in 2005, he said he was taking me somewhere. I went with him to Nanshe. That was the first time I saw that old eight-immortals table.

## Section Two: Clan

I went back to Nanshe Village in Chashan several times afterward on my own. Not on purpose—I went to walk around when I felt stifled. That eight-immortals table, those old people, that smell of old wood mixed with incense ash—it was like a brick placed under my feet. Not high, but solid.

With that brick underfoot, the days didn’t feel so empty.

I had been in Dongguan almost two years, long enough to go from “fresh” to “numb.” Every day I went to work and came home, walking the same roads. Sometimes at night or on weekends I sat in my rented room, opened my computer, and wanted to write something. Write what? Dongguan? It sounded like a place you passed through, not a place where you stayed. Back home in Zhanjiang, I had thought the distant unknown was romantic. After coming here, I found that the distant unknown was just an eight-yuan boxed meal at a fast-food place every night.

That day was a weekend. The sky was a clean blue, sunlight falling on the skin without burning—the kind of light that makes you want to go out. I sat in my rented room, opening and closing the computer, writing three lines in a document and deleting them all. No inspiration.

My phone rang. It was Xie Ge. He said he wanted to take me somewhere. I was free anyway, so I went.

As the car passed through Chashan town, the scenery on both sides of the road began to change. Factories gradually thinned out, replaced by stretches of old houses and old banyan trees. Xie Ge parked at the village entrance.

“Nanshe Village, an ancient village left from the Ming and Qing dynasties, the settlement of the Xie clan.” He pushed open the car door as he spoke.

I followed him in, through a narrow alley. The walls on both sides rose above our heads, leaving only a rectangular strip of sky overhead. The alley floor was paved with stone slabs, some hollowed in the middle—worn down by hundreds of years of feet.

The Xie ancestral hall stood in the middle of the village. Going in, you first came to a courtyard open to the sky, the roofs on four sides enclosing a square of heaven. Sunlight poured down from above and struck the stone steps in front of the main hall. Inside the hall the air had a smell—not the smell of incense exactly, but old wood and incense ash mixed together and warmed by the sun. Old, but not moldy.

Coming out of the ancestral hall, we skirted the main hall and went farther in, then entered a household.

In the main room stood a wooden table.

It was an old-style eight-immortals table, dark brown wood, the surface covered with scratches, the edges worn round and glossy, as if polished by the hands of several generations. There was nothing special on it—a sheet of white paper, a ballpoint pen, a stainless-steel teacup with dark brown tea stains built up on the inside. At one corner lay a plastic folder with the edge of a stack of papers sticking out.

Several old men were gathered around the table. Three, no, four. One sat on the inner side, wearing a gray polyester shirt buttoned to the top, sleeves rolled to the middle of his forearms, revealing forearms with prominent veins. He was smoking, not one drag after another, but letting the cigarette burn between his fingers and taking an occasional pull. Another stood across the table, holding the form and reading it, lips moving as if he wanted to say something but couldn’t get it out. Another sat to the side, saying nothing, fiddling with a ring of keys.

They were writing something. I leaned in. It was a form, written in black ballpoint, with columns for names and amounts. The names were written in the careful, stroke-by-stroke way of brush calligraphy, but in ballpoint they came out a little shaky; some characters were crooked and had been traced over. The numbers in the amount column were steadier, like a hand that had kept accounts for many years.

Xie Ge said quietly, “Collecting the Qingming ancestral worship fees.”

I stood nearby and didn’t leave. The old men didn’t shoo me away or pay me any attention. Occasionally one looked up and glanced at me, with neither curiosity nor rejection in his eyes—just “oh, someone’s here”—and then went back to looking at the form.

They seemed to be discussing something.

“Is Ah Qiang coming back this year?” asked the one smoking.

“No,” said the one reading the form. “He’s over in Huizhou. The factory is rushing orders.”

“Then his share still has to be paid. Is his father here?”

“Here. His father says he’ll pay for him.”

“The one hundred eighty-three yuan left over from last year, is it still with you?”

“Yes. I’m keeping track.”

“Fifty per household this year—is that enough?”

“Enough. More people are coming back this year. Just from Shenzhen, seven or eight are coming.”

“Ah Ming’s grandson is one month old now, right?”

“One month old, yes. The sixteenth of last month.”

“Then add one share.”

I stood nearby listening. Their tone as they discussed these things was very ordinary—not the tone of a meeting discussing important matters, just very everyday, like discussing what vegetables to buy today. When they got to “Ah Ming’s grandson is one month old,” the old man fiddling with the keys even smiled and said, “That kid cries with a voice loud as anything, exactly like his father when he was little.”

Several people around an old table, discussing who had come back and who hadn’t, whether the money was enough, whose family had a new baby—things like this had probably happened at this table countless years ago, in more or less the same tone, with more or less the same expressions.

In this city, everything was new. New apartment blocks, new malls, new factories. Even the trees planted along the road were newly planted, their trunks still tied to supporting stakes. I always felt the ground beneath my feet was empty, as if I were stepping on a thin board with nothing underneath.

But here it was different. That table was old, those faces were old, the things they discussed were old. Old things were becoming rarer and rarer in this city. They made you feel that some things did not change. Even if you changed everything, even if you hadn’t yet decided what you were going to eat tomorrow—this place was still here. This table was still here. These people were still writing names and amounts on white paper with a ballpoint pen.

Later I chatted a bit with one of the old men—the smoker. His surname was Xie, he was seventy-three, and before retirement he had been a primary school Chinese teacher. He put out his cigarette, pressed the butt into an iron tin at the corner of the table, and asked me, “You’re not from Dongguan, are you?”

I said no, I had come with a friend to look around.

He said “oh,” then smiled, showing a set of teeth not quite even but very clean. “Good to look around. Look more. A lot of people come, take photos, and leave. They don’t know what’s inside.”

As if possessed, I asked a question I shouldn’t have asked: “Teacher Xie, now that the young people have all gone out to work, who will manage these ancestral hall matters in the future?”

He glanced at me and didn’t answer right away. He picked up the stainless-steel teacup and took a sip; another layer was added to the tea stains on the inside. Then he said, “My grandson works in Shenzhen, in computers. No matter how busy he is, he comes back every Qingming. If he didn’t come back, his father wouldn’t agree, and I would agree even less.”

He paused, then added, “My grandson set up a QQ group with the computer, called the ‘Clan Group.’ Whose family has a wedding, whose old person has passed away, when we assemble for Qingming, how much we collect this year—it’s all discussed in the group. He taught me how to use it. I can use it now too. There are dozens of people in the group, in Guangzhou, in Shenzhen, all there.”

“You can type?”

“No, I use a handwriting pad.”

I was stunned for a moment.

I had originally assumed that ancestral hall affairs were something opposed to modern life. You either stood inside the wall guarding the old things, or you stood outside the wall moving forward. But here was this old man, seventy-three years old, a retired Chinese teacher, using a QQ group for clan affairs. He wasn’t “sticking to the old”; he was using the handiest tool at hand to maintain something he considered important. Just as people hundreds of years ago used brushes to write names in genealogies, now people used ballpoint pens on white paper, and young people used QQ to notify one another in groups—the tools had changed, the matter had not.

This was not conservatism. This was a way of living, a way of remembering.

When we left Nanshe, the sun was already slanting west. I came out of the ancestral hall and walked back along that narrow alley. The light in the alley had turned a warm yellow, falling from the rectangular strip of sky overhead onto the blue-brick walls. At one doorway sat a small stool with a winnowing basket on it, some kind of vegetable drying inside. I sniffed—dried radish.

At the village entrance I saw a young mother. She was pushing a baby stroller; the child inside was crying, a weak, hoarse cry like a kitten. The young mother bent over, coaxing the child in Dongguan dialect. Her voice was not loud, the tone soft, the ends of sentences sinking, like a leaf slowly settling to the ground. I couldn’t understand what she was saying, but I recognized the tone.

That tone was like my grandmother’s. My grandmother was from Guangxi. When I was little and she coaxed me to sleep, she used this same tone—not singing, just talking, but every word carried warmth. Back then I didn’t understand what security was, but now I know: security is having someone speak to you in that tone, making you feel that even if the sky fell, someone would catch it.

The young mother coaxed for a while, and the child stopped crying. She straightened up and pushed the stroller out of the village entrance. The Dongguan dialect dispersed into the air, and the “glug-glug” of the stroller wheels on the concrete road slowly faded. I stood there without moving.

Not out of sentimentality. Because I felt that perhaps I too could be remembered here, that perhaps there could be a tone to catch me. Not now, maybe later. Maybe many years later.

On the way back that day, Xie Ge asked me, “How was it? Same as always, year after year?”

I said, “Yeah.”

Outside the car window, Dongguan’s evening was the same as ever: traffic, streetlights, construction sites, the fluorescent glow of convenience stores. But something inside me had changed. That feeling of “the ground beneath my feet is empty” had lightened a little. It was as if I had stepped on something solid.

After coming back from Chashan, it was as if a brick had been placed under my feet, and the days were no longer so empty. Not long after Qingming, a colleague from Houjie, Fang Yuan, invited me to his home for dinner. Mother Fang brought out a plate of Daojiao zongzi. Fang Bo had gone to Daojiao three times to learn how to wrap them; on the third trip he helped someone wash dongye for an entire afternoon. I hadn’t expected that after an old ancestral-hall table, a dinner table would be waiting for me.

## Section Three: Daojiao Zongzi

Not long after Qingming, a colleague from Houjie invited me to his home for dinner. His surname was Fang, given name Yuan. He was a local, and his family ran a mold factory.

His home was in Houjie, a four-story self-built house with beige ceramic tiles on the exterior and a black Toyota parked at the door. When I went, I brought a bag of fruit. I rang the doorbell downstairs, and Fang Yuan opened the door. He was wearing a gray T-shirt, the collar a little misshapen, probably from too many washes. He took the fruit and said, “You didn’t need to bring anything, come in and sit,” then turned and headed for the kitchen. I stood at the door changing shoes and smelled a strong spice aroma—star anise, cassia bark, five-spice powder mixed together, drifting over from the kitchen.

Mother Fang was busy in the kitchen. Fang Bo was watching TV in the living room, the volume turned very low; it was the Cantonese news on TVB Jade. I sat on the sofa, not quite sure where to put my hands. Fang Bo didn’t talk much and smiled even less. He answered whatever I asked and said nothing otherwise. The atmosphere was a little stiff.

It wasn’t until Fang Yuan came out of the kitchen with a plate of cut fruit that my awkward situation eased.

Dinner was laid on the table, and it was a feast. White-cut chicken, roast goose, steamed fish, stir-fried gai lan, and a pot of old hen soup—Mother Fang said it had been simmering for three hours. I buried my head and ate; Fang Yuan buried his head and ate; Fang Bo buried his head and ate; Mother Fang occasionally said, “Eat more.”

Halfway through the meal, Mother Fang brought out a plate with several zongzi on it.

She said they were Daojiao zongzi. Triangular, the leaves dark green, still bearing traces of having been boiled, the veins clearly visible. I picked one up, peeled off the leaf, and the glutinous rice inside was amber-colored, wrapped around egg yolk, pork belly, and mung bean paste. As the steam rose, the fragrance burst out. I took a bite: the rice was soft and glutinous but not mushy, the fat from the pork belly had seeped into every grain, and there was a very subtle balance between salty and sweet—not the “uniform flavor” of the ones sold outside, but a flavor adjusted by someone’s hand.

I said, “This zongzi is delicious.”

Fang Bo didn’t look up and kept shoveling rice into his mouth.

Mother Fang said, “He went to Daojiao to learn from someone. Went three times before they would teach him.”

I put down my chopsticks and looked at Fang Bo. He was still looking down, but his chopsticks paused for a moment.

“Why did it take three trips before they would teach him?” I asked.

Mother Fang set her chopsticks on her bowl and began to tell the story.

“The first trip, they didn’t know him and wouldn’t teach him. The second trip, he brought two bottles of Moutai. They took the liquor but still wouldn’t teach him.”

“And the third trip?”

“The third trip he carried a basket himself and went to help them wash dongye for an entire afternoon.”

Dongye. Only then did I learn that the leaves used for Daojiao zongzi are not ordinary zongzi leaves but dongye—winter leaves. Washing dongye is not skilled work, but it is very time-consuming.

“He washed leaves there all afternoon,” Mother Fang said. “They saw that he was ‘sincere,’ so they taught him.”

“And when they taught him, they also said—‘After you learn, don’t sell them. You can eat them yourself.’ Your Fang Bo agreed. That’s why the zongzi he makes are never for sale, only for giving to friends.”

I turned to look at Fang Bo.

He kept his head down, shoveling rice, his ears a little red.

I remembered that scene for many years. A man in his fifties, running a mold factory, with a layer of hard calluses on his fingers—from gripping pliers, and also from wrapping zongzi. In business he was probably the sort who said something and meant it; local entrepreneurs mostly are like that—negotiating contracts, managing workers, entertaining clients, making decisions. Every day he made “decisions,” not “requests.” But in order to learn to wrap zongzi, he was turned down twice by an old man he didn’t know, and on the third trip he went to help wash leaves for an entire afternoon.

This was nothing remarkable. No one would write it into a motivational story. No one would be moved enough by it to post it on social media. But it was the kind of thing you do only when you truly care about something.

What he cared about was not the zongzi. Or rather, the zongzi was only a vessel. What he cared about was the process of “learning something”—not bought with money, not exchanged through status, but one person squatting beside another, using an afternoon to prove “I am serious,” and then the other person saying, “All right, I’ll teach you.” The weight of that process was much greater than the zongzi itself.

After the Dragon Boat Festival that year, Fang Bo also taught me how to wrap zongzi.

That was around the Dragon Boat Festival the following year. He called me and said, “Ah Wu, come to my house, I’ll teach you to wrap zongzi.” I went. He had already set everything out in the courtyard—a basin of soaked glutinous rice, a basin of seasoned pork belly, a bowl of mung bean paste, a stack of softened dongye, a pair of scissors, and a ball of cotton string.

I asked curiously, “Fang Bo, don’t Daojiao zongzi use bamboo leaves?”

Fang Bo said, “There are two kinds. I like dongye. Dongye zongzi are more fragrant.”

He demonstrated first. He took two leaves, stacked them together, folded them into a funnel, put in a spoonful of rice, then egg yolk and meat, then another layer of rice, folded the leaves over to seal, and tied the cotton string around twice to tighten. His movements were quick, about ten seconds per zongzi, and the finished ones had sharp corners, as if measured with a ruler. It looked simple enough.

I followed his steps, but my hands wouldn’t obey. The leaves went limp in my hands and wouldn’t fold into a funnel. Rice kept leaking out. I wrapped the string around three times and it was still loose. After about half an hour, there were four zongzi on the table, each a different shape—one like a pillow, one like a steamed bun, one completely unrecognizable, and one that had fallen apart during wrapping, with rice scattered across the table.

Fang Bo watched from the side and didn’t laugh at me. He only said, “Pull the string tight. Use your hands with force.”

When Mother Fang took them to boil, two of mine still fell apart. When they were fished out, the cotton string had loosened, the leaves had opened, and the glutinous rice and meat had mixed into a pot of water, turning into porridge. Mother Fang scooped them out with a skimmer and couldn’t help laughing. She said, “Forget it, forget it, we’ll have it as a midnight snack.”

Fang Bo ate one that hadn’t fallen apart. He took a bite, chewed a few times, and said, “Not bad.”

I knew he was comforting me. That zongzi was crooked, the rice hadn’t been pressed firm, the leaves hadn’t been wrapped tight, and the texture after boiling was loose. But he said, “Not bad.”

That night back in my rented room, I took out the one that had fallen apart—one of the two fished out of the pot, its leaves already loose. I put it in a bowl, sat at the table, and ate it bite by bite.

Not because it was delicious. Because—I had wrapped it myself. I had spent half an hour, cut two small nicks in my fingers on the cotton string, and in the end the thing I produced was crooked and had fallen apart during boiling. But it was mine. After you spend time on something, your feeling about it changes. That “change” is hard to explain, but the body knows it. The fingers remember, just as the nose remembers the fragrance of glutinous rice and the tongue remembers the moment the salted egg yolk bursts in the mouth. All of this together weighs much more than the word “delicious.”

Later one day, Chen Ah Po came to collect the rent and happened to see that fallen-apart zongzi on my table—she was still my landlady and occasionally came around the building. When she learned I had wrapped it myself, it became a joke she used on me for quite a long time. Not mockery—she was laughing at my disobedient hands.

I wasn’t annoyed. Because from then on, the words “Daojiao zongzi” were no longer just an item on a menu to me. They were connected to a person, an afternoon, a pair of hands nicked by cotton string—and that, perhaps, was what they were supposed to be.

After the Dragon Boat Festival that year, I discovered something: when you stay in a place long enough, the brick under your feet slowly grows into your flesh. You don’t ask for it; the days grow it for you.

The proof was that I had begun to get angry about a question that had nothing to do with me.

## Section Four: The Banyan Tree

Not long after the Dragon Boat Festival, a renovation notice went up in the neighborhood.

The news was an A4 sheet taped to the glass of the notice board downstairs with clear tape, its corners already curling. The plan said the small square at the entrance was to be refurbished: new paving stones, a pavilion, stone tables and stools, and some new landscaping.

And then, under that old banyan tree, people argued for more than a week.

It was not a dramatic conflict—no fighting, no quarreling loud enough for the neighbors to call the police, nothing that would make the news. It was the everyday, small, impossible-to-assign-blame kind of friction. You see it in any neighborhood every day, but most people choose not to look.

At the center of the argument was that banyan tree.

The tenants said don’t cut the banyan. The tree had been there for many years; its canopy spread wide enough to cover half the square. In summer, old people sat beneath it playing chess, chatting, dozing, and children ran around its trunk. One tenant said, “If this tree is cut down, there won’t be a single patch of shade in the square in summer.”

The owners said the banyan roots had cracked the paving stones. And they had. The aerial roots had burrowed under the paving and pushed the concrete surface up into a network of cracks.

The arguing went on for days. Someone brought up old grievances, saying that last year’s typhoon had broken off a branch and it had smashed a car parked nearby. Someone said the banyan roots went too deep, and if it wasn’t cut soon the building foundation would be affected. Someone else said the foundation was fine, and that the property management just wanted an excuse to contract out the square as a parking lot under the name of “renovation.”

I was just passing by.

Actually, what everyone was really arguing about was not the tree or the pavilion. It was “what this neighborhood is going to look like from now on.”

A local man had a loud voice—“You renters, what do you know?”

I froze for a moment.

Then I quickened my pace and walked on.

He was right. We really didn’t know. We hadn’t lived here ten or twenty years; we couldn’t feel that sense of belonging that says “this is my place.” My perspective would always be that of a passerby—I could see the conflict, analyze the conflict, but I could never truly “feel it as my own.” It’s like looking at someone else’s family photo: you can say “that’s a nice picture,” but you won’t see yourself in it.

That night I lay in bed. There was a crack in the ceiling. Not the crack in the paving under the banyan tree, but the crack in the ceiling of my rented room—extending from the corner like a thin river. I stared at that crack for a long time.

I thought of Chen Ah Po. She lived on the ground floor, over seventy, hair completely white, back a little bent. She said she had lived here more than thirty years, moving from a single-story house into a building, from mud roads to concrete roads. When I occasionally ran into her downstairs, she would nod and smile at me and say, “Back already?” To her, this neighborhood was not “a place to live” but “home.” She knew which tree was planted in which year, which water pipe would clog in which year, what the child in the next building was called and where he went to school. I knew none of these things and could not possibly know them.

And I—I had only rented a room. The lease was signed year by year; when it expired I could leave, and after I left no one would remember me. The crack in the ceiling had nothing to do with me; whether the banyan was cut had nothing to do with me; whether the pavilion was built had nothing to do with me. In this neighborhood I was a shadow—visible, but not remembered.

As a passerby, you don’t feel sad. The advantage of being a passerby is that you can leave at any time; you don’t have to be responsible for a piece of land.

That sentence—“You renters, what do you know?”—was crude, but what lay underneath it was heavy: a person defending his relationship with a piece of land. That relationship cannot be explained in words, but it can be seen in action. He would argue there for a week because he felt this had to do with him.

There is no standard answer to this question. Should the banyan be cut? Should the paving be replaced? Should the pavilion be built? Behind every answer lies decades of entanglement between a person and a piece of land. Some of those entanglements you can see—cracks, roots, paving pushed up—and some you cannot—memory, habit, belonging, a certainty that says “I am here.”

***

It was also that summer that I figured out something: sometimes a city’s sense of belonging is not given by a tree, but by a cup of herbal tea so bitter it numbs your tongue.

I went to the herbal tea shop at the head of the pedestrian street almost every week. The owner’s surname was Huang; she was from Guangxi and had been in Dongguan about twenty years. She sold two kinds of herbal tea: bitter and sweet. I usually drank the sweet one.

Once my throat went hoarse, and she insisted on pushing a cup of Twenty-Four Flavors into my hand. It was so bitter that my tongue went numb and felt like someone else’s tongue. She said, “Bitter medicine cures. What are you afraid of?” Her tone was like my mother’s.

Later, after I got to know her better, I learned that her daughter had been born in Dongguan, gone to school in Dongguan, and now worked as a teacher at a kindergarten in Dongguan. I asked, “Is your daughter a Dongguan person?”

She said, “Her ID card says Guangxi. But she says herself she’s ‘a person who can be anywhere.’”

“A person who can be anywhere”—that phrase stopped me cold for quite a while.

I thought of myself. When I came to Dongguan I felt like a passerby; after a year or two I still felt like a passerby. But now? When my mother called and asked when I was coming home, I said, “When I have time.”

A friend had settled in another city and invited me to visit. Every time I said, “Sure,” but I never went. I had more and more things—books, clothes, pots and pans—the kind of quantity that makes moving a chore.

On my key ring hung the company access card; in my phone were the numbers of three convenience stores that delivered; I knew which rice-noodle shop downstairs had the best soy sauce.

Had I also become “a person who can be anywhere”? Or had I, without realizing it, become a “new Dongguan person”—not on my ID card, but in my heart?

***

I figured this out at the Dragon Boat Festival that year.

I went to the river to watch the dragon boats again.

This time I didn’t watch from far away. I walked straight to the riverbank and stood among the crowd.

When the drumbeats started, I felt something in my chest vibrate with them. Not my heartbeat—the drum. The sound passed through the mist, through the river water, through the clothes on my body, and struck me directly in the chest. Suddenly my eyes went red.

Not for any grand reason. Because I remembered my first day in Dongguan, sitting in the back row of a long-distance bus, smelling the various foods and foot sweat in the cabin, with only one thought in my mind: how can this place be so flat?

And now, standing by this river, looking at those bare-chested paddlers, that bald drummer, those old people on the bank laughing with missing teeth—Dongguan was not flat at all. It had rises and falls, gullies and ravines, things bottomless. It was just that these things were not on the skyline, not in the newspapers. They were under your feet, on the roads you walked every day, in the flicker in someone’s eyes when you spoke to them.

***

Later, after that Dragon Boat Festival, Chen Ah Po called me over to help when she was wrapping zongzi.

Around that time she was always busy alone in the kitchen. When I occasionally went downstairs to buy instant noodles and ran into her, she would say, “Come help me wash the leaves.” I went. She didn’t stand on ceremony—handed me a basin of dongye and a basin of water, and I squatted at her kitchen door washing. She taught me while she wrapped, her hands so fast I couldn’t follow. Mine came out crooked and misshapen; she didn’t criticize them, just said, “Take your time. The first time your Fang Bo wrapped them, they looked worse than this.”

When the zongzi were done boiling, the whole kitchen was steam. Chen Ah Po picked one out for me with her chopsticks. I took a bite and sucked in air because it was so hot, but I couldn’t bear to spit it out.

It tasted different from the zongzi Fang Bo wrapped with dongye.

That taste—it wasn’t anything tongue-blowing. It was just right. Like walking a long way, coming home, and having someone pour you a cup of hot water.

## Section Five: The Flower Lantern

Coming out of Chen Ah Po’s house, the bent Hongshuangxi cigarette was still in my pocket. When she said, “Take your time. The first time your Fang Bo wrapped them, they looked worse than this,” I suddenly felt that I was perhaps no longer so transparent in this city.

But what really made me feel transparent was the flower lantern in Zhongtang.

At the end of 2005 I went to Zhongtang. Old Li dragged me there.

Old Li was a friend of mine who liked to wander around—a Dongguan local, though I never asked exactly which town. Like me, he was a little overweight, wore glasses, and spoke slowly. One day he suddenly sent me a message, just three words: “Come see the lantern.”

I replied: “What lantern?”

He replied: “Flower lantern. Zhongtang. You’ll know when you get here.”

My hometown didn’t have the custom of lighting lanterns in ancestral halls, so naturally I wanted to see what the flower lanterns hung in ancestral halls looked like. I agreed to go.

It was the end of the year, and I didn’t have much on hand. I took a bus from Nancheng to Zhongtang. Along the way the scenery outside the window changed from high-rises to factory buildings, then to factory buildings with occasional fish ponds between them. When I got to Zhongtang, Old Li met me at the stop on a motorcycle and told me to get on the back. The wind poured straight into my collar. I shrank my neck and asked him, “What lantern are we actually seeing?”

He said, “Flower lantern.”

Only when we got there did I learn that it wasn’t a lantern exhibition. It was a Li family making a lantern. They were already building the frame.

I stood in Uncle Li’s courtyard and saw for the first time what such a large flower lantern looked like—or rather, what it looked like half-finished.

Sunlight came down from above the courtyard, and the shadows of bamboo strips fell on the ground, crisscrossing like a net. What the net caught was not light, but time. Standing there, I suddenly felt a little dizzy. Not because it was big, but because of that overwhelming “handmade” feeling.

Uncle Li was just past sixty, very thin, with big hands and slightly deformed knuckles. He picked up a bamboo strip, split it with a knife, very gently, as if unwrapping a bandage from a person. I asked him, “Uncle Li, how long does it take to finish the frame?”

He said, “Four of us together, more than a month.”

“So you make a new one every year?”

“More or less. Someone orders it. This one is for the ancestral hall, for lighting the lantern.”

Only later did I learn that the meaning of this kind of flower lantern in Dongguan went far beyond “looking nice.” It was a symbol of a clan’s prosperity. Whether or not it counted as an item on those intangible cultural heritage lists was, in the end, not so important.

I thought of Uncle Li’s hands. If those hands had been born in another era, they might have spent a lifetime holding a hoe, never holding bamboo strips. And the craft of bamboo strips—if no one had stubbornly kept it up, it might really have disappeared.

I asked Uncle Li, “Are there still young people learning now?”

He didn’t answer directly. He called toward the house: “Ah Jie, come out.”

A young man in his early twenties came out, wearing black-framed glasses and a dark blue jacket, sleeves pushed up to his elbows, looking like a college student who had just finished class. Uncle Li said, “My nephew. Still in school.”

Ah Jie was a little embarrassed, standing there rubbing his hands. I asked him, “Can you make lanterns?” He said, “A little. I learned from my uncle when I was little.”

I turned to look at Uncle Li. He was shaving bamboo strips, didn’t even look up, and said, “What he makes doesn’t have ‘hand feel.’”

“Hand feel”—the first time I heard that word was in Fang Bo’s mold factory. Fang Bo said that no matter how good his machines were, some work still had to be done by hand. Hands have temperature, have lightness and heaviness, have “feel.”

Ah Jie didn’t argue with his uncle. He squatted down, picked up a bamboo strip, and began weaving. His fingers were nimble; the strip flipped back and forth in his hands, and soon he had woven a small ring. I watched him weave and suddenly felt that this scene was very Dongguan—a college student, squatting in a courtyard in the twelfth lunar month, weaving bamboo strips in the most primitive way. He was not “inheriting tradition”; he was using his own way to stay connected to this family’s history.

***

I went with Uncle Li and the others to the ancestral hall to see the old lantern, then walked around the village, and finally had a meal at his house.

Uncle Li’s wife—I called her Aunt Li—didn’t talk much, but she cooked extremely well. She made a dish called “basin cuisine.” Not the several-hundred-yuan-per-basin kind in restaurants, but homemade: radish at the very bottom, then layer upon layer of roast pork, chicken, duck, fish balls, shiitake mushrooms, and bean-curd skin, with white-cut chicken on top. You eat from the top down, and when you reach the bottom, the radish has absorbed all the juices, soft and full of flavor.

When I took my first bite of radish, I almost put down my chopsticks.

Not because it was shockingly delicious. Because the flavor was so thick. Not salty, not umami—thick. Like an old book that has been thumbed through until every page has been touched countless times, the edges frayed, but every character still clear. That radish had been pressed at the bottom for so long, absorbing so many layers of flavor; every bite was an accumulation of time.

I looked up at Uncle Li. He was picking up a piece of roast pork; he bit into it and the skin crackled crisply. His face showed little expression, but he chewed very slowly, as if counting every texture.

This was probably why Dongguan people were so attached to these “old things.” Not because of nostalgia, but because these things contained a kind of “slowness.” In this city everything was accelerating—factory assembly lines, bus schedules, breakfast-shop serving times—and only before these old things could time slow down. You could spend most of a year making a lantern, spend a whole day simmering a basin dish, spend a lifetime guarding an ancestral hall.

This “slowness” was not laziness; it was a kind of resistance. Resistance to what? To the logic that “everything must be fast, everything must be new, everything must be useful.” What use is a lantern? It looks nice hanging up, but it’s only hung once a year. What use is basin cuisine? It’s gone after one meal. What use is a dragon boat? Once you’ve paddled, you come ashore. The “use” of these things is not functional use; it is psychological use—it makes you feel that between you and this land, there is still a thread connecting you.

***

After the meal, it was dark. The streetlights stretched our shadows long, overlapping one by one until you couldn’t tell whose was whose.

Old Li came over and handed me a cigarette.

It was Hongshuangxi, soft pack, the box a little crumpled, probably carried in his pocket for a few days. I didn’t smoke it, but took it and held it between my fingers. Only when my fingers touched it did I realize the cigarette was soft, the one inside probably bent. I twirled it between my fingers and didn’t light it.

Old Li squatted down beside me. That day he was wearing a blue work vest washed so pale it was almost white, two symmetrical dark patches on his shoulders—sweat marks from the straps. He took out his own cigarette, lit it with a lighter, drew deeply, and slowly exhaled. The smoke rings drifted up and were scattered by the evening breeze.

He said, “Tomorrow I’ll take you somewhere else.”

I asked, “Where?”

He said, “Shipai. To see puppets.”

I said, “Puppet theater?”

He said, “Yeah. Shipai puppets are different.”

Then he didn’t explain further. He stood up, patted the dust off his knees, threw the butt on the ground, and crushed it with his foot. Before leaving he looked back at me and said, “Tomorrow at eight. I’ll wait for you downstairs.”

I stood under the streetlight, the bent cigarette still between my fingers. I looked down at it, then put it in my pocket.

I didn’t know what puppet theater was like. But I knew that in this city there were always things worth making a special trip for.

## Section Six: Puppet Theater

After coming back from Tangwei, I often thought of what Master Chen had said. Wood dries out and needs oil; strings loosen and need tuning; joints stiffen and need grinding. When a person stays with an old thing long enough, the old thing grows memory. Only then did I understand that the scenes in the previous sections that made me stop in my tracks—dragon boats, flower lanterns, basin cuisine—were all the same thing, only I hadn’t been able to name it yet.

The next morning at 7:50 I was already waiting downstairs.

Old Li came on a motorcycle with a helmet on the back seat. I put it on and got on. When he twisted the throttle and the bike shot forward, I almost didn’t grab his waist in time.

The road to Shipai took about forty minutes. After leaving the city, the road narrowed, and low houses and rice fields began to appear on both sides. When the wind blew, the whole field of rice rippled as if on water. Sitting on the back, the helmet visor rattling in the wind, I squinted. Old Li didn’t drive fast; the motorcycle puttered along the country road, the engine sound seeming especially loud in the fields and yet especially small—like a pebble dropped into a river.

Tangwei Village arrived.

Tangwei is an ancient village dating from the Ming and Qing dynasties. It is smaller than Nanshe, but laid out differently. Nanshe has deep alleys and dense houses; Tangwei has a walled compound, with a central open courtyard, surrounded by two-story houses, like a bowl turned upside down on the ground. The walls are blue brick; in some places the mortar between the bricks has fallen out, exposing the brick beneath, varying in shade like an old person’s face.

The puppet stage was set up on one side of the courtyard.

The courtyard was square, maybe a few dozen square meters, with the roofs on four sides enclosing a rectangular patch of sky. The stage was not big, set against the eastern wall, about two meters square. A blue cloth served as the backdrop, its edges a little frayed, as if used for many years. In front of the stage were several rows of plastic stools; some had uneven legs and wobbled on the blue-brick ground.

The audience was small, a dozen or so. Most were old people, sitting in twos and threes, holding fans or teacups. A few children ran around in front of the stage. I found a seat toward the back; the plastic stool did indeed wobble, and I shifted my weight to steady it. Old Li went to see a friend and said he’d come over later.

The weather that day was good. Blue sky, white clouds; sunlight leaked down between the roofs of the compound and fell on the stage. In those shafts of light you could see dust floating—not dirty dust, but dust lit by the sun, golden, slowly falling. The light fell on the blue cloth, and the blue faded, as if washed by water.

Behind the stage were two people: one sitting on a low stool, the other standing beside him holding two puppets and adjusting the strings. The puppets were carved from wood; a row of them was laid out near the front—about seven or eight, heads up, bodies down, like a row of seated people. They were painted in the colors of Cantonese opera roles: sheng, dan, jing, mo, chou. I was too far away to see details, but I could tell that the general puppet had two red pheasant feathers on his helmet hanging down over his shoulders; the female-role puppet wore a pink costume with very long sleeves trailing below the knees.

After a while, the play began.

The singer began first. It should have been Cantonese, but the singing style was very old-fashioned, different from the Cantonese I usually heard—the tones drawn out long, some notes rising, some sinking, like crying and yet like laughing. I couldn’t understand the words, but I could hear the emotion: this passage was sorrowful, that passage urgent, another angry. The singer’s voice was no longer young, a little hoarse, but full of breath support; every word carried far, and even the compound walls seemed to help bounce the sound back.

The puppeteer began to move.

He held the puppet with both hands, each string controlling a joint—head, shoulders, elbows, wrists, knees, ankles. His fingers moved, the strings moved, the puppet moved. A general entered on horseback; the puppet’s feet lifted and fell, the riding crop in its hand swung—the crop was made of a thin bamboo strip with a few strands of red silk thread tied to the end. When swung, the threads spread out like the real thing.

The puppet flipped from one side of the stage to the other, spun twice in the air, and landed steadily. It was not a simple flip—you could see the puppet’s body curl, extend, and curl again in the air, like a cat. When it landed, the knees bent slightly, as if a real person were absorbing the impact. Several children below cried “Wow!” One child stood up, pointed at the stage, and shouted “Again, again!” before his grandmother pulled him back down.

Sitting there, I felt the sun make my eyes hot.

Not because the play was good. I couldn’t understand the libretto, didn’t know what story it was telling, and the singing style was too old for me—so old that I couldn’t call it “pleasant.” But my eyes were hot anyway.

I moved forward two rows to see more clearly. Then I saw behind the stage.

The puppeteer—he was probably fifty or sixty, hair graying, cut very short, his scalp shining in the sun. He was almost lying on the stage, upper body stretched forward, both hands holding the puppet, shoulders rising and falling. His back was arched, the bones of his spine protruding beneath his thin undershirt. His head was low, chin almost touching the stage, eyes fixed on every movement of the puppet—not on the strings, but on the puppet itself, as if looking at a person’s face.

He was not “performing.” He was “becoming” that puppet.

When the puppet somersaulted, his waist twisted too. When the puppet landed, his shoulders sank too. When the puppet swung the riding crop, his wrist swung too. His body moved with the puppet’s movements, not by much, but every movement had a response—like a mirror. The puppet did what he did; he did what the puppet did.

I couldn’t tell who was controlling whom.

Was he controlling the puppet—with strings, with fingers, with a feel developed over decades? Or was the puppet borrowing his body to come alive—borrowing his waist to somersault, his wrist to swing the crop, his shoulders to absorb the landing? If the puppet had memory, what it remembered would not be the tightness of the strings, but this person’s body. How many times this person’s waist had twisted, how many times his wrist had swung, how many afternoons his shoulders had sunk.

***

After the play ended, the audience dispersed quickly. The old people stood up slowly, patted the dust off their trousers, and spoke to one another in Dongguan dialect I couldn’t quite follow. The children ran ahead; the adults followed. The plastic stools were stacked against the wall. The courtyard grew quiet again; the sunlight still leaked down, the dust still floated.

I went backstage to find the puppeteer.

Backstage was just the space behind the stage, very small, piled with puppets, spools, and tools. He sat on a low stool, untying the strings from the puppets one by one and winding them onto spools. His fingers were thick, his knuckles twice the normal size—from years of pinching strings. His nails were cut very short, and there was a layer of hard callus on his fingertips.

I squatted down to be at eye level with him. I said, “What is your surname, Master? How long have you been making this puppet?”

He didn’t look up, kept winding the string. The spool turned in his hand with a faint “shasha” sound.

“Surname Chen. The puppet isn’t made,” he said. “It’s ‘raised.’”

He paused, tied off the string, tucked the end into a gap in the spool, then looked up at me.

“My father passed it to me. I’ve raised it for many years.”

“Raised?”

“Yes.” He picked up the general puppet beside him, laid it on his lap, and wiped its face with his thumb—wood, painted, but the paint worn away in places, revealing the pale yellow wood grain beneath. “Wood dries out and needs oil. Strings loosen and need tuning. Joints stiffen and need grinding. Only if you’re with it every day will it listen to you.”

He pointed to the puppet’s wrist joint. There was a copper axle there, running between two pieces of wood. “I’ve replaced this axle three times. The first time my father replaced it, the second time I did, the third time five years ago. Every time after replacing it, it has to be ground for half a month to fit the wood. A new axle is dead; only after grinding is it alive.”

Then he said something I remember to this day:

“A puppet has no life, but it has memory. How you move it today, it will remember tomorrow. If you slack off with it today, it will slack off with you tomorrow.”

I stood backstage looking at the puppets leaning against the wall.

They had no expressions—or rather, their expressions were fixed, carved, sheng, dan, jing, mo, chou, each with a face that never changed. But I felt they were looking at me. Not the horror-movie kind of looking, not eyeballs rolling or pupils contracting, but a quiet, waiting kind of looking. Like a group of people sitting there, saying nothing, but you know they are waiting for you to speak.

That general puppet, the pheasant feathers on its helmet hanging down over its shoulders, looked like it had just finished a battle, was tired, and had sat down to rest. That female-role puppet, its pink sleeves trailing on the ground, looked like it had just been crying and hadn’t yet wiped its face.

They leaned against the wall, waiting for the next performance.

***

When I walked out of the compound, the sun was already slanting west. The shadow of the compound covered half the courtyard. Old Li was waiting at the village entrance, leaning on the motorcycle and smoking. When he saw me come out, he threw the butt away and said, “How was it?”

I said, “Not bad.”

He smiled and said, “Let’s go, time to eat.”

I got on the back, put on the helmet. The engine puttered to life; the motorcycle turned out of Tangwei Village and onto that country road. I pulled down the visor. My eyes were a little hot, but the wind dried them.

Dragon boat—one boat, a dozen people. Flower lantern—Uncle Li and three others working together. Basin cuisine—Aunt Li made it alone, but the ingredients were contributed by every household in the village. Puppet theater—at least two people. Ancestral worship—the whole village.

“Dongguan people don’t like doing things alone,” Old Li had said. “Not because they can’t, but because doing it alone is meaningless. When something is done, there have to be people to look at it together, eat it together, laugh together, argue together—only then is it ‘done.’”

I thought of that night in Zhongtang. The streetlights stretched those people’s shadows long. They didn’t leave together—each went back to his own home—but they had just come out of the same place. This “togetherness” was not organized; it grew. Like the aerial roots of a banyan, hanging down from a branch, touching the ground, taking root, becoming another trunk. What looks like the shadow of one tree is actually a whole grove.

After I got back to Nancheng, I had a few quiet days. Every day I went to work, came home, went downstairs to the convenience store to buy instant noodles. Xiao Yang still didn’t smile much, but sometimes she gave me an extra sausage. Chen Ah Po still occasionally called me upstairs to help wash dongye. Sister Huang’s herbal tea shop was still open, the air conditioning still freezing.

But some things were different.

I began to notice things I hadn’t noticed before. For example, the shoe-repair man at the neighborhood entrance always had a radio beside him, playing Cantonese opera. For example, the woman at the breakfast shop downstairs didn’t wrap zongzi but nuomiji—wrapped in lotus leaves, square, different from the triangular zongzi Aunt Li and Fang Bo made. For example, the old man selling fish at Huangcun Market always said something to me in Dongguan dialect when he gutted the fish. I couldn’t understand it, but the tone was kind.

These things had been there before; I just hadn’t “seen” them. Not that my eyes couldn’t see, but that my heart wasn’t on them.

Now I had been in Dongguan almost three years, and my heart seemed to be slowly settling. Not the solemn kind of “I’ve decided to make my home here,” but the natural kind—your key opens this door, your feet know how to walk this road, your tongue recognizes this taste.

That night I lay in bed and stared at the crack in the ceiling for a long time again. The crack was still the same crack, but now I didn’t think it looked like a river. It looked like a thread. One end connected to the place I came from, the other to the place where I was now. There were many knots in between—each knot a person, an event, a taste, a sound.

The thread was still being woven. I was still in Dongguan.

That winter I went back to Zhanjiang for the New Year. My mother asked how things were in Dongguan. I said, “Not bad.” She didn’t ask again. Early in the new year I returned to Dongguan; the sky was still gray-white. I turned the key to my rented room; the crack in the ceiling was still there. I put down my luggage and felt that the New Year was over.

## Section Seven: Cantonese Opera
When I came back after the New Year in 2006, I carried that thought—the crack like a thread—into spring.
Uncle Zhang was the company’s procurement and admin man, a Daojiao local. It was already past the Lantern Festival, a Friday, and just before quitting time he asked me, “Want to go see a play?”
“What play?”
“Cantonese opera. Daojiao. You’ll know when you get there.” He handed me a work matter along with it: “The billing system at the residential compound over there has some problems. Take a look while you’re at it.”
He sent the address by phone. The next morning I took the bus from Nancheng. When I got to Daojiao, I first went to the property office of the compound under the company’s name and dealt with the work, then hailed a motorcycle to the address he’d given me.
After crossing the bridge, the road narrowed and a fishy dampness entered the air—not stench, but the smell of river water, water weeds, and wet earth mixed together. Daojiao is a water town; canals spread through it like capillaries.
The place was not in some cultural-center building. It was deep in an alley in the town center, converted from an old factory. A white sign with red characters hung at the door, the characters handwritten and so faded they were hard to make out, though traces of “Cantonese Opera Society” could vaguely be seen. Pushing the door open, I was met by a smell of tea mixed with face powder.
A few dozen plastic chairs were set out in the room, about twenty of them occupied. A crude stage stood at the front, covered with a red carpet whose edges had curled up. A man and a woman were singing on stage, an old man playing the erhu beside them, an old woman on the yangqin. They were singing *The Princess Changping*, the part that goes “Falling flowers fill the sky and hide the moonlight.” The woman’s voice was a little cracked, but she sang with force, each word squeezed out from between her teeth. The man’s voice was all right, but his bearing was stiff; when he raised his hand it looked like he was directing traffic.
Standing at the door, my first reaction was a urge to laugh. Not mockery—it was the kind of feeling when you expect to see “art” and instead see a few ordinary people in an old factory earnestly doing something “unprofessional.” The contrast itself had an indescribable flavor.
But the urge to laugh quickly faded.
Because I saw the audience. They sat bolt upright, eyes fixed unblinking on the stage; several old people were softly humming along. One old uncle held a thermos, the lid open, steam curling up, but he didn’t drink—just held it, as if holding up something ceremonial.
When the woman sang the “Fragrant Death” section and knelt, head lowered, someone in the audience began wiping their eyes. Not exaggerated crying—just a gentle dab at the corner of the eye with a thumb. Beside me sat an old woman in a navy-blue padded jacket, clutching a pack of tissues, not pulling one out, just clutching it.
Suddenly I felt my throat tighten.
Not because the libretto was moving. Because of that image—an old factory, a few dozen old people, one erhu, a passage of opera sung for decades—together they had an indescribable “solemnity.” They were not “entertaining themselves”; they were “making an offering” to something. Offering a way of living: the way of living in which, while this city runs desperately forward, some people are still willing to stop, sit down, and listen to a stretch of old opera.
When the section ended, the two on stage came down, and the audience applauded. The applause was not enthusiastic, but very neat—as if one person clapped first, the others followed, the rhythm in unison, then all stopped together.
***
Uncle Zhang took me backstage. Backstage was even smaller, piled with costumes, prop boxes, several thermoses. An old woman sat in the corner removing her makeup. She was probably sixty or seventy, deep wrinkles on her face, but you could still see what she had looked like when young. Uncle Zhang introduced her: “This is Aunt Zhen, the lead. She used to be in the county troupe.”
Aunt Zhen glanced at me and said nothing, went on removing her blush with cotton. I felt a little awkward, stood to one side. Uncle Zhang didn’t rush her either, just waited. After a few minutes Aunt Zhen finished, stood up, took a jacket from the rack nearby and put it on, and only then spoke. Her voice was hoarse. She said, “New here?”
I said no, just looking around. She said “oh” and asked nothing more.
Uncle Zhang said, “Sister Zhen, I’m showing him your costumes.”
Aunt Zhen didn’t refuse, but she wasn’t warm either. She led us to a cabinet and opened it. Inside hung several opera costumes—a python robe, a cape, a folded robe—some already very old, the cuffs frayed, but washed very clean and folded neatly. She pointed to a red cape and said, “This one my mother passed to me. She was in the troupe too.”
I leaned in. The fabric was silk, dark red, embroidered with golden peonies. The embroidery was not especially fine, but the stitches were dense; the flower centers used seed stitch, one bead at a time, raised. I reached out and touched it lightly. The fabric was cool, but the embroidery thread felt a little rough—the trace left by decades of fingers rubbing it over and over.
Aunt Zhen said, “When my mother passed, this garment was draped over her. Later I took it back from the funeral home, washed it, dried it, hung it up. I told my daughter, when I’m gone, this garment goes to her. She said she didn’t want it. I said whether you want it or not, you have to take it—this is your grandmother’s.”
Her tone was very flat, as if saying the weather was nice today. But in that “whether you want it or not, you have to take it” hid an obstinacy that brooked no negotiation.
I asked, “Your daughter doesn’t sing opera?”
“No. She’s an accountant in Dongcheng. She says Cantonese opera is ‘old people’s stuff.’”
She paused, then added, “She’s right. It is old people’s stuff. But old people die too. When we’re dead, what happens to this stuff?”
She didn’t expect me to answer, and didn’t wait for one herself. She closed the cabinet door, turned to pour a cup of tea, and carried it to the window. Outside the window was a wall covered in ivy; new leaves had just come out, tender green.
I stood behind her and suddenly felt this scene was very Dongguan—an old factory, a woman over seventy, a costume passed down two generations, a sentence about “old people’s stuff.” Not some grand cultural narrative, but an ordinary person facing “what happens to this stuff after I die” with a silent, answerless, yet still-choosing-to-sing stubbornness.
***
I stayed at the Cantonese Opera Society until afternoon. During the break everyone gathered around boxed meals. Someone had brought homemade pickles—pickled radish, pickled ginger, pickled cucumber—in plastic containers, passed around. I took a piece of pickled radish; at the bite, the sourness jolted my teeth, and then a sweetness rose from the base of my tongue. Not the sweetness of sugar, but the radish’s own sweetness, forced out by salt and fermented by time.
I chewed and listened to them chat. The topics were scattered: whose grandson got into university, whose spouse was in the hospital, next month’s performance schedule, which costume needed mending. When they talked about opera, their voices got a little louder. One old uncle said, “Young people don’t listen to this anymore.” Another said, “They don’t listen. We sing for ourselves.”
“We sing for ourselves”—said lightly, but it carried weight. It was not surrender; it was self-sufficiency. They didn’t need an audience, didn’t need recognition, didn’t need “transmission.” They only needed—to sing.
In Zhongtang, watching Uncle Li make lanterns, he spoke of “hand feel”; in Shipai, watching Master Chen work the puppets, he said, “A puppet has no life, but it has memory”; now Aunt Zhen said, “We sing for ourselves.” These three sentences were like a hidden thread—these old things in Dongguan don’t need a reason to exist: not for exhibition, not to educate the next generation, not for “cultural preservation,” but for themselves. Doing something for decades—stop? Can’t let go.
“Can’t let go” is the most honest customary motive in this city. I saw it when Fang Bo wrapped zongzi—he’d finish a pot, not eat them himself, give them all away, but still wrap them every year. I saw it by the canal watching dragon boat training—the paddlers got up at 5:30, no spectators, but they still came. I saw it in Uncle Li’s courtyard—bamboo strips shaved one after another, calluses worn into his fingers, and he was still shaving.
On the way back from Daojiao, the wind was light, the sun still warm. Sitting in the back of the bus, I suddenly remembered something.
Last time in Zhongtang, Old Li had asked me, “Ah Wu, tell me—why is it always old people doing the things I take you to see?”
I didn’t answer right away. After a long while I said, “Because young people haven’t reached that point yet.”
“What do you mean?”
“When you’re in your twenties, you think everything is new. You want to run forward, break through, try things, you won’t look back. When you reach forty or fifty, you can’t run anymore, or don’t want to, and you start to think—where did I come from? What do I carry with me? What will I leave behind? That’s when these things come in handy.”
I thought for a moment, then added, “It’s not that young people don’t need them; they haven’t reached the point of needing them. When they do, these things will be there waiting. Provided—someone holds them up until then.”
Aunt Zhen is one of those “someones.” Uncle Li is. Master Chen is. The bald drummer by the canal is. Teacher Xie writing forms in Chashan is. Fang Bo is. Chen Ah Po is. Sister Huang is. The locals arguing over whether to cut the banyan in the neighborhood are, in a sense, too—wasn’t he arguing about “what this place will look like from now on”?
And me? Am I one of those “someones”?
I don’t know. I’m still young, still running. But at least I’ve seen. Maybe one day I’ll become one of those “someones.” Maybe one day I’ll sit in some old factory, holding a thermos, listening to a stretch of old opera, and gently dab at the corner of my eye.
***
## Section Eight: Muyu Songs
After coming back from Daojiao, things were quiet for a few days.
When my mother called, I told her I was listening to Cantonese opera. She was silent for two seconds on the other end, then said, “You? Listening to Cantonese opera?” I said, “Yeah.” She laughed and said, “Are you getting old?”
I didn’t laugh. I thought of what Aunt Zhen had said—“It is old people’s stuff. But old people die too.” Maybe one day I’ll die too. But before that, I want to see more of these people still holding things up, see more of the deepest, least visible things in this city. Because they are the true face of Dongguan: not factories, not GDP, not the label “world’s factory,” but the old people singing in old factories, the hands shaving bamboo strips in courtyards, the sweat on the oars at 5:30 by the river, the pens writing forms in ancestral halls, the bowl of bitter tea handed over in the herbal tea shop. They won’t appear in any city promotional film, but they are the base color of this city—the layer beneath the bright things. You can’t see it, but you know it’s there. Stepping on it, the ground beneath your feet is solid.
My phone buzzed. A text from Old Li, again three words:
“Go sell yourself.”
I stared at the screen for five seconds, then replied: “Old Li, what are you talking about?”
He sent another: “Selling-body Festival. Dongkeng. Tomorrow.”
I asked Uncle Zhang at the company. Uncle Zhang said the Dongkeng Selling-Body Festival is a very strange Dongguan festival, on the second day of the second lunar month. Legend has it that in ancient times poor people came out to “sell themselves” to landlords as laborers; later it gradually evolved into a big festival of blessing, water-splashing, and market-going.
I replied to Old Li: “Okay.”
***
Dongkeng town was livelier than Daojiao—not the liveliness of a commercial district, but the liveliness of “people packed against people.” A main road in the town center had been closed off, stalls set up on both sides—selling sugar paintings, balloons, clothes, grass jelly, chicken wings. The air was mixed with cooking smoke, perfume, sweat, and an indescribable sweetness. There were many people, mostly locals with their families, and quite a few young people from elsewhere holding cameras.
Old Li led me toward the square in the town center. From far off I heard gongs and drums, dense as rain pounding on sheet metal. Up close I saw decorated floats parked along the road, carrying all sorts of figures—traditional lion dancers, modern cartoon characters. Crowds surrounded the floats; children sat on their fathers’ shoulders, arms stretched out long.
“Is this a parade?” I asked.
“Yes. Each village and community makes its own float, competing for the best-looking.” He paused. “But the real highlight isn’t this.”
“What is it?”
He pointed at the sky.
I looked up—someone was spraying water into the crowd with a high-pressure hose. Not a light drizzle, but straight jets that smacked against people with a “pap-pap” sound. The people being sprayed didn’t dodge; they laughed. A stall nearby sold water guns, ten yuan each; children lined up to buy.
“Water splashing?”
“Yes. The tradition of the Selling-Body Festival—being splashed means good luck for the year. The wetter you get, the luckier you are.”
Just then a jet swept in from the side and hit me square on the shoulder. I shuddered; turning, I saw a boy in his teens holding a water gun and laughing. Before I could react, another stream came from behind—this time a whole basin of water, poured from head to foot.
I stood there soaked through, T-shirt stuck to my back, sandals full of water. Everyone around was laughing; Old Li was laughing too. I laughed too—not forced, I really wanted to laugh. That cold—the February wind wasn’t hot yet, the sun was shining, water poured over me, cold and warm mixed—suddenly woke me up. Like being given a hard shove out of a half-dream.
Old Li handed me a bottle of water. I took it, twisted it open, drank. The water wasn’t cold, lukewarm, but good. I said, “Does this water have a meaning too?”
He said, “No. Just so you don’t die of thirst.”
***
After the water splashing, we walked toward the edge of town. Old Li said he’d take me to see “the real thing.” What was the real thing? He didn’t answer. At a street corner he bought a few jin of fruit.
We came to an alley. The door was open, and someone was singing inside. The sound didn’t come from a loudspeaker; it came straight from a person’s throat—no accompaniment, no erhu, just one person’s voice, clean, like a thread pulled from the house, through the alley, drifting out to the street.
I walked to the doorway. An old man sat in the center of the main room, holding a piece of wood—not a wooden fish, just an ordinary block—striking it with a stick as thick as a chopstick, singing as he struck. His voice was unusual: not high, not bright, but every word bitten clear, as if planting each word from his mouth into the air.
Old Li whispered to me, “This is Uncle Li. Almost seventy this year.”
I stood at the door listening. The lyrics were in Dongguan dialect; after several years here I could understand everyday speech, but not a single line of this tune. But the melody was simple, just a few notes turning back and forth, and the way it turned was unusual—not like singing, like speaking, like a person sitting before you, tapping wood and telling you a very long story; at the moving parts his voice would tremble slightly, but quickly level out again.
Uncle Li finished a section and stopped to drink water. Old Li took me over. Uncle Li glanced at us, no expression. Old Li said, “Uncle Li, I’ve come to see you, brought a friend to listen.” He set the bag of fruit on the table nearby.
Uncle Li nodded, asked nothing. He pointed to a stool beside him, motioning me to sit. I sat down and found the stool legs a little crooked, the seat tilting to one side; I shifted my weight to steady it.
Uncle Li began tapping again, a faster rhythm this time. Though I couldn’t understand the words, I could feel the emotion shifting—rising slowly from low, like a river flowing out of a valley, widening as it went.
After about ten minutes he stopped, set the wood block on the table, and rubbed his fingers. I noticed a thick callus between his index and middle fingers—not a thin hard callus, but a worn, shiny one, like a layer of glaze.
I said, “Uncle Li, that callus…”
He looked down at his fingers and said, “Decades of tapping. If there were no callus that’d be strange.” His tone was ordinary, as if saying “I ate today.”
I thought of Aunt Zhen’s costume passed down two generations, Uncle Li’s deformed knuckles, Master Chen lying on the stage with shoulders rising and falling. These people’s hands—each pair different, but all bearing the same trace: left by a thing rubbed against for a very, very long time.
I asked, “This is muyu singing, right?”
He thought for a moment and said, “Yes. Last month a college student came to record it, said she was doing some kind of ‘fieldwork.’ I let her record. Afterward she asked if she could learn. I said yes. She said she’d come once a week. She came three times; the fourth time she didn’t come.”
He smiled—not a bitter smile, the kind that says “I knew it would be like this.”
“But it doesn’t matter,” he added. “Whether she comes is her business. Whether I sing is my business.”
Again a variation on that sentence. Aunt Zhen said “We sing for ourselves,” Uncle Li said “hand feel,” Fang Bo said “not for sale, only for giving,” now Uncle Li said “whether I sing is my business.” These people all seemed to share one thing: not expecting. Not expecting anyone to learn, not expecting anyone to understand, not expecting the thing to be “passed down.” They just—do. Doing the thing itself is enough for them.
***
Coming out from Uncle Li’s, we ate laifen at a small shop in the alley. The shop was tiny, three or four tables; the owner did the cooking, the cashiering, and the dishwashing all herself. I ordered pork-organ laifen, Old Li ordered beef brisket.
The noodles came; the broth was milky white, a few chopped scallions floating on top. I took a sip and the freshness made my tongue curl. Not MSG freshness, but the thick freshness of bones simmered a long time. I asked the owner, “How long is this broth simmered?”
“All night. From midnight.”
“Every day?”
“Every day. Without it there’s no flavor.”
“Aren’t you tired?”
She glanced at me and said, “Tired. But I’ve done it a lifetime.”
Again “a lifetime.”
In all my years in Dongguan, I’d found this word appearing with abnormal frequency: Aunt Zhen sang Cantonese opera a lifetime, Uncle Li made lanterns a lifetime, Master Chen worked puppets a lifetime, Uncle Li tapped muyu a lifetime, Fang Bo wrapped zongzi a lifetime, Chen Ah Po lived here decades, Sister Huang ran a herbal tea shop twenty years.
“A lifetime” here is not a rhetorical flourish; it is a fact. These people really did spend a lifetime on one thing. Not because the thing is so remarkable, but because the thing is their deepest connection to the world. When they do it, they are not “working,” not “performing,” not “preserving culture”—they are “living.”
You spend a lifetime on one thing, and the thing becomes you; you become its vessel. When you die, the vessel breaks, and what’s inside—the calluses on the hands, the flavor in the bone broth, the tunes sung ten thousand times—scatters. Whether it can be put into another vessel, nobody knows.
This is probably the truest state of life: it is scattering, but while it scatters, someone is still adding to it.
***
Old Li dropped me at the entrance to Nancheng Pedestrian Street, and I walked slowly back to my rented room.
It was late; only the convenience store was still lit. I pushed the door open and went in.
“Give me a pack of Soft Classic.”
Xiao Yang stared at me, didn’t reach for the cigarettes, pulled out a pack of gum instead, and said, “Don’t you hardly smoke? A pack lasts you three days.”
She was probably half-asleep. I didn’t call her on it, took the cigarettes, paid, and put them in my pocket.
Pushing the door open to leave, the street was very quiet. I touched the pack through my pocket; I could feel it was soft. Just like this city—hard on the outside, warm inside. I looked up at the window of my rented room; the light was still on. The access card on my key ring clinked.
I’m still in Dongguan. The thread is still being woven.
The water from the Selling-Body Festival had long since dried. Spring retreated from the alleys of Dongkeng, and Dongguan grew hotter day by day. A freezer appeared at the door of the convenience store; Xiao Yang still didn’t smile much, but occasionally gave me an extra sausage. Old Li’s father was discharged from the hospital, and he started riding his motorcycle around again. Those months I worked and went home as usual, feeling the days flow like the canal water, quietly. By the time I smelled mugwort again, the Dragon Boat Festival was near.
## Section Nine: Guanxiang Agarwood
As the Dragon Boat Festival approached, a muggy dampness began to rise in the air.
One day Chen Ah Po called me over downstairs and handed me a bundle of mugwort, saying, “The festival’s coming. Hang it at your door to ward off evil.”
I took it: “Thanks, Ah Po.” The smell of mugwort was sharp, medicinal, with a stubborn plant bitterness. I hung it on the door handle, and afterward every time I opened or closed the door the smell came through the crack, reminding me of the turning of the solar terms.
Sister Huang’s herbal tea shop was still open, the fan creaking overhead. She handed me a bowl of sugarcane and imperata root water and said, “Free. Today’s my birthday.” I took the bowl and drank; it was sweet, going down smooth, pressing down the dryness at the bottom of my heart. I said, “Happy birthday.” She smiled, the wrinkles at the corners of her eyes spreading.
Xiao Yang still didn’t smile much. But the day before yesterday, when I bought instant noodles, she suddenly stared at me for two seconds and blurted, “You’ve lost weight.” I was taken aback and said, “No, have I?” She said, “Yes.” Then handed me a sausage, still not charging me.
These small things—hanging mugwort, drinking sweet tea, being told “you’ve lost weight,” getting an extra sausage—they are not some grand “folk custom.” But they are life. And folk custom is, more or less, the shape life takes after being worn by time.
Dongguan is still being worn. I am too. What shape will come out, I don’t know.
***
Old Li hadn’t contacted me lately. I heard his father had fallen, and he was keeping vigil at the hospital; his texts came slowly, sometimes not at all. I sent him two messages asking how things were; he said “Not bad, he can walk, just slow,” followed by a period, and nothing more. I could imagine him sitting by the hospital bed, listening to the monitor beep, that kind of exhaustion where even typing feels like too much effort.
One weekend at the end of June, I had planned to sleep until noon, but was blasted awake by firecrackers mixed with drumming outside the window. Not the scattered “pap-pap” kind, but continuous, dense, like beans poured into a hot wok. I leaned out the window. A crowd had gathered at the alley entrance downstairs—red, gold, smoky.
I squinted for a while and recognized the lion dance. Not the perfunctory lion dance at tourist sites—two people holding up a lion, jumping and shaking in front of a newly opened hardware store, a middle-aged man in red lighting a third string of firecrackers, fanning the smoke from his face with his hand, his face all smiles.
After watching for a few minutes, I suddenly remembered—Liaobu. I didn’t seem to have seriously visited Liaobu yet.
Liaobu is a bit far from Nancheng, forty minutes by bus. I washed my face, changed clothes, and went out.
The Xiangshi area was older than I expected. Not dilapidated, but aged—arcades, narrow alleys, old banyan trees, faded rental ads on utility poles, the air floating with an aged smell like old wood and dust mixed. I asked an old uncle by the road, “Where is Xiangshi?” He pointed ahead: “Turn the corner and it’s there.”
“Xiangshi”—Dongguan used to have two great market fairs, “Xiangshi” and “Yaoshi”; the Xiangshi in Liaobu sold Dongguan agarwood. Guanxiang is the incense formed by the white agarwood tree; in ancient times it was tribute, and now a jin can sell for thousands or tens of thousands. My knowledge of it had always stayed at the “heard of it” level; I had never seen the real thing.
Turning the corner, I came to an old street called “Yaxiang.” The street wasn’t long, a few hundred meters, with many incense shops on both sides. Not the incense of incense-burning shops, but true guanxiang—pieces of wood, dark brown, with an oily luster on the surface. Held to the nose, the smell was very faint, like the earth in a forest after rain—not sharp, but deep.
I walked into an incense shop. A handwritten wooden board hung at the door with “Chen Ji” in brush calligraphy, half the paint gone.
Inside, an old man was shaving something with his head down. Up close, he was shaving a piece of agarwood—not into powder, but into thin slices, thin enough to be translucent. His knife was small, the tip curved, the edge worn bright.
“Boss, how much is this?” I pointed to a small pinch of incense slices on the counter.
He looked up at me. Seventy or so, thin, very dark skin, fingers short and thick but very steady. He said, “Two hundred.”
A small pinch. Not cheap. I hesitated, then paid. Not because I liked it so much, but because I’d come all this way; not buying something seemed to be missing something.
He took the money, put the slices in a small red paper bag, and handed it to me. The bag was very light, but the smell came through—faint, like something very far away.
“Boss, how long have you been doing this?” I asked.
“Incense? Decades.” He said, “My father passed it to me.”
“Do people still buy it?”
“Some. Not many. Mostly old customers. Some come from Hong Kong, some are locals. Very few young people; they buy it as a gift.”
He paused, then added, “But few really understand it. Incense sticks, incense slices—many people buy them and just leave them there, don’t burn them. Guanxiang isn’t for looking at; it’s for burning. Only when burned do you know the flavor.”
He pointed to a small censer behind the counter, where a small piece of agarwood was slowly burning. The smoke was straight, not scattering, rising like a transparent thread.
I stood there watching the smoke. The smoke was very fine, but very steady; when there was no wind it didn’t bend. I thought of the thread-like voice when Uncle Li tapped his muyu songs, of the net the bamboo shadows made on the ground when Uncle Li made lanterns, of the raised dots of seed stitch on Aunt Zhen’s costume. Smoke, thread, net, dots—all fine, light, easily overlooked, yet it is precisely these things that hold up the deepest layer of Dongguan folk custom.
***
Coming out of the incense shop, I wandered around the old streets of Liaobu. The alleys were narrow; in some places two people walking side by side had to turn sideways. Moss grew on the walls; the ground was blue stone, worn shiny by countless feet.
At a fork I heard people arguing. Not fierce arguing, but heated debate. One said, “This tree can’t be moved. Move it and the feng shui is broken.” The other said, “If we don’t move it, how do we fix the road? If the road isn’t fixed, fire trucks can’t get in. If it burns down, who’s responsible?”
I stood on the periphery and listened for a while, roughly understanding: the alley was to have drainage pipes installed, and the construction plan required moving this banyan two meters to the side. The nearby residents disagreed, feeling that moving the tree would affect “feng shui.” The construction side said it couldn’t be done otherwise; every time it rained it flooded.
This scene was very familiar. Not because I’d seen similar arguments—because the argument itself was part of Dongguan folk custom. Not the rituals in ancestral halls, not the drumbeats on dragon boats, but this everyday, concrete argument about “whether a tree should be moved.”
Why couldn’t this banyan be moved? Not because it was precious, but because it had “always been there.” It had grown here seventy or eighty years, witnessed this alley go from mud road to stone road, from single-story houses to buildings, from no running water to every household connected. It watched generation after generation pass by—born, grew up, left, came back, grew old. It was itself a living piece of history. To move it was not to move a tree, but to move a piece of memory.
But the drainage pipes were a real problem too. After heavy rain, the water in the alley could rise above the ankle; if an old person stepped in and slipped, then what? That was real too.
Two realities collided; neither was wrong. Only “what to do.”
Later I heard an old woman among the onlookers say that in the end the village chief decided: the tree doesn’t move, the drainage pipes are rerouted around it, at an extra cost of twenty thousand yuan. The construction side wasn’t happy, but there was nothing they could do—in this alley, that tree’s “say” was greater than the construction contract.
I laughed when I heard that. Not mockery—I just felt that this was probably Dongguan’s truest political ecology. Not ballots, not documents, but the weight of the sentence “this tree is older than my grandfather.”
***
I stayed in Liaobu until afternoon, then took the bus back. The bus was a little crowded; I stood holding the handrail, watching the street scene recede outside the window.
In my years in Dongguan—I’d seen dragon boats, flower lanterns, puppets, heard Cantonese opera, heard muyu songs, been drenched in water, eaten basin cuisine, drunk herbal tea. I seemed to have always been “looking,” “listening,” “recording.” But had I ever truly “burned” anything?
No.
I had always been the one standing by the river, the one sitting in the back row of the old factory, the one squatting in the courtyard washing bamboo leaves. I was present, but I didn’t participate. I was watching, but I didn’t “burn.”
This isn’t a bad thing. An outsider, here only a few years, can’t just become “one of them” overnight. But the question is—do you want to? Do you want to, one day, be able to “burn” something in this city too?
I thought about it. It seemed—I did. But I didn’t know where to start.
Back in the neighborhood, I went to the convenience store downstairs to buy water. Xiao Yang wasn’t there today; a new boy had replaced her, thin, wearing glasses, looking like he’d just graduated, head down stocking shelves, only looking up when I came in to say softly, “Welcome.” I bought a bottle of water without looking up the whole time, and felt inexplicably a little empty.
Walking out with the water, I stopped at the door. A notice was taped to the glass door of the convenience store, printed on A4:
“This neighborhood will hold its first Neighborhood Festival next month, including: a potluck, a fun sports meet, and a flea market. Residents and tenants are welcome to sign up. To register, please contact the village committee.”
I stood at the door looking at it for a long time.
Potluck. I thought of Aunt Li’s basin cuisine, of Fang Bo’s zongzi. Weren’t these things essentially a “potluck”? Bringing each household’s food together, everyone sitting down to eat, and as they eat, the relationships change.
Our neighborhood had never held such an event. Between local residents and tenants, apart from arguing, there was almost no interaction. But someone—maybe the village committee, maybe some enthusiastic resident—wanted to do this, wanted everyone to come out of their rooms and sit at the same table.
I didn’t know whether this “Neighborhood Festival” would come off. Maybe people would sign up, maybe nobody would. But at least someone wanted to do it.
I stared at the A4 sheet for a while, then pushed the door open and went out. The sun was still high outside, bleaching the ground white. I twisted open the water bottle and drank. The water wasn’t cold, lukewarm.
That night, lying in bed, the crack in the ceiling was still the same crack, but today I looked at it differently. Not a river, not a thread—today it looked like a road. From the incense slices in Chen Ji’s shop, to the argument under the banyan, to the A4 notice at the neighborhood entrance. This road wasn’t straight; it curved, took many turns, but it kept going forward.
I thought of the old man at Chen Ji. He’d shaved incense a lifetime, each slice thin enough to be translucent. Finish one, set it down, shave the next.
I’d been in Dongguan three years, from initially feeling “how can this place be so flat” to now feeling “this place is actually very deep.” Deep where? Not under the high-rises, not inside the factories, but deep in the alleys, in the old factories, under the banyan, behind the incense counter—in those people still doing one thing, whether or not anyone watches.
And I, an outsider, a passerby, someone still renting—I am also doing something. I am watching. I am recording. I am writing all this down.
Maybe one day these words will become something—not anything remarkable—but at least the smoke after “burning.” Faint, straight, not scattering.
Just like the smoke in Chen Ji’s incense shop.
After coming back from Liaobu, I quieted down for a while. Every day I worked and went home, bought water at the convenience store downstairs; Xiao Yang was sometimes there, sometimes not, and the new boy gradually became more familiar, occasionally asking if I wanted my water heated. The Neighborhood Festival notice at the neighborhood entrance had been rained on once, its corners curled, but it was still there. In the end I didn’t sign up. Not that I didn’t want to—I just felt that as an outsider, what business did I have joining the fun?
I found this hesitation funny myself. But I just couldn’t take that step.
Like that thread of smoke—knowing full well that only when burned does it have flavor, yet still keeping it enshrined in the censer, unwilling to light it.
I didn’t light that smoke. After coming back from Liaobu, I worked and went home as usual, went to the convenience store for water; Xiao Yang was sometimes there, sometimes not. The Neighborhood Festival notice at the neighborhood entrance had been rained on once, its corners curled, but it was still there. I didn’t sign up. The days passed one by one; the bit of summer heat at the end of June hadn’t yet dispersed when mid-July arrived.

## Section Ten: Fishing Bay

In mid-July, my mother called and said she wanted to come to Dongguan to see me.

I said sure.

She asked where she'd stay. I said a hotel. She asked what we'd eat. I said out. She was silent for two seconds, then said, "You don't even have a place where you can put someone up?"

I said, "I have a place. It's just small."

She was silent again for a moment, then said, "Then I'll bring some dried fish."

After hanging up, I suddenly realized—my mother was coming, which meant I had to "host" in this city. Not take her to a restaurant, but let her into my door, eat food I cooked, sleep in my bed. This was different from all my previous "being present." Before, I had always been hosted by others—Chen Ah Po gave me zongzi, Aunt Li gave me basin cuisine, Fang Bo taught me to wrap zongzi, Aunt Zhen poured me tea. Now it was my turn.

This thought made me a little panicked. I wasn't even sure my rented room could be called a "home"—a bed, a table, a kitchen, a windowsill where you could see the canal. That was all.

***

The day my mother arrived was a Saturday. I picked her up at the Nancheng bus station and took a taxi back to my rented room. She looked out the window the whole way and didn't say much. When we got downstairs, she looked up at the building—four stories, white ceramic tiles on the exterior, some already fallen off—she said nothing, but I knew she was grading the house in her mind. The grade was not high.

Inside, she set the dried fish she'd brought on the table, looked around, and said, "Tomorrow take me to see what's fun in Dongguan."

I thought about it. The Tea Garden Fair had passed, the dragon boats were over, the Selling-Body Festival was over, the flower lanterns had to wait until the first lunar month. What could I take her to see? Take her to a mall? How was that different from Guangzhou? Take her cycling around Songshan Lake? She wouldn't.

I thought of a place—Humen. Not the Opium War Museum; of course we'd go there, but that was a "scenic spot." What I wanted to show her was—Xinwan Fishing Harbor.

Humen Xinwan, Dongguan's last fishing harbor. I'd remembered it after a local colleague mentioned it in passing. He said, "When I was little I went to Xinwan and could still see the harbor full of fishing boats. Now there are fewer, but there are still some."

Sunday morning I took my mother by bus. After the bus entered Humen, the road began to narrow, the houses on both sides grew shorter, and that familiar fishy dampness slowly entered the air—but saltier, sharper than in Daojiao, carrying diesel and fish-scale smells.

When we got to Xinwan, I got off and stood on the embankment looking into the harbor. A row of fishing boats was moored, about twenty or thirty, hulls painted blue or green, some already peeling. Tattered nets hung from the masts, swaying in the wind. On the shore someone was mending nets—not machine-woven nets, but hand-knotted nylon rope nets, knot by knot, fingers flying.

My mother stood beside me and looked for a long time. Our hometown is Zhanjiang, near both river and sea; she had seen harbors. She said, "These boats are so small. Same as ours."

"Coastal fishing boats. They don't go out to the open sea," I said.

"Can they still catch fish?"

"They can. But fewer."

We walked along the embankment. Passing a small seafood shop, several styrofoam boxes out front, inside freshly landed shrimp, crab, fish. An old woman sat on a low stool picking shrimp; beside her a young woman—probably her daughter-in-law—was gutting fish. Fish blood splattered on the concrete, dark red. The air was all fishiness. My mother leaned over to look and asked, "How much is this shrimp per jin?"

The old woman looked up at her and said, in heavily accented Mandarin, "Thirty-five. Just landed."

My mother didn't buy. She walked a couple of steps to the side and suddenly said, "This smell is the same as ours."

I glanced at her. Her expression was a little distant, as if she were looking through those fishing boats and that fishiness at something very far away.

At the other end of the harbor, in front of a single-story house, an old man sat in a rattan chair at the door, waving a palm-leaf fan. Seeing us approach, he showed no expression, just slowed the fan a little.

My mother took the initiative and said something in Zhanjiang dialect. The old man didn't understand; I translated. The old man smiled and said in Mandarin, "Your son brought you to see the Tanka?"

"We're outsiders," my mother said.

"Do you still fish?" I asked.

"No. Sold the boat. Now I help my son mind the shop." He pointed outside. "That seafood shop."

"Do you still go on the boat?"

"Once in a while. When old friends come back, we take the boat out for a spin. Not to fish, just to go around."

He paused, then added, "Once ashore, the ground under my feet feels unsteady instead. Rocked on a boat all my life; walking on shore I feel the ground is soft."

I thought of the old man at Chen Ji's incense shop saying "hand feel," of the calluses on Uncle Li's fingers, of the way Fang Bo's ears went red, of this old man in Xinwan—his legs and waist rocked by a boat all his life, and once ashore he was "not used to it."

The body doesn't lie. You spend a lifetime on one thing, and your body remembers. Your fingers, your waist, your ears, your tongue—all become vessels for that thing. When you leave the thing, your body still "remembers" for you.

My mother, beside me, after hearing my translation, suddenly said, "He's like your father."

I was startled. "Who?"

"That old man." My mother said, "Like your father. Says he's not doing it anymore, but his heart is still in it."

I thought of my father. He'd said similar things—"I'm done, done, tired after a lifetime." And then in less than two months he'd sneak back to the fields to wander around.

***

Back from Xinwan, my mother sat in the rented room for a while, then said, "You cook tonight."

I said okay.

I went down to the wet market. Bought pork ribs, white radish, a sea bass, a bunch of greens. Ribs stewed with radish—my mother had taught me: cold water in the pot, blanch, add ginger, simmer on low heat. Sea bass steamed—three cuts on the back, ginger slices, steam eight minutes after the water boils, pour off the steaming water, drizzle soy sauce, pour hot oil over. Greens stir-fried with garlic.

Standing in the kitchen doing these things, my movements were slow because I wasn't skilled—I hadn't made these dishes many times. I was thinking of Aunt Li simmering basin cuisine, eating from the top down to that piece of radish at the bottom; of the old man at Chen Ji shaving incense slices, each cut translucent, with patience; of the old man at Xinwan saying "the ground is soft" in that faint, neither-sad-nor-joyful tone.

These people passed through my mind. Then I put the ribs in the pot.

My mother sat in the living room and didn't come in to help. She didn't like others giving orders in the kitchen, so she wouldn't do it either. She just sat there, occasionally poking her head in to see what I was doing, then pulling it back.

Dinner was ready. Three dishes and a soup, laid on the small table bought from the secondhand market. My mother tasted the rib and radish stew; the soup was white, the radish translucent. She took a sip of soup, said nothing, then picked up a piece of radish, chewed, and said, "The radish is stewed all right."

"All right."

Those two words again.

I laughed. My mother asked what I was laughing at; I said nothing. She didn't press.

That night after dinner, after clearing the dishes, she sat on the bed watching TV, and I stood by the windowsill. The canal was still the canal, the water still not clear, the lights on the opposite bank reflected on the surface, fragmented. I remembered the first time I leaned on this windowsill watching dragon boat training—back then I'd just arrived in Dongguan, felt the place was flat and white, with nothing. Now standing here, it seemed I could see a little more.

My mother said from behind, "You're here, alone, it's all right. But you can't always be alone."

I didn't turn around. I said, "Mm."

***

Over the next few days I took her to Humen again. Not sightseeing—buying clothes for her and Dad.

Took her to Xicun Market, crowded, lively. She picked out a shirt, tried it on, said, "Your dad will look good in this color." She picked out a T-shirt for me too, said, "You wear this."

Took her to eat Dongguan roast goose. Crispy skin, tender meat; she didn't eat much, but said "not bad."

My mother stayed three days and then left. The morning she left, she left the dried fish with me and said, "Cook for yourself, don't always eat instant noodles. If you can't finish it, give some to friends."

"Okay."

I walked her to the bus station. Before going in she looked back and said, "Next time I come, move to a better place."

I said, "Okay."

She left. I stood at the station entrance watching the bus recede. The sun was shining, the ground white.

Back in the rented room, I opened the fridge and found she had quietly slipped in a few pieces of dried fish before leaving—I hadn't noticed when I went out to see her off. I took out a piece, sniffed it; salty, with a smoky smell.

I hung it behind the door, the way Chen Ah Po hung mugwort.

The days after my mother left, the rented room became especially quiet. This quiet was not emptiness; it was the hollow left after something has been taken away—you can see the shape, but can't touch it.

I held that piece of dried fish, not knowing who to give it to. Xiao Yang was at the convenience store; not convenient to give something like this. Old Li was at the hospital with his father; forget it. The others—Chen Ah Po, Sister Huang, Aunt Li—didn't seem close enough for that.

In the end I cut up the dried fish and ate it myself. With white rice, so salty it was bitter, but very fragrant.

That night lying in bed, the crack in the ceiling was still the same crack. I thought for a long time about what my mother had said—"you can't always be alone."

I'd been in Dongguan three years. Seen dragon boats, flower lanterns, puppets, heard Cantonese opera, heard muyu songs, been drenched in water, eaten basin cuisine, drunk herbal tea, gone to the incense shop in Liaobu, gone to Xinwan in Humen. I seemed to have always been "looking," "listening," "recording."

But had I ever truly "burned" anything?

This question had followed me from that night in Liaobu to now. I turned over, looked at the crack in the ceiling. It was still the same crack, but today I looked at it differently—not a river, not a thread, not a road. It looked like a thread of smoke. Faint, straight, not scattering.

Just like the smoke in Chen Ji's incense shop. And like the dried fish my mother brought.

You have to unwrap it first, light it first, give it away first. You have to cut it open, put it in your mouth—the flavor of that moment is the answer time gives you.

I closed my eyes.

Tomorrow I should go to the wet market. The dried fish was almost gone. Maybe I should buy some ribs, stew some radish. Maybe I should call Old Li. Maybe—

I shook my head and pushed the thoughts down. Sleep.

Outside the window, the canal water flowed quietly. A few lights still on across the water, fragmented, like scattered silver.

Dongguan is still being worn.

I am too.

After that July, I stayed in Nancheng for a long time. The crack in the ceiling was still there, Xiao Yang was still at the convenience store, Old Li was still riding his motorcycle, Chen Ah Po was still collecting rent. Only some things began to change—Dongguan had more factories, more people, wider roads. And what my mother said, "you can't always be alone," I kept remembering. By September, when the weather cooled, I started running to those towns again.

## Section Eleven: Rattan Weaving

In early September, Dongguan's weather was still hot, but mornings and evenings began to have a bit of cool wind. I got off work after six and walked back to my rented room. Passing a pork-trotter rice shop, I smelled braising spices. I stopped, hesitated about going in. Just then a middle-aged woman pushing a bicycle called out to me from the roadside.

"Hey—handsome!"

I laughed. "Aunt Feng?"

"Yes." She patted the bamboo basket on the back of the bike. "Three Po said you wanted to see weaving?"

"Yeah, I mentioned it to Three Po in Second Alley."

"You can come over this weekend if you're free."

***

Aunt Feng's "workshop" was a single-story house deep in Hengmei Village. Not rented—her own family's. Her husband was a Hengmei native; the village had allocated land in the nineties and built this row of houses. Blue-brick walls, tin roof, a few children's clothes drying at the door, an old Phoenix-brand bicycle leaning against the corner.

She took me inside. The room was brighter than I expected. A long table by the window, piled with rattan strips, bamboo strips, scissors, an awl. Several finished pieces hung on the wall: a rattan vegetable basket, a bamboo cricket cage, a small winnowing basket the size of a palm. Sunlight came through the window and fell right on that row of things; the grain of the rattan was lit up clearly, as if each strand glowed on its own.

"Do these sell well?" I asked.

"Not very well. So I give them away too. And use them myself." She picked up the small winnowing basket to show me. "I use this to dry tangerine peel."

She had me sit, pulled up a low stool opposite, picked up a few rattan strips and began weaving. Her fingers were thick, nails cut very short, a fresh scratch on the side of her right index finger, covered with a thin scab. The rattan strips flipped back and forth in her hands with a dry "shasha" sound. I picked up a few strips and sniffed—a faint plant smell, like dried straw, or the moss by the well in an old backyard.

"Who did you learn this from?" I asked.

"My mother. She could weave this." She said, "I knew how before I married over here. After I married over here I found many people here weave too. Here they use water rattan; Guangxi uses mountain rattan. Water rattan is soft, mountain rattan is hard."

"What is water rattan?"

She paused, hands still moving, eyes looking out the window at the big banyan, as if trying to find an answer in the tree's shadow.

"I don't know what it's called... anyway, water rattan. Grows by rivers and ponds; only soft after soaking in water. Mountain rattan is cut on the mountain, very hard." She came back to herself, bent a strip into a curve and handed it to me. "See, water rattan can weave finer things, comes out smooth. Mountain rattan suits coarse things—baskets, crates, big winnowing baskets. We're by the water here, so we use water rattan."

I took the strip; indeed it was very pliable, bent into a circle without cracking. Her hands didn't stop while she talked. The strips folded, threaded, tightened between her thick fingers, the movement as familiar as breathing—you don't think about how to take the next breath, it comes on its own. I watched her weave for a good while before realizing her hands had been moving the whole time, but her eyes were on me. Not sizing me up—habitual, the way you work and talk at the same time, peripheral vision is enough.

She talked and wove, hands never stopping. In a moment a palm-sized basket was finished—bottom, sides, rim, handle, a complete little basket. She handed it to me. I took it; very light, but feels sturdy. The surface of the rattan was smoothed very fine, not a single burr on the edge. I turned it over and over. The weave was dense, the gaps between strips almost the same width, as if measured with a ruler. But I knew it wasn't measured. It was measured by hand. Hands are more accurate than rulers.

"Want to try?" she said.

I picked up a few strips and crossed and threaded them the way she did. The first one went in backwards. After adjusting, the second was loose. She didn't laugh at me, just said, "The force has to be even. Too tight and the rattan breaks, too loose and it comes out scattered."

I tried for about twenty minutes and produced a crooked "thing"—couldn't say what it looked like, maybe a squashed bowl. The bottom was crooked, the sides slanted, the edge strips sticking out every which way like a hedgehog's spines. Aunt Feng looked at it and said, "Not bad. For a first time, that's not bad."

"Not bad" again.

I think this evaluation has a special weight in Dongguan. It is not perfunctory, not encouragement; it is a confirmation that "I see your effort, it's not perfect, but it's real." "Not bad" means "you came, you tried, that's enough." I'd heard it at Three Po's too—when wrapping zongzi, my zongzi corners stuck up like they were about to take off, and Three Po looked at them and said "not bad." Now I realize these two old people are using the same vocabulary.

Aunt Feng's work was somewhat famous around Hengmei Village. The old people in the village used her baskets for shopping, her winnowing baskets for drying things, her bamboo rice baskets for storing rice. These things last longer than plastic crates, three to five years without falling apart. I'd been to the old woman's house next door; beside her stove sat a rice basket Aunt Feng had woven, worn shiny, as if touched by countless hands and worn by countless days.

"Young people don't use these anymore," she said. "They buy shopping bags from the supermarket. But I still weave. Woven a lifetime; if I don't weave my hands itch."

"Itch" again.

I asked Aunt Feng, "Have you ever thought about teaching young people?"

She stopped her work, thought about it, and said, "I have. My daughter. When she was little she wove with me, not bad. Later school got busy, she stopped. Now she works in Dongcheng, comes back once in a while, sees me weaving, and says, 'Mom, stop weaving, just buy them.'"

She smiled. "I say okay. But the next day I'm sitting here weaving again."

"Is she angry?"

"No. She knows it can't be changed."

This "can't be changed" moved me more than any "declaration of transmission." Not "I want to pass this craft down"—it's "I can't change." Not a choice, a fate. You've been tangled with one thing your whole life; it's grown into your bones; you couldn't pull it out if you tried. I thought of my mother back home soling shoes—you tell her to stop, just buy a pair. She agrees, then at night when the light goes out her hands are fumbling for that needle by the pillow again.

***

I sat at Aunt Feng's until almost eleven. There were no streetlights in the alley, only light from the windows of the houses. As I was leaving she gave me that small winnowing basket—the one she'd woven to show me. I asked how much. She said no charge. I said that wouldn't do. She said, "Three Po says you can wrap zongzi. Next time you wrap some, just give me one to eat."

"Wrap zongzi?" I went silent—this is probably how embarrassing stories travel a thousand li. One old woman tells a basket-weaving woman about an outsider who wrapped a zongzi that fell apart. Never mind. This face.

Information is like the aerial roots of a banyan, hanging down from one person, touching another, taking root. Three Po had chatted with Aunt Feng while buying a winnowing basket, and Aunt Feng learned I could wrap zongzi. The story of my zongzi falling apart had probably reached Three Po's ears too—most likely by way of Aunt Feng's mouth. In this village, messages travel by walking, by visiting, by a casual word while sitting at the alley mouth shelling beans. It's slow, but solid. Every sentence carries body warmth, unlike city phone calls that end when you hang up—here words have to be said face to face, sitting down, looking into each other's eyes.

I took the basket and said thank you. Walking out of her single-story house, the alley was very quiet, only an occasional car horn from the main road far away. The moon was bright, shining on the blue stone, giving off a cold white light. I looked down at the basket in my hand—it showed a warm yellow in the moonlight, contrasting with the cold white. One light from the sky, one thing from the hand.

Back in the rented room, I hung the small winnowing basket on the kitchen wall. The mugwort Chen Ah Po had given me had dried; I tucked it into the basket too. The dry fragrance of mugwort mixed with the plant smell of rattan—it smelled like a person sitting by a window, using two hands to weave time into shape.

I stood in the kitchen looking at that basket for a while. It was small, palm-sized, hanging on the white wall like an ornament. But it wasn't an ornament. It was Aunt Feng sitting on a low stool, using a pair of thick hands, weaving strip by strip. She had touched every strip, looked at every gap. It wasn't perfect—the colors of the rattan varied, the weave wasn't perfectly symmetrical. Like what Aunt Feng said: "Not bad."

That night I called my mother. She was back home, and sure enough, soling shoes again. I said, Mom, today I learned to weave a basket, it came out ugly. She laughed on the other end and said, you're just like your father, clumsy hands. I said but someone praised it as "not bad." She paused, then said, then keep trying.

After hanging up, I looked at the basket on the wall again. Moonlight had moved in from the window a bit, falling right on the gaps between the rattan strips. I thought, "not bad" is probably like this—one person does a thing a lifetime, until it becomes part of them; another person sees it, can't find any fine words, and squeezes out: not bad.

Enough. That's enough.

## Section Twelve: The Ancient Tree

For the National Day holiday, I didn't go back to Zhanjiang.

My mother said on the phone, "Enjoy the holiday by yourself." I said, "Okay."

After hanging up, I opened the computer in my rented room and couldn't type a single word. The screen glowed like a mirror that had waited half a day for someone who never came. These past few days I'd been wanting to write something—about dragon boats, about flower lanterns, about the things I'd seen, heard, touched. But they crowded in my head, none willing to come out first.

Someone had mentioned to me: "Qishi has a thousand-year-old tree, the one by the river."

A thousand years. A place like Dongguan having a thousand-year-old tree—I didn't quite believe it. I did some mental calculation and decided to go see.

***

The bus ride took an hour and a half. Out of Nancheng, onto the Eastern Expressway, the scenery outside changed from high-rises to factories, then to factories alternating with farmland, finally to large fish ponds and banana groves. The old woman in the back seat held a plastic bag with a few live chickens inside; a chicken head poked out of the bag, eyes half open, as if also curious where we were going.

When I got to Qishi, I found this town was different from the ones I'd been to before—it was "slower." Not desolate, but slower in rhythm. The cars on the road weren't in a hurry, the pedestrians weren't in a hurry, even the shop signs looked like they hadn't been changed in a dozen years. I saw a "Faji General Store," stationery and batteries in the glass counter, a calendar on the wall, the beauty on the calendar smiling the same smile as in the nineties.

I followed a passerby's directions to the East River. The East River flows through here, wide, the water a deep green with gray, like an old piece of jade. A row of banyans on the embankment, and at the very end—that thousand-year-old tree.

Standing before it, my first reaction was not "wow," but "..."

Not because it was spectacular. It wasn't tall, the trunk not especially thick—not comparable to the ancient banyans I'd seen in Guangzhou. But it had a strange "density." Look at its canopy: the branches and leaves so dense there was almost no gap; sunlight leaked down and became little spots of light on the ground. Look at its roots—aerial roots hanging from the branches, some already rooted in the soil and become trunks, some still suspended in the air like an old man's beard.

A thousand years old. Dongguan's history as a city is only a few decades, but this tree has stood here a thousand years. In the Song dynasty it was already here. Back then Qishi might not even have existed? Just a mudflat by the river. It watched the river rise and fall, watched people come and go, watched this land change from fishing and hunting to farming, from farming to factories, from factories to this city that's hard to describe. In a thousand years it said nothing, but everything left its mark on it—branches broken by typhoons, cracks split by lightning, characters carved by children, hollows worn by adults leaning against it. It doesn't speak, but it remembers.

Weeds all around. I walked a circle around it. The bark was very rough, the fissures deep, moss growing in some places. I reached out and touched it—cool, not the cold of shadow, but the cool of "always soaked in water." The East River's moisture rises year-round, absorbed by the bark, seeping into every crack. This tree is wet. For a thousand years it has been wet like this.

Under the tree was a stone platform with a few incense sticks stuck in it, already burned out, but the ash still there. Beside it a plastic cup with water, a few small wildflowers soaking in it. Not some carefully prepared offering—just someone passing by, picked a few flowers, put them here.

Standing before the stone platform, I suddenly felt—this tree is Dongguan's oldest "folk custom." Not dragon boats, not ancestral halls, not Cantonese opera. This. A tree that has lived a thousand years, and people passing by the river put a flower before it. No ritual, no organization. Just—you've stood here a thousand years, I put a flower, consider it me saying hello.

This relationship is older than any temple fair.

***

I sat under the tree for a while. The stone platform was a little cold; I moved to a big rock nearby. The river wind blew over, carrying fishiness and an indescribable, rust-like earthiness—as if the land itself were breathing.

An old man came down from the embankment, carrying a plastic bag with something inside. He walked to the tree, opened the bag—a few oranges, placed on the stone platform. Then he put his palms together and bowed three times. Not a very formal bow—just bending, palms together, nodding, quick, like greeting an old friend.

After bowing he turned to leave, saw me sitting on the rock, and paused. He asked, "You're here to see the tree too?"

"Yeah," I said. "I heard it's a thousand years old."

He smiled, showing a few yellow teeth. "A thousand? I'd say more. My grandfather's generation said the same." He sat down on the rock beside me, crumpled the plastic bag and stuffed it in his pocket. "This tree is spiritual. I played here when I was little. It was about the same size then."

"When you were little—what year was that?"

"The fifties. I'm seventy-three this year."

He pointed to a fork on the trunk. "See that scar? When I was ten I climbed up to get a bird's nest, fell, broke my arm. My dad took a bamboo strip to my backside, whacking and cursing."

He laughed loudly, as if telling someone else's story. I laughed too. Laughing, I suddenly thought, this tree has probably heard many stories like this—children's cries, fathers' curses, the flapping of birds flying away. It listens to them all, never says a word.

"Is the nest still there?" I asked.

"Long gone. But the scar is still there." He patted the grass clippings off his trousers and left with the empty plastic bag.

I looked up at the scar. A hollow on the trunk, half hidden by moss, not visible unless you look closely. But it's there. Sixty years ago a little boy fell from there, a piece of bark was scraped off, seventy years later the tree is still growing, the scar is still there.

I thought of the old man at Xinwan saying "the ground is soft." Of Aunt Feng saying "can't be changed." Of the calluses on the old man's fingers at Chen Ji. This tree's scar—the same kind of thing. The trace time leaves on a living thing doesn't disappear; it becomes part of it. A person, a tree, a city—all like this. Whatever has worn you grows a harder layer of skin where it rubbed.

***

On the way back from Qishi, I fell asleep on the bus. When I woke, the bus had stopped at an unfamiliar stop, the sunlight outside had moved from the left to the right. I looked out the window for a while and suddenly realized—I'd been in Dongguan all these years and had never truly "left." Not physically, but psychologically. I'd never thought "I'm going to leave." That old "passerby" feeling—I didn't know when it had faded.

Not because of some "putting down roots" ceremony. Because of those things scattered in every corner—the dragon boat drumbeats, the flower lantern frames, the puppet strings, the Cantonese opera arias, the muyu tapping, the Selling-Body Festival water, the smoke in the incense shop, the argument under the banyan, the texture of the rattan basket—these things slowly layered trace upon trace on me.

Each layer is thin. So thin you might not feel it. But layered together, they become a "garment." You wear it, walking on Dongguan's roads, and when the wind blows, you don't feel cold.

***

The last day of the National Day holiday, I was tidying up in my rented room. Put some summer clothes into the cabinet, pulled out long sleeves. Chen Ah Po called me from downstairs, said she'd dried some vegetables and packed me a bag. I took it; the dried vegetables were made from gai lan, dark green, hard, smelling of sunshine.

"Use it for soup in winter," she said.

"Okay."

She turned to leave, then looked back at me. "You seem to have gained a little weight lately."

"Have I?"

"Yes. Your face is rounder."

I touched my face. Maybe it really was a little rounder. Then looked at my clothes—mm, right, the clothes must have shrunk, the quality must be bad. And the mirror must be warped too. I noted this conclusion in my mind and found it very reasonable.

I'd been in Dongguan so long, sometimes I wondered—if one day I really left, what would I take with me?

Some things can't be taken. They can only stay here. By the canal, in the alleys of Hengmei Village, on the embankment at Qishi. They will stay here, waiting for the next outsider, the next passerby, the next person like me—to come, look, listen, eat, touch, and then slowly, slowly, turn this place into "their own place."

And I—I'm still here. Still by the windowsill watching that river.

Winter really came. Dongguan's winter is not the kind northerners understand—no snow, no below-zero, most of the time you don't even need a down jacket. But Dongguan's winter has its own way of letting you know it's here: the wind blows from the north, through the whole of Hongfu Road, without a bit of moisture, dry, hard, cold. Walking on the road, your face feels tight, as if someone has gently rubbed it with sandpaper.

I put my hands in my pockets and touched the thing I'd brought back from Qishi—a small piece of aerial root, dried, curled, like a closed hand. I'd picked it up under the tree, meant to throw it away, but in the end stuffed it in my pocket. It was so light, almost weightless. But I knew it had fallen from a thousand years.

## Section Thirteen: Lap Cheong (Cured Sausage)

At the end of November, Chen Ah Po called up to me from downstairs: "Handsome, come here."

I went down. She stood at her door holding a string of something—not cured meat, but cured sausage. Stuffed into a thin casing, reddish-brown, with a fine layer of oil frost on the surface. She handed it to me and said, "Made it myself. Try some."

I took it. Heavy. My fingers could feel the graininess of the meat inside the casing—not the paste texture of a factory hot dog, but grain by grain, fat and lean clearly distinct.

"You stuffed these yourself?"

"Mm. Hind leg meat. Two parts fat to eight parts lean." She turned and went inside, leaving me standing at the door clutching that string of sausage.

I carried it upstairs and hung it on the balcony. The north wind blew; the sausage swayed gently. I leaned in and sniffed—a smell that mixed wine, soy sauce, and the raw sweetness of pork itself. This was the smell of time. The wind was doing the sun's work: drawing the moisture out bit by bit, locking the flavor in bit by bit.

Suddenly I thought of—my mother. I pulled out my phone and called her.

"Mom."

"Mm?"

"Chen Ah Po gave me lap cheong."

"Is it good?"

"Haven't steamed it yet. But it smells like yours."

She was silent on the other end for two seconds. Then said, "Then steam it and eat it. Don't leave it too long."

"Okay."

After hanging up, I stood on the balcony watching that string of sausage sway. The wind made my eyes sting a little. Not because I was homesick—because of that smell. The smell of lap cheong is the smell of winter, and winter is the season that makes you homesick. No matter where you are, the moment you smell lap cheong, you think of a certain kitchen, a certain person, a certain meal.

I wanted to see how people in other parts of Dongguan make cured meats.

Gaobu's cured meats are also famous, but lower-key than Houjie's. I decided to go take a look.

***

One weekend morning, I took the bus across the Wanjiang Bridge. Once over the bridge, the road narrowed, and large fish ponds and vegetable plots began to appear on both sides; occasionally a few egrets stood on the field ridges, startled up by the sound of the bus, lazily flying off toward the distant high-voltage lines. Gaobu felt a bit like Qishi—slow. But Qishi's slowness is the slowness of the riverside; Gaobu's is the slowness of the water town. More canals, more bridges; at the end of almost every alley you can hear water.

In Gaobu's old market I found a cured-meat shop. No signboard, just an old street-facing house, the doorway hung full of lap cheong, cured meat, cured duck, cured fish, densely hung on bamboo poles from under the eaves all the way to the corner. When the wind blew, the whole row swayed gently, like rows of silent people nodding to you as the wind passed.

The owner was a man in his forties. He was in the courtyard behind the house cutting meat—hind leg, first into strips, then into cubes. The knife was big, but he used it lightly and deftly, each cut following the grain, fat and lean cut separately. A bamboo sieve beside him already held a pile of cubed meat, waiting to be mixed with seasoning.

"Master, may I watch?" I asked, walking into the courtyard.

He glanced up at me, didn't stop. "Watch."

"May I ask your surname, Master?"

"Mo."

"Local?"

"Mm."

And that was it. As if those were the three most important facts of his life. He lowered his head and went on cutting; the rhythm of the knife on the board was steady—tok, tok, tok—like keeping time for himself. I stood beside him and suddenly felt this kind of conversation was very common in Dongguan—locals don't like to talk much, but if you really want to watch, they let you watch. Silence doesn't mean refusal; it just means they don't see the need to explain.

He finished a basin, poured it into a large stainless-steel bowl, added wine, soy sauce, sugar, salt, five-spice powder. Then he mixed it with his hands. Not chopsticks, not a spoon—hands. The whole hand went in, grabbing, kneading, turning, pressing. Fingers threaded among the meat cubes as if massaging them. The meat slowly changed color, from pink to deep red, and an oily sheen began to appear on the surface.

"Only mixing by hand gets the flavor in," he said, without looking up.

"Wouldn't a machine be easier?"

"A machine mixes unevenly. Some parts salty, some parts bland. Hands can feel it."

"Hand feel" again. I'd heard it too many times in Dongguan—the force of Uncle Li shaving bamboo strips, the tightness of Aunt Feng's rattan weaving, the thinness of the old man at Chen Ji shaving incense slices, the threading of Master Mo's fingers among the meat cubes as he mixed the cured meat. What a machine can never do is that "can feel it" thing. You're not making an object; you're in conversation with a basin of meat, asking it "is that enough?" and it answers you with temperature.

When he finished mixing, he stuffed the meat into the casings. Not with a funnel, but with a copper sausage stuffer—like a small jack, pressing the meat into the casing. After stuffing, he tied it off section by section with cotton string, hung it on bamboo poles, and carried it to the doorway to dry.

"How long does it need to dry?" I asked.

"Depends on the weather. If the north wind is strong, a week. If no wind, two weeks."

"What if it rains?"

"If it rains, bring it in. Can't let it get rained on. If it gets rained on it spoils."

He pointed to the row of cured meats at the door. "These were stuffed the day before yesterday. Three more days of wind and they'll be ready."

I leaned in to look at the sausages. Oil frost had already begun to form on the surface; the color had gone from deep red to dark red with a touch of black. Sunlight fell on them with a warm, moist luster. I suddenly understood why Dongguan people stuff sausages in winter—not for the utilitarian purpose of "preserving food." It's because the process itself is a "ritual of getting through winter." Choosing meat, cutting meat, mixing seasoning, stuffing casings, drying—every step is you in conversation with the season. You're using your hands to tell winter: I know you're here, I'm ready.

It's not a ritual—not like grave-sweeping with its clear religious meaning. It's not a performance—not like Cantonese opera with an audience. It's not a competition—not like dragon boats with winners and losers. It's "getting through the days" itself. You stuff sausages in winter the way you plant vegetables in spring, dry fish in summer, harvest rice in autumn—a way of living that follows the seasons.

This "getting through the days" kind of thing is the easiest to lose. Because modernization gives you substitutes: the supermarket has ready-made lap cheong, frozen, buy anytime. You don't need to cut meat yourself, mix seasoning yourself, stuff casings yourself, dry for a week yourself. You just open the fridge, take it out, steam for ten minutes.

Convenient. But something's missing?

Probably the wind. The sunlight. The feel of fingers threading among the meat cubes. The daily expectation of pushing open the balcony door each morning to see if the sausage has gotten drier. That confirmation—"I made this."

***

I weighed out a few strings at Master Mo's and paid. He wrapped the sausage in old newspaper, handed it over, and said, "Don't put it in the fridge when you get home, just hang it on the balcony. Wind works better than a fridge."

I carried the newspaper package back. Passing a small shop at the old market entrance, a "Hongshuangxi" poster on the glass door, steam from boiling noodles drifting out. An old woman sat at the door picking vegetables, glanced up at me, said nothing.

Back in my rented room, I steamed the string Chen Ah Po had given me.

Washed the pot, added water, put the sausage on a plate, plate on the steamer rack. High heat, twelve minutes. Turned off the heat, lifted the lid—steam rushed at my face. The sausage skin had wrinkled, the oil had seeped out, pooling at the bottom of the plate. I picked one up with chopsticks and cut it into thin slices. The cross-section was pink, the fat part translucent, like amber.

Just as I was about to carry it out, someone called from below the balcony. I leaned over—Xiao Yang. She stood downstairs holding a plastic bag, face tilted up at me.

"Come up and sit?" I called.

She didn't move, just nodded. I set the bowl of sausage on the table, grabbed two raw strings, and went to the door to meet her.

She wore a down jacket washed pale, her nose red from the wind, a few boxes of cold medicine in the plastic bag. She handed it to me and said, "For you. You said a couple days ago your throat was uncomfortable."

"You remembered."

"Just happened to."

She came in, sat at the table, eyes sweeping this room of less than twenty square meters, finally landing on the bowl of freshly steamed sausage. I handed her chopsticks; she didn't stand on ceremony, picked up a slice and put it in her mouth. Chewed twice, her eyes curved.

"Going home for New Year?" I asked.

"No." She pushed up her glasses. "You?"

"Depends." I stuffed the two raw strings into her arms. "Hang these up, in a couple days you can steam them."

She looked down at the sausage in her arms and smiled, nose wrinkling: "So much, I can't finish it alone."

"Then don't finish it. Save a section, steam it on New Year's Day."

She nodded, gathered up the bowl and the sausage and stood. At the door she looked back at me; I waved, and only then did she turn—took two steps, stopped, pinched a slice of sausage from the bowl and put it in her mouth, mumbled "delicious," and then really left.

I stood at the door watching for a while. The north wind poured in, puffing out my sleeves.

Back inside, I bit into a slice from my own plate.

Salty, sweet, wine aroma, meat aroma—all there. The fat had half melted, dissolving in the mouth, not greasy. The lean meat had chew, but wasn't dry. The casing was crisp; at the bite, a "gezhi" sound.

I suddenly very much wanted to call my mother and tell her "Chen Ah Po's lap cheong is steamed, tastes as good as yours." But I didn't call. I was afraid that if I opened my mouth, my voice would shake.

I sat alone at the table and ate two bowls of white rice with the sausage. At the bottom of the bowl a little sausage oil remained; I scraped the rice clean, not a drop left.

After eating, I washed the plate and set it on the rack to dry. A faint red ring remained at the bottom—sausage oil had seeped into the fine cracks of the porcelain. I wiped at it; it didn't come off. Forget it. Leave it.

Some traces don't need wiping.

The sausage oil still on the plate, the year's end drew nearer day by day. On the twenty-eighth of the twelfth lunar month, I went to Machong.

## Section Fourteen: Spring Couplets

On the twenty-eighth of the twelfth lunar month, I went to Machong's old market. A local colleague had said, "On the twenty-eighth, Machong's market is the liveliest. Go see for yourself."

Machong's old market is on a road called "Machong Main Street." The road isn't wide, and both sides are all stalls—not the cultural-creative stalls of a tourist street, but real New Year goods stalls. Selling spring couplets, selling New Year tangerines, selling narcissus, selling candy, selling melon seeds and peanuts, selling cured meats, selling dried goods. And a few stalls selling "fried oil horns (fried crescent dumplings)" and "fried sesame balls"—all freshly fried, the oil bubbling, food dropped in with a "zila" sound, fished out golden and crisp.

The air was all fried fragrance. I took a deep breath—sweet, salty, oily, fragrant—mixed together, as if someone had poured all the flavors of New Year into one pot.

I stopped at a stall selling spring couplets. The vendor was an old man, dozens of hand-written couplets laid out before him. Not printed—written on the spot. Brush, ink, paper, inkstone beside him; customers pick a text, he writes it on the spot. I leaned in to look; he was writing one: "Heaven adds years, people add longevity; spring fills the universe, blessings fill the door." The calligraphy wasn't especially fine, but the strokes were steady, every one set down solidly. Beside him stood a little boy of seven or eight, probably his grandson, responsible for turning the finished couplets onto an empty table to dry the ink.

"How much per couplet?" I asked.

"Thirty yuan."

I ordered one too: "Career smooth, rising step by step; spring breeze on the face, joy overflowing." The old man picked up the brush, dipped it in ink, and began to write. I stood beside him watching his brushwork. His hand didn't tremble—a man over seventy, yet his hand was steady as a young man's when writing brush characters. Ink fell on the red paper, black characters spreading on the red, each stroke as if "growing" out. The little boy puffed at the ink beside him, a drop splattering on the back of his hand; he didn't care, grinned wide. The old man finished and scolded with a laugh: "What are you blowing for, you can't blow it dry anyway." The boy stuck out his tongue and carefully moved the couplet to the empty table behind.

The old man told me to come back for it later, or wait. After a while the boy rolled up the couplet and handed it to me, saying, "Happy New Year." I said, "Thanks. Happy New Year to you too."

I bought some candy and dried goods too, and walked back carrying big bags and small.

***

On New Year's Eve, I pasted that couplet in my rented room. The tape wasn't very sticky; I pasted it twice more before it held. After pasting I stepped back to look—red paper, black characters, especially conspicuous on the gray-white wall, like the only ball of fire in this winter.

The New Year's Eve dinner was made by Xiao Yang. She brought up a thermos, inside steamed lap cheong, stir-fried shredded potato, a pot of chicken soup, and a whole white-cut chicken—not cut, lying whole on the plate. Three dishes and a soup, two bowls of rice, two pairs of chopsticks and two bowls on the table.

While eating, my mother called and said, "What are you eating?" I said, "Lap cheong, shredded potato, chicken soup, and chicken." She was silent for a moment on the other end, then said, "Not bad. As long as there's a dish and a soup."

Those two words again. Like a hidden thread running through my years in Dongguan. Fang Bo said "not bad," Aunt Feng said "for a first time, that's not bad"—all the "not bad"s added together mean: you, in this city, have been seen.

After the New Year's Eve dinner, I washed the dishes; Xiao Yang was still there. The two of us sat by the window. Outside, people had started setting off fireworks; in the empty lot downstairs, sparks sprayed with a "cici" sound. In the distance, scattered firecracker sounds. The canal flowed in the darkness, the lights on the opposite bank reflected on the water, fragmented.

I remembered my first day in Dongguan. Sitting in the back row of the long-distance bus, smelling instant noodles and foot sweat in the cabin, looking at the gray-white road outside, with only one thought in my mind: how can this place be so flat.

Now three years had passed. I stood at this window, watching the scattered fireworks in the distance, and felt Dongguan was not flat at all. It had rises and falls, gullies and ravines, things bottomless. Those things were not on the skyline; they were under your feet, on the roads you walked every day, in the flicker in someone's eyes when you spoke to them.

***

On the first day of the New Year I slept until past nine. The curtain wasn't fully drawn; a shaft of light split in through the gap, striking the foot of the bed, dazzlingly bright. I squinted at the crack in the ceiling for a while—I'd looked at that crack for three years; it hadn't changed, but my mood looking at it had. Before I thought it looked like a river, a thread, a road; today I felt it looked like nothing—it was just a crack. The wall had cracked; very normal. Walls grow old too.

I rolled out of bed, barefoot on the tiles, cold. Went to the kitchen to boil water. Yesterday I'd sworn that today I'd eat something "decent," but the fridge held nothing but lap cheong and that whole chicken—leftover from New Year's Eve. I took it out, and following what Xiao Yang had taught me, cut along the bone seams. The cuts weren't pretty; bone stubs showed in several places, but at least it was divided into pieces. Steamed it, ate with rice, alone.

Then I walked east along Hongfu Road. Dongguan on the first day of the New Year was very quiet—not the quiet of an empty city, but the quiet of "everyone has closed their doors to celebrate." Few cars on the road, the traffic lights seemed to have slowed too; a green light could last a long time.

I remembered when I first came to Dongguan. Back then I really didn't know where I was. Body in Dongguan, heart drifting in Zhanjiang. Later, slowly, the dragon boat drumbeats, the flower lantern frames, the chopsticks of the potluck, the water of the kumquat leaves—these things stood up like road signs, one by one, telling me: you are here.

North along Hongfu Road is the central district of Nancheng, high-rises, traffic, today deserted. I walked a circle and came back.

***

On the second day of the New Year, I went alone to Qiaotou. A local friend had mentioned "there are still people in Qiaotou's old street making nian gao," and I wanted to try my luck.

On the old street of Qiaotou town, I passed a shop called "Luo Family Rice Shop." Several large bamboo baskets at the door, filled with various rices—glutinous, japonica, brown, red, black. A handwritten paper on the wall: "Nian gao pre-orders open. Radish cake, taro cake, nine-layer cake. Pickup from the twentieth of the twelfth lunar month."

Nian gao. Dongguan people must make it for New Year.

I went in. A middle-aged woman—called Sister Luo—was kneading rice-flour dough at the work board. Several steamers of finished radish cake already sat on the board, steaming. She glanced up at me, didn't stop. "What are you buying?"

"Just looking at the nian gao."

"The nine-layer cake needs waiting. I just steamed two steamers of radish cake today; want to try?"

She cut a small piece of radish cake and handed it to me. I bit in—the soft glutinousness of the rice flour wrapped around the fresh sweetness of shredded radish, plus the savor of lap cheong cubes and dried shrimp. Not the kind bought outside with too much MSG, but the home-made kind—the flavor was "scattered," not "gathered." Every bite tasted something different: the salt of the lap cheong, the savor of the dried shrimp, the sweetness of the radish, the faint heat of pepper. They didn't melt into one; each stood independent yet propped up the others—like the people in this city of Dongguan.

"Good?"

"Good."

"Then buy it. Thirty-five per steamer."

I bought it. Carrying that steamer of radish cake along Qiaotou's old street, the sun warm on my back. Someone by the road was drying lap cheong, someone hanging laundry. A child in new clothes ran past, a string of candied haws in hand.

I remembered New Year when I was little; my mother would steam nian gao on the twenty-eighth. She made fa gao—flour, sugar, yeast, steamed until puffed and fluffy, the top split open in a few cracks, like laughing. I'd always wait by the pot, and the moment the lid came off I'd reach for one, sucking in air because it was so hot. My mother would say, "Slow down!"

Now I buy radish cake myself, carry it myself. But that feeling of "New Year is here" is the same.

***

On the third day of the New Year, Xiao Yang was still at the rented room. The steamer of radish cake I'd brought back from Qiaotou had gone cold; I broke off a piece and put it in my mouth. The sweetness of the radish, the salt of the lap cheong, the savor of the dried shrimp—all there. Just as I picked up another piece, she reached over and snatched it away. She took a bite and said, "Not bad." Then smiled.

"Not bad" again.

I thought of Sister Luo kneading the rice-flour dough. Her hands pressing back and forth on the dough, every press carrying a rhythm—not an even rhythm, but with fast and slow, like breathing. She didn't need to look at a clock, didn't need a timer; her hands knew when to press hard, when to let go.

That "knowing" is ground out over years, decades, a lifetime. Like Master Mo's fingers threading among the meat cubes when mixing cured meat, like the evenness of force in Aunt Feng's rattan weaving, like the steadiness of every stroke when the old man at Machong wrote spring couplets.

Dongguan's folk custom is not written in books. It grows in these people's hands, eyes, breath. When you see them do a thing, you are seeing the deepest thing in this city.

On the fourth day of the New Year, Xiao Yang left. The room suddenly felt empty.

I dug out an old notebook—a supermarket promotion giveaway from the year I first came to Dongguan, a company logo printed on the cover. Inside were some scattered notes: how much rent, how much utilities, bus routes, phone numbers of a few friends. Flipping to the last page, I found I'd written something at some point—

"Dongguan hasn't given me anything. But it hasn't taken anything away either. It just let me stay. And staying and staying, I stayed this long."

I looked at those words, couldn't remember when I'd written them. Maybe some late night, maybe some drunk evening. But every word was true.

Dongguan hasn't given me anything—no house, no household registration, no "local" identity. But it hasn't taken anything away either—didn't take my time (I gave it willingly), didn't take my curiosity, didn't take my wish to stay.

It just let me stay. And staying and staying, I grew my own roots.

Those roots are very fine, very shallow. But each one connects to a person, an event, a flavor, a sound. Chen Ah Po's lap cheong, Aunt Feng's rattan basket, Master Mo's cured meat, the grandfather and grandson's spring couplets at Machong, Sister Luo's radish cake, the piece Xiao Yang snatched away—these things are like fine threads reaching out from every corner of Dongguan, winding around me.

I wasn't tied up. I reached out myself and caught hold of them.

## Section Fifteen: Seven-Vegetable Dish

On the fifth day of the New Year, work resumed.

At eight in the morning the alarm went off. I turned it off, lay there five more minutes. Then got up, washed, changed, went downstairs. The convenience store was open; Xiao Yang stood behind the counter, same expression—not smiling, but her eyes were alive. I bought a bottle of water; she handed it to me and said, "Good luck with the start of work."

I was taken aback. Then smiled and said, "Good luck with the start of work."

Walking out of the convenience store, the cold wind hit my face. I shrank my neck and headed for the bus stop. More people on the road now; the traffic lights had returned to their usual rhythm; the breakfast shop's steamer was sending up white steam again. Dongguan was waking from its New Year languor, like a person stretching, turning over, getting ready to start a new day.

***

On the seventh day of the New Year, Renri.

Only then did I learn that Dongguan people call the seventh day of the first lunar month "Renri"—the birthday of people. Not of some particular person, but of everyone.

I learned this at the wet market.

That day I went downstairs to buy groceries—not because I was going to make the seven-vegetable dish, but because the fridge was empty, even the instant noodles gone. When I got to the market, I found the whole place taken over by a strange kind of green. Not the usual green, but seven different shades of green crowded together, piled on most stalls' counters, as if someone had knocked over a paint palette onto the vegetable leaves.

An old woman was picking scallions. Not grabbing a handful at random, but looking at them one by one, picking out the ones with too much mud on the roots and yellowing leaves, keeping the straightest few. I stood beside her waiting until she finished, then asked, "Ah Po, why is everyone selling these today?"

She looked up at me. "Renri. You didn't know?"

"Didn't know."

"You're an outsider." She reached her conclusion, paused, then said, "You buying? I'll put together a set for you. Seven kinds, not one can be missing."

She took a small handful from several baskets beside her: celery, scallion, garlic, Chinese chives, cilantro, white radish (a small slice cut off), and finally a handful of spring greens. Not much of each, just enough for one person for one meal. She put them in a plastic bag and handed it to me. "Ten yuan."

I paid and carried that bag of green home. Sunlight shone on the plastic bag, the seven vegetables' colors showing through, like a watercolor. I suddenly felt—this was more "New Year" than the spring couplet. The couplet is pasted on the door, for others to see. This bag of vegetables is to be eaten, going straight into your body, becoming your blood, your flesh, your strength for the new year.

But I didn't know how to cook the seven-vegetable dish. My mother had never taught me—Zhanjiang didn't seem to observe Renri. I carried the bag of vegetables downstairs, hesitated a moment, then headed for Chen Ah Po's.

***

When she opened the door she was gnawing on an apple. Seeing the vegetables in my hand, before I could speak she said "oh," and stepped aside to let me in.

Chen Ah Po's kitchen was small, but everything had its fixed place. The cutting board by the window, the knife hanging on the wall, the soy sauce bottle and vinegar bottle standing side by side by the stove. She took the bag of vegetables, dumped them in the sink, turned on the tap.

"Washed?" she asked.

"No."

"Wash first. There's mud on them."

I stood beside her watching her wash. She washed slowly, opening each leaf to rinse, her fingers rubbing along the veins. Water rushed over the leaves with a very soft "shasha" sound. After washing she fished them out, drained them, put them on the cutting board. Then she began to cut. Celery into sections, scallion into chopped bits, garlic smashed and minced, chives into inch-long sections, cilantro chopped fine, white radish into shreds, spring greens torn into pieces.

The sound of the knife on the board was "tok, tok, tok," neither hurried nor slow. Her hands were no longer young—the knuckles a little deformed, the skin hanging loosely on the bones—but very steady when cutting. Each cut landed in about the same place, the sections cut to the same length.

"Why eat these seven?" I asked.

"For the auspicious meaning." She didn't look up.

"What meaning?"

"Celery—diligence. Scallion—cleverness. Garlic—good at reckoning. Chives—long-lasting. Cilantro—fate, affinity. White radish—purity. Spring greens—vitality." She cut as she spoke, like reciting a long poem, every word familiar, but the meaning when put together had to be savored slowly.

"So if you eat them you'll really be clever, really be good at reckoning?"

She stopped her knife and glanced at me. "You young people, why do you always ask 'is it real or fake'?"

I felt a little embarrassed at her words.

"It's not real or fake." She picked up the knife again. "It's that when you eat it, you have to be thinking about these things in your heart. When you eat the celery you think 'this year I'll be more diligent,' when you eat the scallion you think 'I need to use my head more.' It's not the vegetable that's spiritual; it's your own thoughts that are spiritual."

Standing in the kitchen, I suddenly said nothing.

This answer weighed much more than I expected. Not the light, floating explanation of "traditional custom," but a very plain truth—on the seventh day of the New Year, with seven kinds of vegetables, you remind yourself: this year, be diligent, be clever, be good at reckoning, be enduring, cherish fate, be clean and pure, have vitality. This isn't superstition. It's setting the tone for yourself.

Like looking toward the southeast on the morning of the first day of the New Year. Not because the God of Joy is really in that direction, but because after you look, you have a "beginning" in your heart.

***

Chen Ah Po taught me to cook the seven-vegetable dish. The steps were simple: heat the wok, add oil, first pop the minced garlic and white scallion parts, then in turn add the shredded white radish, spring greens, celery, chives, cilantro, and finally sprinkle the chopped scallion. No meat, no oyster sauce, just a little salt and a little white pepper. She said, "The Renri dish should be vegetarian. There are plenty of chances to eat meat all year; on this day eat vegetarian, cleanse the stomach and cleanse the mind."

The wok heated, the oil went in, "zila." The garlic and white scallion tumbled in the oil, the fragrance bursting out. I stood at the stove holding the spatula, suddenly a little nervous—afraid the fire was too high, afraid I'd put in too much salt, afraid I'd overcook it. Chen Ah Po stood beside me, not lifting a hand, only saying a word at the key moments: "Add the radish." "Turn it." "Salt." "Okay, turn off the heat."

When it finally came out of the wok, all seven colors were still there—the green of the celery, the green of the chopped scallion, the deep green of the chives, the flecked green of the cilantro, the pale green of the spring greens, the white of the shredded radish, the pale yellow of the minced garlic. Mixed together, not a mess, but a "harmony." Each still recognizable as itself, yet tangled with the others, no longer separable.

I divided it into two takeout boxes. Pushed one toward Chen Ah Po, put the lid on the other and tucked it into a plastic bag.

Swung by the convenience store, left a box for Xiao Yang. She was stocking shelves; I set the box down across the counter. She glanced at it, didn't ask, just nodded. I wanted her to taste it too—not entirely to repay the favor of that New Year's Eve dinner. It's just that this thing is meaningless eaten alone; seven vegetables, it's supposed to be shared.

Back in the rented room, I filled a bowl and tasted it. Light. Not bland—there was flavor, but the flavor didn't come from seasoning; it came from the vegetables themselves. The sweetness of the white radish, the tenderness of the spring greens, the crispness of the celery, the fragrance of the chives—each independent, yet propping up the others. Like a group of people sitting at one table eating, each saying their own thing, but the voices together are the sound of "being together."

I thought of what Chen Ah Po said, that she blanches the seven vegetables and freezes them in the fridge, to stir-fry for her son when he comes back. Nothing remarkable. But think about it: she spends Renri alone, makes the seven-vegetable dish, eats one meal herself, then cleans up the rest and freezes it, waiting for her son to come back. In that word "wait" is hidden a mother's whole understanding of "reunion."

Not the grand reunion kind. It's—when you're not here, I keep your portion.

***

Day after day, year after year. With enough days, you grow into this place. Like the aerial roots of that banyan—not plunging down all at once, but hanging down bit by bit, touching the soil, then rooting in bit by bit.

Passing the wet market, I saw that old woman from that morning was still there. Her vegetables were almost sold out; only a few scallions and a bit of cilantro left in the basket. She sat on a low stool, a thermos and a piece of nian gao before her. The nian gao was sliced and pan-fried, golden on both sides, the edges a little charred and crisp. She took a small bite, chewed slowly, eyes on the passersby.

I walked over and squatted down.

"Ah Po, your vegetables are sold out."

"Mm. A little left, I'll eat it myself."

She smiled. A few teeth missing, but the smile was still bright.

I squatted on the market floor, looking at an old vegetable seller, sitting on a low stool, a piece of fried nian gao in her hand. Sunlight fell on her face, every wrinkle filled with light.

This is all of Dongguan's folk custom—dragon boats, flower lanterns, puppets, Cantonese opera, muyu songs, the Selling-Body Festival, grave-sweeping, lap cheong, the seven-vegetable dish—they all ultimately point to the same thing: remember.

Remember who you are. Remember where you came from. Remember whom you have loved. Remember whom you have waited for. Remember every day you have lived in this place.

These things don't need to be written on paper. They grow in your hands (calluses), on your tongue (flavor), in your body (habit), in your memory (a bowl of seven vegetables).

I stood up and said goodbye to the old woman. She nodded and took another bite of nian gao.

The wind blew from the north, a little warmer than in the morning. I didn't know whether this year I'd really be more diligent, more clever, better at reckoning. But I knew—from today on, whenever I eat celery, I'll remember what Chen Ah Po said standing at the stove:

"It's not the vegetable that's spiritual; it's your own thoughts that are spiritual."

## Section Sixteen: The Lamenting Songs of Marriage

> Some of the material in this section was transcribed from recordings afterward with the help of a local colleague.

***

Before the clean, clear breath of the seven-vegetable dish had fully dispersed, spring arrived.

After the Beginning of Spring, I went to Shatian.

I'd heard it while chatting with a colleague about Dongguan customs: "The Tanka people here in Shatian—when a daughter marries, they sing laments, crying marriages, and can sing for days on end."

That weekend I dragged along a colleague from Shatian town, and the two of us took the bus over. Once in town, though, I couldn't name the specific village—I only remembered it was somewhere along the river. So we were like two headless flies, going village by village in roughly the right direction, asking around.

I asked for directions at the mouth of one alley. An old woman sat on her doorstep picking vegetables. After hearing me out, she tossed the vegetable leaves to the ground and pointed toward the river: "Over there. There's an old woman. When she married over, she was brought by rowboat. Her feet never touched the ground."

I walked in the direction she pointed. A row of old houses along the river, and at the very end a wooden door with peeling green paint. The door was open. A small, thin old woman sat in the main hall, wearing a dark blue front-buttoned jacket, her hair gathered in a black hairnet and coiled at the back of her head. She looked up at me, her eyes very bright.

Across from her sat several people, tags like work badges hanging on their chests—I couldn't quite make them out. Someone was taking notes in a notebook, someone was holding a camera, and another was shouldering a video camera. They looked like they were from the town cultural station, or some survey team from the city—only later did I learn they were doing an inventory and registration for intangible cultural heritage. The year was 2006; this kind of thing was happening everywhere.

My colleague and I stood at the door, not wanting to intrude, just leaning against the doorframe, listening quietly to the conversation inside.

***

"When you married, were you brought over on the water?"

She gave an "mm." "My husband rowed a boat to come get me. My feet didn't touch the ground. He carried me onto the boat, the boat went to his family's landing, then he carried me ashore. Ashore we stepped over a brazier, then entered the house."

"Why couldn't your feet touch the ground?"

"Tanka custom. When the bride leaves her natal home and enters the groom's house, the stretch in between is not road—it's water. Water is the sedan chair. If your foot touches the ground, the sedan chair is broken." She paused. "Nobody cares about that now. When my granddaughter married she wore a wedding dress and rode in a car; her feet stepped all over the ground."

When she said "stepped all over the ground" there was a smile on her face—not mockery, but the kind of smile that says "the times have passed." As if speaking of something very far away, something that had to do with her and yet didn't.

"And the laments? Can you still sing them?"

Her eyes flickered.

"I've sung them. Three nights before I married, my mother, my older sister, my aunt sat around me. One line each, singing. My mother lamented first—'Daughter, you've eaten your mother's rice and grown to seventeen or eighteen, tonight you cross the boat to another house.' My sister lamented—'Sister, sister, father and mother raised you like a pearl; the pearl goes and the box is empty, mother's heart withers.' My aunt urged—'When you go to another's door, rise early and sweep the courtyard, come evening serve your parents-in-law.' I lamented myself—'Mother, mother, outside the hedge there is bamboo, inside the hedge there is orchid; the orchid moves to another garden, and the bamboo too grows cold.'"

She recited in Shatian speech, with a Tanka accent—not singing, but with a tune. Every final sound sank downward, like a leaf falling into water. I couldn't understand all of it, but when those three words "Mother, mother" fell, the hall suddenly went quiet. The flame of the little oil lamp before the shrine didn't move, but I seemed to see it tremble.

"By the third night, my mother cried. Not loud crying—tears running down her face, her mouth still singing. I cried too."

She picked up the cup of glutinous rice tea on the table, took a sip, set it back.

***

While they paused their questioning, I quietly made out the notebook by the doorframe—not a book, but a thin hand-copied pamphlet, the paper the kind from a blue-covered student exercise book, the corners curled, blackened by hands. Inside, ballpoint pen filled every page with the lyrics of laments; beside some characters were notes in homophones, clearly written by a younger generation helping to record the sounds.

The old woman lowered her head, lips moving, no sound. As if in her heart she were lamenting those lines once more.

I thought of listening to Aunt Zhen sing *The Princess Changping* at the Daojiao opera society—when she sang "Fragrant Death," someone in the audience wiped the corner of their eye. Of watching Master Chen work the puppets in Shipai—lying on the stage, shoulders rising and falling. Of watching Uncle Li shave bamboo strips in Zhongtang—the calluses on his fingers shining. Of listening to Uncle Li tap muyu songs in Hengmei—planting each word into the air like seeds.

This old woman now, in the hall, silently moving her lips—it was the same thing as those others.

Not "performance." It's that there's a string inside the body; when it loosens it itches, when it tightens it hurts. Only by singing it out, tapping it out, shaving it out, moving it out, does the person become steady again.

The Tanka people call this string "lament." Lamenting the family sister, lamenting fate, lamenting marriage. The word "lament" here is not sighing—it is slowly releasing your fate from your mouth, releasing it for your mother to hear, for your sister to hear, for Lord Hong Sheng to hear, for the water to hear.

***

The old woman's hall was not large. A small shrine faced the front—not a Buddha, probably "Lord Hong Sheng," the sea god of the boat people. Beside the shrine hung an old couplet, the red paper faded to pinkish white: "Favorable wind and rain, peace on water and land."

On the wall hung an old photograph, black and white, yellowed. In it she wore a red wedding jacket, the collar embroidered with floral borders, a red cloth over her head—not the usual red bridal veil, but the Tanka woman's "mother-covering umbrella": a cloth umbrella with red strips tied to the rim. Her husband stood beside her, wearing a felt hat, a red cloth across his chest, hands hanging down, smiling a little stiffly.

The background of the photo was a boat. At the bow, a pot of flowers, a pot of grass.

"Was that put there for the betrothal?" asked the cultural station people.

"No. That's ordinary. Flowers at the stern means the family has an unmarried daughter; grass means the family has an unmarried son. My natal family's boat had flowers at the stern, his family's boat had grass. The matchmaker saw it and came to ask. That's how it became."

People around three hundred years ago did this; when the old woman was young, they did this. Now on the river of this village, those pots of flowers and grass are long gone, leaving only this one in the photograph.

***

"Antiphonal singing? Was there antiphonal singing at the wedding?"

"Yes. When his family's boat came alongside, his older cousin sang first—'Flowers bloom at the bow, fragrance at the stern; I ask the elder sister, will you cross the boat corridor?' My bridesmaid replied—'Water runs swift at the bow, deep at the stern; without a song you won't win the elder sister's heart.' Back and forth, until the people on the bank were all laughing. My brother said 'All right, all right, he's won.' Then my husband took the red cord and led me over."

As she spoke, the corners of her mouth curved; that bit of a smile quickly leveled off again.

The cultural station people asked a few more things—mostly what year she was born, at what age she learned the laments, who else in the village could still sing them. The old woman answered briefly, sometimes just "mm." When the questioning wound down, the girl with the notebook closed it and said, "Ah Po, we'll go look for other families."

The group gathered their things and headed out. Only then were my colleague and I "seen"—one of the girls asked what we did; my colleague answered a few words in the local dialect, saying a friend was curious and had been brought along to look. She didn't ask more, just nodded and moved on.

After they left, the hall emptied out. The old woman didn't see them off, didn't ask us to stay—just picked up that cup of glutinous rice tea again. Sunlight slanted in from the doorway, illuminating a few white hairs that had escaped her hairnet.

***

Suddenly singing came from inside the house again. Very soft, as if she couldn't hold it back, or as if she'd never intended anyone to hear—

> Mother, mother, raising a child is like raising a riverside willow,
> The willow grows fifteen feet tall, its shadow covers the boat,
> The boat goes and mother is still here; the willow goes and the shadow does not stay.

She lamented alone. In the hall there was no mother, no sister, no Lord Hong Sheng—only a small oil lamp on the wall that never went out. But she lamented anyway. Her voice was so low it was almost talking to herself, yet the tune sank down, sank beneath the floorboards, and came up again from beneath the floorboards.

It was a thing an old woman, on the night before her marriage, had lamented line by line before an oil lamp. When the lamenting was done, the singer grew old, the listeners dispersed, and it could only lie in a notebook. But today, it was recited once more by an old woman in Shatian speech, recited for two uninvited guests, an oil lamp, and a river that no longer had pots of flowers and grass.

The old woman's hand-copied book was not knowledge. It was life. Her grandmother's life, her mother's life, her own. Life is a thing that cannot be borrowed; it can only be encountered.

I stood at the door, my throat tight, and for a long while didn't dare make a sound.

***

On the bus back to Nancheng, I leaned against the window.

The bus went a long way; I looked back through the rear window. She stood in front of that row of old houses, the green-painted door half open, her whole body shrunk within the doorframe, like that pot of flowers in the photograph, like that pot of grass at the stern.

Writing about Dongguan's customs up to here, for the first time I felt it wasn't "I am recording them," but that they came one by one and touched me.

The dragon boat touched my chest—struck by the drum. The flower lantern touched my eyes—the shadow of bamboo strips. The puppet touched my shoulder—Master Chen's shoulder rising. Cantonese opera touched the corner of my eye—the cotton in Aunt Zhen's hand as she removed her makeup. The muyu song touched my eardrum—Uncle Li's thread. The Selling-Body Festival water touched my whole body. The incense smoke touched my nose. The argument under the banyan touched my temples, the rattan basket touched my palm. The thousand-year-old tree touched my fingertips. The ashes of Double Ninth touched my shoes. The lap cheong oil touched the bottom of my bowl. The ink of the handwritten spring couplet touched the pads of my fingers. The roots of the Renri vegetables touched my tongue.

Today, the old woman's "Mother, mother" touched my throat.

My throat is still tight now.

After coming back from Shatian, I didn't speak for several days. In early February, on the pedestrian street, I ran into Teacher Liu.

## Section Seventeen: Lighting the Lantern

In early February, on the pedestrian street. Teacher Liu was coming out of the training center with a cloth bag; I happened to run into him. His son Liu Yi had come back from Shenzhen for the weekend, and he was dragged over to be introduced to me. Liu Yi was in his early twenties, wore glasses and a gray hoodie, looked like someone who wrote code. He called me "Bro," I called him by name.

While chatting, Liu Yi suddenly said, "My dad went back to Zhangmutou last year to light a lamp."

Teacher Liu added from the side, "His grandfather passed a few years ago, ninety-one. Before he went, he said one thing—'You still haven't lit a lamp for me.'"

For a moment I didn't know what to say. Teacher Liu looked down, flipping through the lesson plans in his cloth bag. Liu Yi glanced at his father and didn't continue.

Only later did I learn that "lighting the lamp" is a tradition of the Hakka town of Zhangmutou. If a male child is born in the previous year, in the first month of the following year a paper lantern must be hung in the ancestral hall—"lamp" is homophonous with "ding" (male member), so it's called "reporting the ding." Some ancestral halls have several hundred hanging from the beams, layer upon layer like a tree of flowers. There is also a "lamp wine" banquet, to which the whole village is invited.

But what the materials didn't say was—what if the child born was a daughter?

Only later did Liu Yi make that implication clear. Grandfather had been waiting for him to add a grandson so he could go light the lamp in the ancestral hall, but after he married he had a daughter. Grandfather said—a daughter doesn't count, the lamp still wasn't lit.

His tone was very flat. But I could hear in it a kind of suspended feeling of "unfinished." Like you owe someone a "thank you," and that person is gone, and you can never repay it in this life.

***

On a Saturday in mid-February, after much pestering, Liu Yi agreed to take me to see their village ancestral hall.

More than an hour's drive from Nancheng. After leaving the city, the hills gradually increased. Zhangmutou is a mountain town; the houses are built along the slopes, the roads curve—unlike most Dongguan towns with their flatland factory cities, it's more like a Hakka mountain village from eastern Guangdong transplanted into the Pearl River Delta.

Liu Yi's village is at the northernmost edge of the town. A concrete road leads in, rice fields and fish ponds on both sides. In February, the rice fields had just been transplanted; the water surface reflected the sky, a tender green.

The ancestral hall is in the center of the village. Not an old building turned tourist site, just an ordinary Hakka ancestral hall: blue-brick walls, gray tile roof, a pair of stone lions at the door, half the red paint gone. A red couplet pasted at the entrance: "Pengcheng lineage, Lu Pavilion family renown."

Walking in, a dozen or so lamps hung from the beams of the main hall—not the spectacular scene of several hundred I'd imagined, just a dozen paper red lanterns, some faded, some still new. Below each lamp hung a red cloth strip with a name and birth date written on it. No candles needed in daytime, but they hung there quietly, and you could feel a kind of "weight"—behind each one stood an infant, a pair of parents, a family's expectation.

I counted. Fourteen lamps. Fourteen lamps, corresponding to fourteen people. That number stirred something in me—not because it was few, but because it was "real." Some villages don't even get one lamp in a year.

In the corner of the hall were piled old things: bamboo baskets, a rake missing teeth, a rusted iron censer. A thick layer of dust. The lamps were new, representing "coming"; the old things represented "going." Coming and going, the ancestral hall is a transfer station.

***

We stood in the hall for about ten minutes. Then a man in his fifties walked in, wearing a blue work jacket, carrying a plastic bag. He saw Liu Yi and paused.

"Yi boy, you're back?"

"Uncle, just got back today. You're here to light a lamp for your grandson?"

Uncle Liu walked to the offering table, took a new lantern from the bag—a size smaller than those on the beams, more finely made, painted with "Qilin Bringing the Child." He brought over a stool, stood on it, and hung the lamp on the beam, his movements very gentle, as if laying down a baby who had just fallen asleep. After hanging it, he stepped back two paces and looked up for a while.

"My son works in Chang'an. The grandson stays home, I take care of him. His mother is at the Humen electronics factory, comes back once a month." He said all this in a flat tone, like reading a menu.

"Your son didn't come back to hang it?"

"Didn't come back. I hung it for him."

He pulled out his phone, a Nokia flip phone with a color screen. He found a photo to show me—very blurry, just this lamp hanging on the beam, beside it the fourteen old ones. The light was dim, but the red lantern stood out.

"What did he say?"

"He said 'good.'"

One word "good." No exclamation mark, no expression. Just one word. I stood in the ancestral hall watching this "good" extend from the phone screen to the new lamp on the beam, then to this man in his fifties. He hung the lamp for his son; the son is in Chang'an, the daughter-in-law in Humen, the grandson in Zhangmutou cared for by grandfather. One family scattered across three towns in Dongguan, but at this moment, because of one lamp, connected.

This is not some "transmission of traditional culture." It is a migrant worker family using one lantern to complete a "return to the ancestors."

***

After Uncle Liu finished hanging the lamp, he patted the dust off his hands and invited us to his home for tea.

A few minutes' walk along the alley brought us to a two-story house. Bougainvillea planted at the door. Inside, a child of just over a year was crawling in the living room, barefoot, wearing a little yellow shirt. Seeing Uncle Liu come in, he grinned, drool running from the corner of his mouth. Uncle Liu bent down and picked him up, wiped his mouth with his sleeve.

"This is my grandson."

The child wasn't shy, stared at me for a while, reached out to grab Uncle Liu's glasses. Uncle Liu laughed and dodged.

He held his grandson and sat on the sofa, his tone still flat. I suddenly realized—"lighting the lamp" for him was not just "reporting the ding." His wife had passed a few years ago, his son was not by his side, the grandson was his only "continuation." He went to the ancestral hall to hang the lamp, completing a wish for his wife—she must have also longed to hold a grandson, to go light the lamp in the ancestral hall. Now she couldn't see it, but he went for her.

The living room was very quiet. The child crawled back and forth on his lap, occasionally giving an "ah-ah." Sunlight came in from the east, falling on the child's back, warm yellow.

I thought of the Shatian old woman's hand-copied book. She said when she was gone she'd burn it. The lamp Uncle Liu hung for his son today was, in a sense, the same thing—turning one person's longing into something visible, hanging it somewhere. What the old woman hung was "lament," what Uncle Liu hung was "lamp." One draws inward, one releases outward. But both are "keeping."

***

While drinking tea, Uncle Liu told of lighting lamps when he was a child.

"In my father's generation, lighting lamps was very lively. Starting from the eighth day of the first month, every day someone's family went to the ancestral hall. After hanging the lamp they held 'lamp wine,' and all the men of the village came to eat. Whose family's lamp wine was biggest, that family had face."

"And now?"

"Now it's not so lively. The young people have all gone out. Some villages don't hang a single lamp in a year. Our village is still all right—last year we hung three. The year before, not one."

"Do you think this custom will continue?"

He set down his teacup and looked at the grandson in his arms.

"I don't know. But today I hung the lamp. That's enough. My son's generation, maybe they won't hang any at all."

"Enough" again.

In all my years in Dongguan, I'd heard too many people say "enough." Aunt Zhen finished a section of Cantonese opera, said "enough." Uncle Li finished a flower lantern, said "enough." Aunt Feng finished a rattan basket, said "enough." Fang Bo finished a steamer of zongzi, said "enough." The old woman finished a marriage lament, said "enough."

These people's "enough" is not surrender; it is—I did this thing. I finished it. It existed. No matter whether anyone does it in the future, at least today, it is here.

Today Uncle Liu hung a lamp on the beam of the ancestral hall. That lamp will stay there. Hanging together with the fourteen old ones, becoming fifteen. Fifteen lamps, fifteen names, fifteen lives.

***

The next day Liu Yi went back to Shenzhen. I took the bus back from Zhangmutou alone. The bus went a long way; I looked back through the rear window. The ancestral hall was no longer visible, the hills no longer visible, only rows of factory buildings on both sides of the highway. But that red lantern, painted with Qilin Bringing the Child, seemed still to hang before my eyes.

On the way back to Nancheng I fell asleep on the bus. When I woke, the bus had entered the city. I got off, bought a bottle of water, stood by the road drinking.

February, Dongguan's wind is warm. The kapok trees along the road had bloomed; big red flowers fell to the ground, no one picked them up. I looked at those fallen flowers and suddenly thought of the lamps in the ancestral hall. Paper lanterns, also red. Kapok flowers falling to the ground will rot; lanterns hanging on the beams will also grow old. But the color red—in Dongguan's spring it's everywhere. The lamps of the ancestral hall, the bride's veil, the red paper of spring couplets, the red of lucky-money envelopes.

Red in Dongguan is not as simple as "festive." It is—"I am here." You hang a red lantern on the beam of the ancestral hall, paste a red couplet on the door, stuff a red envelope into someone's pocket—you are using red to mark your existence. Telling the ancestors: I have come. Telling those who come after: I am here.

I thought of Uncle Liu's grandson. When he grows up, will he also go to the ancestral hall to hang a lamp? Or will the ancestral hall, by his generation, have become a "cultural space," the lamp an "exhibit."

I don't know. But at least today, his grandfather hung one for him. That lamp is still hanging on the beam now, red, painted with Qilin Bringing the Child. It doesn't light up, but it is there.

## Section Eighteen: Hanging Paper

Before the weight of that red lantern had faded from my heart, Qingming arrived. Dongguan's sky began to "return to damp." Not the kind of damp of the southern-return days when walls sweat beads of water, but a deeper, slower damp—the moisture in the air seemed to have been absorbed full by something; walking outside, clothes stuck to your back, not sweat, but the air itself pouring water into your pores.

Xiao Yang wore a light gray hoodie today, hair tied in a bun. In her hand she carried a plastic bag holding several dark blue-green things the size of a fist—round, with a fine fuzz on the surface.

"What are these?"

"Ai ban. Hakka Qingming food."

"You're Hakka?"

"I'm from Hunan. My mom is in Zhangmutou. My dad now is a Zhangmutou man; he made them, gave me a few."

She handed me one. Taking it, my palm felt the warmth—steamed not long ago. The smell of mugwort seeped from the plastic bag, faint, bitter, but behind the bitterness a layer of sweetness.

"Your dad is Hakka, so his family does 'hanging paper' at Qingming?"

She glanced at me. "How do you know?"

Her eyes curved. "His family goes to hang paper at Qingming. He told me to come this year too."

"Are you going?"

"Going. Can't really treat myself as an outsider forever. The ai ban my dad makes are a hundred times better than what's sold outside."

She left. I stood downstairs, that ai ban in my hand. Warm, the mugwort smell growing stronger. A Hunan girl, led by a steamer of ai ban into a completely unfamiliar Hakka ritual. I thought of the lamp on the beam of the Zhangmutou ancestral hall—this mountain town seemed fated to me. This time, I would go with Xiao Yang to see the Hakka Qingming.

***

The day before Qingming, I went with Xiao Yang to Zhangmutou. Her father, Uncle Qiang, was just past fifty, a mason on the town's construction sites, his skin tanned very dark, his palms rough as sandpaper.

In Uncle Qiang's small courtyard, ai ban were being steamed. Xiao Yang's mother still had to work today, wasn't home. Xiao Yang wore an old T-shirt, her hands covered in glutinous rice flour and mugwort juice, greenish. She bent over wrapping the ban—pinched off a ball of rice dough, flattened it, wrapped in peanut-sesame-sugar filling, sealed, rolled round, then pressed a little flat, placed on a banana leaf, stacked into the steamer. Her movements were very skilled.

Uncle Qiang sat on a low stool nearby, a large basin of kneaded glutinous rice dough before him. He was responsible for kneading—the greenish dough turned over and over in his hands, like kneading a living thing. The mugwort juice was already worked in; the green was uneven, darker in some places, lighter in others, like marble veining.

"Uncle, why is the ai ban dough green?"

"Mugwort. The mugwort around Qingming is the tenderest. Pick it, blanch it, crush it, squeeze the juice, mix it into the glutinous rice flour." He didn't look up. "Mugwort dispels damp. Around Qingming the damp is heavy; eating ai ban is just right."

"And the filling?"

"Sweet uses peanut, sesame, sugar. Savory uses cured meat, dried shrimp, dried radish. Today we're making sweet. Xiao Yang likes sweet."

Xiao Yang gave an "hmph" beside him, but the corner of her mouth was up.

I squatted down to watch her wrap. Her technique was slower than Uncle Qiang's, but each ban came out neat. Asked how long she'd been learning, she said since last Qingming; last year hers were ugly, this year much better.

"Uncle taught you?"

"He taught. But his hands are too thick; the ban he wraps look like rocks. I learned from my mom."

Hearing this, Uncle Qiang's hands paused on the dough, then went on kneading. "Her mom is from Hunan, knows how to make ciba. She uses the ciba gestures on this; she wraps better than I do."

A Hakka man in his fifties admitting that a twenty-four-year-old Hunan girl wraps ai ban better than he does—in many places this might not happen, but in Dongguan, in this small courtyard, it happened just like that.

***

That day, I went with them to hang paper.

"Hanging paper" is the Hakka term. The Cantonese call it "bai shan," worshipping the mountain; the Hakka call it "hanging paper"—because the most important step in the ritual is to press a stack of paper money (called "earth paper") onto the grave with a stone, like "covering the grave with a quilt." The paper money is yellow, stamped with coin patterns, a stack of about twenty sheets. After pressing it on, from a distance the grave has an extra layer of yellow, like a tile laid over it.

Uncle Qiang carried the basket and led the way. In the basket: three bowls of rice, three cups of wine, a piece of roast pork, a white-cut chicken, several fruits, a stack of earth paper, several sticks of incense, a roll of firecrackers. Xiao Yang followed behind, carrying a thermos—inside, the freshly steamed ai ban, still hot.

The hill wasn't far, just on the low slope behind the village. The path was dirt, softened by the rain of the past two days; a step sank in half an inch. Xiao Yang wore white sneakers; after a few steps they were covered in mud. She didn't complain, occasionally stopping to stamp her feet.

We reached the grave. Uncle Qiang first used a sickle to cut the grass around the grave. Xiao Yang helped carry the cut grass to the side. Then the offerings were laid out: roast pork in the middle, chicken on the right, three bowls of rice, three cups of wine. Incense lit, stuck into the earth before the grave.

Then—hanging paper.

Uncle Qiang picked up the stack of earth paper, walked to the grave, and pressed it down with a stone. The wind lifted a corner of the paper; he added another small stone. The yellow paper was very conspicuous on the green grass, like a small flag.

"Why press the paper?"

"To tell others—this grave has someone tending it. A grave without paper is a grave no one tends. After a few years the grass grows thick, passersby think it's an unowned grave, and leave it alone." Uncle Qiang said, "With paper pressed on, others know—this family is still here."

That sentence stirred something in me. "This family is still here." Not "this person is still here," but "this family is still here." A grave with paper pressed on means the chain of this family has not broken. Someone remembered to come, someone remembered to bring food, someone remembered to clear the weeds. Even if the family is scattered across Chang'an, Humen, Shenzhen, Hunan—as long as someone comes every Qingming to press a sheet of paper, this grave is still "alive."

Xiao Yang stood before the grave, three sticks of incense in her hand. Uncle Qiang taught her how to bow—holding the incense with both hands, raising it to the space between the eyebrows, bending, three bows. She did as told, then stuck the incense into the earth. After the incense, she poured a few ai ban from the thermos onto a small dish before the grave.

The wind blew her hair across her face; she brushed it aside.

I stood a few steps away, didn't go closer. Sunlight leaked through the gaps in the leaves, falling on the yellow paper on the grave, on Xiao Yang's white sneakers, on Uncle Qiang's reddened eyes.

Xiao Yang came here today not just to "accompany Mom and Dad." She was using a steamer of ai ban she'd wrapped herself to establish a relationship with an old man she had never met. This relationship is not of blood, not of law; it is—"I wrapped the ban, put it before your grave, you eat it, and we're acquainted."

This is the deepest way for a new Dongguan person to integrate into this city. Not learning the dialect, not getting a household registration booklet. It is—on a Qingming, carrying ai ban, walking a stretch of muddy path, saying before a grave, "Please taste."

***

On the way back, Xiao Yang's sneakers were so dirty you couldn't tell their color. She looked down at them and suddenly said, "My mom says you can't buy new shoes at Qingming."

"Why?"

"Shoes is homophonous with 'evil.' Buying shoes at Qingming brings evil home."

"Then your shoes are dirty now—does that count as bringing evil back?"

She thought about it and laughed. "No. These are my own shoes. Evil can't get in."

Uncle Qiang walked ahead, heard us talking, glanced back. The empty basket in his hand, his steps a little lighter than when we came.

***

Back at Uncle Qiang's house, Xiao Yang went to wash her shoes. Uncle Qiang sat in the courtyard drinking tea; I sat across from him.

"Uncle, will your son bring his son to hang paper in the future?"

"Don't know. He's in Chang'an now; later he might go to Shenzhen. But today Xiao Yang came, Xiao Yang can wrap ai ban now. Enough."

"Enough what?"

"The thing is passed on. Not necessarily to the son. To Xiao Yang is fine too. She's also a Liu family member—she can wrap ai ban, she can hang paper, she knows where the grave is. She passes it to her children—no matter what surname—then the grave still has someone tending it."

I sat in the small courtyard, the sun on my back, the smell of mugwort still lingering in the air. I thought of Uncle Liu saying "I hung it, that's enough" when he lit the lamp. Now Uncle Qiang said "Xiao Yang can wrap ai ban now, enough."

Between these two "enough"s lies a change—from "I do it" to "someone can do it." This "someone" is not necessarily the son, not necessarily the same surname, not even necessarily a local. It is a girl from Hunan, who because of a steamer of ai ban walked into a grave hill, who because of a "please taste" walked into a family's memory.

This is what is happening in Dongguan. Not some big word like "cultural fusion." It is the Zhangmutou mud on a Hunan girl's white sneakers, the ai ban she wrapped laid before a Hakka old man's grave, the wind lifting a corner of the yellow paper as she bowed three times.

That yellow paper, every Qingming, is replaced with new. The old is blown away by the wind, falls into the mountain gullies, rots. But new is pressed on again. Year after year, yellow and green, layered together.

Like the people in this city—some come, some go. But there is always someone pressing a sheet of paper on a grave, always someone kneading a basin of glutinous rice dough in a kitchen, always someone hanging a lamp in an ancestral hall.

***

In the evening, Xiao Yang's mother came home from work. The smile on her face couldn't be contained, and the "eat more" never stopped. Xiao Yang's ears went red from the nagging; she kept her head down, putting food in her bowl.

After dinner, Xiao Yang and I took a taxi back to Nancheng. As the car left the village entrance, I looked back. Uncle Qiang was still standing at the courtyard gate, one hand raised, not lowered.

In my arms I still held a few ai ban.

Still warm.

Before the warmth of the ai ban had faded from my arms, at the end of March, the company arranged for me to deliver documents to Qingxi.

## Section Nineteen: The Qilin Dance

At the end of March, I went to Qingxi again. The company sent me to deliver some documents; I arrived early, and by the time the business was done it wasn't yet noon.

While chatting, I heard that Qingxi had a qilin team, and half its members were "new Dongguan people"—young people from other provinces working in Qingxi's electronics and toy factories, learning the qilin dance from a local master.

I was handling business at a sister company in Qingxi; the colleague there offered to go with me to find this qilin group.

The team was called the "Huang Family Troupe," but several in the group were young men from other provinces.

***

They were on the outskirts of Qingxi town, hills on both sides of the road, lychee groves in patches at the foot of the hills—not yet in bloom at this time, only deep green leaves. The village was neither new nor old, with remnants of Hakka walled houses and three-story buildings faced with ceramic tiles. Master Huang's home, which doubled as a "martial arts hall," was on the ground floor of one such building; the roller shutter was half pulled down, the light on inside.

We ducked in.

It was bigger than I'd imagined. About a hundred square meters, concrete floor, a qilin head propped against the wall—a frame of yellow bamboo, covered with gauze paper, a five-colored horn on the forehead, eyes of two copper mirrors, gold tracery at the corners of the mouth, peonies and peach blossoms painted on the cheeks. Beside the qilin head hung the qilin blanket—horizontal stripes of red, yellow, blue, white, and black, ending in ruyi cloud heads. Two more sets were spread on the floor, older, the gold thread on the blanket dulled.

Gongs, drums, cymbals leaned against the other wall. A big drum, its body painted red, a glued crack along the rim where it had split and been repaired.

In the middle of the room, four people were practicing.

A man in his fifties, wearing a gray front-buttoned jacket, trouser cuffs tucked into liberation shoes—this was Master Huang. He was tapping out the "single beat" with a drumstick for the other three. Of those three, two were in their early twenties, one not yet twenty. One was tall and thin, with a buzz cut and very white skin—clearly not a southerner; later I learned his surname was Bai, from Ningxia, working at a factory in Qingxi. One had a round face and was short, from Henan, doing quality inspection at a toy factory, called Xiao Zhou. The youngest was a local, in his second year of high school, Master Huang's nephew, called A Feng.

Master Huang finished a section, looked up and saw me, his stick stopping. "Who are you looking for?"

I said, "Hello, I heard this is where the qilin dance is. I've never seen it, so I came to take a look."

He looked us over twice, didn't shoo us out. Jerked his chin toward the Ningxia young man: "Xiao Bai, you explain to him."

Xiao Bai wiped off some sweat. By the end of March in Qingxi it was already hot; no air conditioning in the room, only an oscillating fan. He was still holding the bamboo frame of the qilin tail—a one-meter bamboo with a red cord tied to it, not a real qilin tail but a practice substitute. He said, "I can't explain it clearly. Just watch us practice and you'll know."

***

Master Huang raised his stick again. The beat changed, from "single beat" to "opening gong beat"—dong dongdong qi dong qi dong qi dong qi. Xiao Bai and A Feng bowed at the same time, one lifting the qilin head, one gripping the bamboo tail, ducking into the qilin blanket. Xiao Zhou picked up the cymbals, clang.

The qilin "came alive."

The Qingxi qilin dance routine is divided into the head set and the tail set. The head set is the qilin just descended from the heavenly palace: exploring the path, strolling in the garden, grooming, licking its feet, licking its tail, washing its face, dozing, waking. The tail set is "picking the green"—seeking the green, sniffing the green, startling at the green, testing the green, teasing the green, eating the green, spitting out the green. A full set takes over half an hour.

But these people were clearly still at the stage of "taking it apart to practice." Master Huang had them practice only the "exploring the path" and "licking the feet" of the head set.

"Exploring the path" requires the qilin head pressed very low, a T-step, feet hooked up, step by step probing forward. The Hakka say the qilin is a benevolent beast, afraid of crushing ants as it walks, so every step is careful. Xiao Bai lifted the qilin head, body sitting back, knees splayed outward, neck straining—the qilin head weighed four or five jin; held long, the shoulder blades tremble. A Feng, burrowed in the blanket behind, waist bent to ninety degrees, both hands supporting the horizontal bamboo of the blanket, retreating with Xiao Bai's backward steps. A stretch of cloth between them, neither could see the other; they relied entirely on the drumbeat and the touch of elbows to keep the rhythm.

"Stop." Master Huang tapped the drum rim. "Xiao Bai, your head lifted again. The qilin is shy of strangers; head held that high looks like a lion, not a qilin."

Xiao Bai, panting, took off the qilin head and set it on the ground. A red mark on his forehead where the bamboo frame of the head's inner wall had pressed.

"Master Huang, I keep feeling the T-step is twisted against the grain," Xiao Bai said.

"Twisted is right. You're tall and big; your legs are straight. Hakka walk mountain paths; legs are splayed. You have to forget those legs, grow them anew." Master Huang tapped the outside of his own thigh with the stick. "Here, push outward, toes on the ground, heels lifted. Not tiptoeing—'afraid'—afraid of stepping on something."

Xiao Zhou laughed from the side: "Brother Bai, don't be afraid, there are no ants on the floor."

Master Huang glared at him. Xiao Zhou shut up.

***

We stood by the wall watching. In the hot wind stirred by the fan there was a smell.

The qilin blanket is cloth; a sweating person burrowed inside for twenty minutes, the cloth absorbs the sweat, and there's a salty, fermented smell, like an old quilt sunned and then put away.

A thin layer of dust on the drumhead; shaken by the stick, a little floats up. A few longan leaves on the floor—"green" is usually longan leaves, stuffed into the qilin's mouth when picking the green, then spat out, stems still attached.

These things mixed together—not the smell of an "intangible heritage exhibition hall," but the smell of the living.

By noon, Master Huang called a stop. Everyone hung the qilin head back on the wall; Xiao Bai and Xiao Zhou went to wash their hands, A Feng went to pour water.

I followed Xiao Bai to the tap outside. He turned on the water, stuck his head under. The water was cold; he hissed.

"You're from Ningxia—how did you end up in Qingxi learning this?"

He straightened up, wiped his face with the back of his hand. "My dad works back home; after vocational high school I didn't want to study, so I came to Dongguan with some guys from my hometown. First in Chang'an, then Qingxi, making PCB boards, three shifts. One night after work, passing Shangguancang, I saw Master Huang and the others doing the qilin dance on the first day of the New Year. Back home we have shehuo too, dragon and lion dances, but that qilin was different. It wasn't fierce; it was careful; it poked its head around like a cat. Maybe it was curiosity."

He turned off the tap, leaned against the wall. "After that I found Master Huang, said I wanted to learn. He said outsiders can learn too, but first wash the qilin blankets for a month. I did. After that he had me practice the T-step; I practiced for two months, my legs swelled up once. Only then did he let me touch the qilin head."

Xiao Zhou came over with two cups of cool boiled water, handed one to Xiao Bai, one to me. "Brother, don't just listen to him. Let me tell you." He leaned against the wall, round face tilted up. "I'm from Henan. Been in Qingxi seven years. First served food in a canteen, then at the toy factory. Our factory has a Qingxi local guy; at New Year he invited us to his home for basin cuisine. After eating he said, 'You outsiders can come learn too, it doesn't cost anything.' So I came. I play the cymbals. Cymbals are simple, but the cymbals' 'breath points' are hard—when to draw in, when to release, has to match the drum. Master Huang says the cymbals are the qilin's breathing; you can't pant randomly."

He smiled. "My girlfriend is also from Henan, in Fenggang. Last time she came to watch me practice, she said, 'You sound like you're banging a pot.' I said what do you know, this is the qilin breathing."

I laughed too.

A Feng came out with an enamel mug, inside was pangdahai and chrysanthemum. He handed it to Master Huang. Master Huang took it, took a sip, and beckoned me: "Young man, come in and sit."

***

The light inside was dimmer. Master Huang dug a photograph out of a drawer and pushed it toward me.

The photo was taken last year on the fifteenth of the first month. At the entrance to Shangguancang village, the whole Huang Family Troupe—about a dozen people, wearing uniform black practice trousers, the two in front holding up the qilin head, the qilin blanket trailing on the ground, behind them gongs, drums, cymbals, suona, and behind that a crowd of children. In the photo Xiao Bai is lifting the qilin head, face flushed red, A Feng's half head showing from the blanket, Xiao Zhou holding the cymbals, mouth open as if shouting.

"This is the first-day-of-the-year village circuit. Starting from the Earth God shrine, entering every family's main hall. Did the outsiders go too?" I asked.

"They went. Xiao Bai lifted the head, Xiao Zhou played the cymbals, two others were on the night shift at the factory and couldn't come." Master Huang pointed at Xiao Bai's face in the photo with his finger. "He lifted it to the seventh house, sweat ran into his eyes, he couldn't even see the road through the qilin head, and he still insisted on finishing. When he came back I treated them to pig-trotter ginger stew."

"What did the villagers say?"

He paused. "In the past the qilin team only took people of the same clan from this village. Not anymore. The young people have all gone out to work; of the village's own young men willing to burrow into the qilin blanket, there aren't many. If you don't take outsiders, in ten years this qilin head will hang on the wall as decoration. If you take them, at least there's still someone to lift it."

"And the 'opening the light'? When a new qilin head is unsealed, going to the Earth God at midnight to pick the green—do the outsiders go?"

"They go. Xiao Bai went. He doesn't know who the Earth God is, I'll tell you. Does he kneel? He doesn't kneel. But he stood before the Earth God and bowed three times with me. I said believe it or not is up to you, but this qilin head is Qingxi's; if you lift it you have to acknowledge this way of doing things. He nodded."

***

What dripped from Xiao Bai lifting the qilin head was sweat. But that sweat was also Qingxi's sweat, wasn't it.

The fan blades turned overhead. Xiao Bai and Xiao Zhou were scrolling on their phones in the corner, A Feng doing homework. The qilin head on the wall in the dim yellow light, the copper-mirror eyes reflecting a bit of light, as if really watching everything in this room.

I suddenly felt that the identity of "new Dongguan person" in Dongguan's folk customs perhaps cannot be dismissed with the three words "outsider." Xiao Bai lifted the qilin head until sweat ran into his eyes—was that sweat Qingxi's sweat? Yes, and no. It was his sweat, falling on Qingxi's land, absorbed by the qilin blanket, becoming a layer of memory in the cloth. Next year on the fifteenth, when he lifts this head again, the head is still the head passed down by the Huang Family Troupe, but inside it there's an extra layer of him.

The true meaning of "new Dongguan person" is that you stood before the Earth God with your master at some midnight, that you lifted the qilin door to door on some first month, that your legs swelled up practicing on some June concrete floor. You shed sweat, the sweat was taken in by this place, and you grew into it.

***

When I left, Master Huang saw me to the roller shutter.

The taxi I'd called by phone was waiting at the village entrance. As the car pulled away, I looked back at that small building. The roller shutter half pulled down, the light still on, faint drumbeats drifting out—dong dongdong qi dong qi dong. Not the formal opening gong, but Xiao Bai and the others practicing the single beat.

The Qingxi hills turned deep blue in the dusk. The lychee groves row upon row, like someone had laid a dark green carpet on the slope.

Perhaps Dongguan's folk customs are no longer only the locals'. They're like the canal water—the source is local, but along the way countless tributaries flow in. The water in those tributaries is from Hunan, Henan, Ningxia. They flow in, the water still flows east to the Dong River, but the taste of the water has changed, grown thicker.

Xiao Bai had been in Qingxi six years. He might never learn to speak Qingxi dialect in his life, might never worship the Earth God. But he had lifted the qilin head, sweat had run into his eyes, and a ninety-year-old woman had said "the qilin recognizes him." That was enough.

Just like me. I'd washed dongye and bamboo leaves, wrapped crooked zongzi, received dried vegetables from Chen Ah Po, looked up at the lamps in the Liu ancestral hall, listened to the old woman in Shatian lament "Mother, mother." I hadn't become a Dongguan person. But Dongguan had become part of me.

The taxi reached the Qingxi bus station. I got out; the wind came from the mountain pass, carrying the smell of lychee leaves. In the distance there were gong and drum sounds—some village's qilin team practicing at night.

I stood at the station entrance and suddenly very much wanted to send Xiao Bai a message—but I didn't have his number. Never mind then. Next year on the first month, if he was still in Qingxi, he'd probably still be lifting that qilin head, starting from the Earth God shrine, entering house by house. If I was still in Dongguan, I'd go see him. See the sweat run into his eyes, the qilin head sway slightly.

The drumbeats of Qingxi still sounded on the other side of the hills. May arrived. Around the Dragon Boat Festival, my father had a stomach bleed; I brought him from Zhanjiang to Dongguan.

## Section Twenty: Jidi Congee

May. Around the Dragon Boat Festival, my father had a stomach bleed; I brought him from Zhanjiang to Dongguan to stay for a while—to stay. I discussed it with Chen Ah Po and rented the empty room next door for him temporarily.

At the gastroenterology department of Nancheng People's Hospital, I registered him and took him for a gastroscopy. I sat in the congee-noodle shop across from the hospital waiting, a bowl of jidi congee before me. The congee had just been served, steaming hot. I was about to drink when an old man beside me suddenly slapped his chopsticks onto the table with a "pa."

"This congee is wrong."

His voice wasn't loud, but it was like a pebble dropped into a bowl.

He was about sixty, very thin, wearing a blue work vest washed pale, a gray shirt over it, unbuttoned. The jidi congee before him was almost untouched.

I glanced at him. He turned his head; his eyes were very bright, like two freshly polished brass buttons.

"The pork liver is cut too thick. The pork intestine isn't slit open. The pork kidney isn't cleaned of sinew properly. What kind of jidi congee is this?"

I didn't respond. My bowl was also mediocre—the liver indeed a bit old, the intestine unchewable.

"You're an outsider, aren't you?"

"Mm."

"I knew it. A local knows it's wrong with one sip."

I put down my spoon. "Were you a congee shop owner before?"

He pushed his bowl aside, pulled a pack of Hongshuangxi from his shirt pocket, took one out, didn't light it, twirled it between his fingers.

"Ran one for over twenty years. Pork liver must be sliced thin, not thick. Pork intestine must be slit open and washed, rubbed with cornstarch three times. Pork kidney halved, the white sinew must be picked clean—if the white sinew isn't removed, the congee is fishy. The congee base must be simmered over open flame, the rice must be Zengcheng Simiao rice, rice added after the water boils, high heat to a boil then turned to low, simmered until the rice blooms and the congee forms a gel—this is called 'water and rice in harmony.' Those shops now, one pressure cooker, three pots in ten minutes, and they call it jidi congee?"

The cigarette between his fingers had been pinched a little out of shape.

"So why did you stop doing it?"

"My son came back from university and went into IT, said I got up too early—up at three in the morning to simmer congee, he said it disturbed the neighbors. I said disturb your head, you grew up drinking this congee. Later the shop lease expired, the landlord raised the rent, I did the math, it wasn't worth it. Closed."

He was silent for a moment.

"After closing, I woke at six every morning. Couldn't sleep once awake, lay in bed till seven, got up, went downstairs to eat a bowl of congee. None of them as good as mine."

My father came out of the gastroscopy room. I helped him sit down, ordered a bowl of plain congee—his stomach was still recovering, he couldn't eat jidi congee. I turned to say a few more words to the old man, but he had already stood up and left. The bowl of congee on the table untouched, the chopsticks still pressed against the rim.

Blue work vest, gray shirt, steps not fast, but steady.

***

Only later did I learn his surname was He. He lived nearby, in the old family compound of Nancheng Hospital—built in the nineties, mostly retired doctors and their families.

The second time I saw him was on the second floor of a congee shop at the pedestrian street entrance. He sat at the innermost seat, a bowl of congee before him, eating. I carried my bowl over.

"Uncle He?"

He looked up. "You know me?"

"That time at the hospital entrance."

"Oh." He lowered his head and went on eating.

I sat down. That day's pork liver looked thinner than last time. I asked, "Is this one better than the last?"

"A little better. The liver is thinner. But the intestine still isn't slit open."

After eating, he pushed the bowl away, stood up and left. Before leaving he glanced at me. "Yours—the liver is too thick."

I looked down at my bowl—about five millimeters.

The third time, a week later, at the wet market. He stood before the meat stall, telling the woman selling meat, "This piece won't do, too old. I want the piece next to the tenderloin, tender."

The woman rolled her eyes. "Uncle He, you know pigs better than I do?"

"I ran a congee shop over twenty years. If I don't know pigs, who does?"

She laughed and cut the piece next to the tenderloin and handed it to him. He took it, pressed it with his finger, nodded. "This one will do."

I stood nearby watching. When he chose pork liver he didn't look at the color—he touched it, pressing with his finger, judging tenderness by elasticity. Later he said something to me that I remember to this day:

"Pork liver is like a person. They all look about the same. But you press it once, and you know which one is alive and which is dead."

***

We gradually became familiar. He wasn't teaching me to make congee—he seemed to assume I wouldn't learn—but chatting about "the old days."

He said when he was young he worked at the Dongguan County Food Factory, quit in the nineties to open a congee shop. "Back then Nancheng was still a town, Hongfu Road didn't exist yet. My shop was where Yinfeng Road is now, ten square meters, two tables. Sold congee in the morning, noodles at noon, rice in the evening. Jidi congee was the signature; many people rode bicycles from Wanjiang to eat it."

"Why is it called jidi congee?"

"Legend has it in the Qing dynasty a scholar ate this bowl of congee every day before the imperial exam. Inside were pork liver, pork intestine, pork kidney—'liver' sounds like 'official,' 'intestine' sounds like 'long,' 'kidney' sounds like 'want.' Put together it's 'officials want it long'—all officials want it. The scholar really did pass the provincial exam. So it's called jidi congee."

He paused. "I didn't make congee for that. I made it to be delicious."

"What's the standard for delicious?"

"Three words: smooth, tender, fresh. Pork liver must be smooth, pork intestine tender, pork kidney fresh. Not one can be missing. Achieve these three words, and the plating, the name, the story, none of it matters."

I asked, "Your son really doesn't eat your congee anymore?"

He was silent for a long time.

"When he was little, every morning at six-thirty I'd carry a bowl of jidi congee to his bedside. He'd drink it with his eyes closed, then turn over and go back to sleep. Later he went to university, to the provincial capital, then came back to work in Dongguan. Now he eats bread and milk for breakfast. He says, 'Dad, yours is too oily.'"

"Are you angry?"

"Not angry. He grew up. Growing up is not eating the same breakfast as you."

He said this very lightly. But "not eating the same breakfast as you" is more accurate than any explanation of a "generation gap."

***

My father stayed in Dongguan two weeks, then went back to Zhanjiang. Before he left I took him to eat at the congee shop Uncle He had mentioned—not Uncle He's own, but an old shop on Yinfeng Road, the cook said to be a former apprentice of Uncle He's. My father took a sip and said, "The liver's all right. The intestine is so-so."

After Uncle He learned my father had gone, one morning at the congee shop he said to me, "Your father knows food."

"How do you know?"

"Last time he sat there, at the first sip, his brow furrowed. That was the taste of intestine not washed clean. He didn't say it, but he furrowed. People who know food, know at the first sip."

I felt there was a connection between Uncle He and my father that didn't need words. Two people with near-merciless demands on a bowl of congee. They hadn't even formally exchanged a word—Uncle He wasn't in the shop the day my father left—but Uncle He recognized a kindred spirit from that one furrow of the brow.

This is what fascinates me most about Dongguan. Not the big folk customs—dragon boats, qilin, flower lanterns—but the "small obsessions" scattered in every corner of the city. An old man's obsession with a bowl of jidi congee runs deeper than many "cultural inheritors" I've met.

***

Later, the site of Uncle He's old congee shop was demolished. Not the shop—that had closed years ago—but the building. Yinfeng Road was redeveloped; that row of old houses all torn down. I went to look once. A "urban renewal" slogan on the construction hoarding, inside only a few concrete pillars left standing.

I stood outside the hoarding, thinking of what Uncle He had said. Up at three in the morning to simmer congee. Rice must be Zengcheng Simiao. Pork liver three millimeters. Intestine slit open and washed three times. Kidney sinew removed.

These things won't appear on any "intangible heritage" list. Jidi congee doesn't count as "cultural heritage." But it's a thing one person did in Dongguan for over twenty years. Over twenty years, up at three every morning, simmering congee, cutting pork liver, slitting pork intestine, removing kidney sinew. After these motions are repeated countless times, they become part of his body. Like Master Mo's fingers threading among the meat cubes when mixing cured meat, like the evenness of force in Aunt Feng's rattan weaving, like the trembling of Xiao Bai's shoulder blades when he lifts the qilin head.

Uncle He later said to me, "You ask why I still go drink that wrong bowl of jidi congee every day. Because if I don't go, I'll forget my former self."

Not "forget my craft," but "forget myself." A man who made jidi congee for over twenty years, if he doesn't go drink a bowl of congee, is no longer "that congee maker," but has become a "retired old man." He doesn't accept this.

So he goes every day. Even if the bowl is wrong, even if the liver is too thick, the intestine not slit, the kidney fishy. He goes because that motion—sitting down, picking up the spoon, taking the first sip—reminds him: you are still that man who got up at three in the morning to simmer congee.

This is the same as Uncle Liu in Zhangmutou hanging the lamp. Uncle Liu hung the lamp not to "transmit culture," but to confirm "I am a member of this family." Uncle He drinks congee not to "taste fine food," but to confirm "I am still that congee maker."

Confirm. These two words are the deepest logic of all Dongguan folk customs. Not performance, not display, not for others to see. It's confirming with yourself—I am still here. I am still me.

***

Last winter, Uncle He didn't come to the congee shop.

I went to that old family compound to look for him. The neighbor said he was hospitalized, pneumonia. Old age, poor resistance.

I found him in the hospital corridor. He sat on the bed, hooked to an IV, his complexion poor, but his eyes still bright.

"Uncle He."

"Mm."

"Want some jidi congee?"

"Want."

I ran out and found a congee shop nearby—not the old shop, a new one—and bought a bowl back. Opened the lid, steam rising.

He took it, picked up the spoon, took a sip.

"The liver is too thick," he said.

Another sip.

"The intestine still isn't slit."

Another sip.

"But the congee base is all right. The rice has bloomed."

He finished the whole bowl. At the bottom remained a slice of liver, a section of intestine. He set down the bowl and leaned back against the headboard.

After that, I never heard him complain again.

Not that he accepted "wrong." It's that he no longer needed to complain. He drank that bowl of congee not to evaluate whether it was good, but to—sit down, pick up the spoon, drink. This motion itself was his "jidi." Not the imperial exam's jidi, but the jidi of being alive.

***

Sometimes I think, if one day I leave Dongguan, what will I miss most?

Not the dragon boat drums, not the flower lantern frames, not the qilin dance steps.

It's a bowl of wrong jidi congee. An old man sitting against the wall, slapping his chopsticks on the table, saying "this congee is wrong." And then still finishing it.

Uncle He finished that bowl of wrong jidi congee. May passed, June came.

## Section Twenty-One: The Sunken Dragon

It was about June.

A colleague and I went fishing by the Wanjiang River. Sat there half a day, the float never moved—the fish here seemed not to favor me.

I reeled in my rod, set it aside, said a word to him, and went up the bank.

Not far off was a sizable tin shed, the door half closed. Not a roller shutter, but two iron doors opening outward, rusted red, through the gap you could see half-finished work and old dragon boats.

I knocked. From inside came a "Who is it?"

The door opened a crack. A head poked out. Sixty or so, bald, a scar on his forehead running from above the left brow bone to the hairline. Face tanned very dark, but with red showing through the black—not the red of drink, but the red of years of sun soaking through, capillaries dilated.

"Boss, hello, may I look at the dragon boats you make?"

"Look at dragon boats? No dragon boats to show you now. The dragon boats are all at the bottom of the canal. Have to wait till next April."

"I know. I just want to see the workshop—how you repair, how you build them normally."

He looked me over for a few seconds.

"What do you do?"

"Just want to see how you repair, how you build. I won't disturb your work, all right?"

Then the door opened fully.

"Come in."

***

The workshop was bigger than I'd imagined. About two hundred square meters, high ceiling, tin roof, leaking in a few places, plastic basins on the floor catching water. Lumber stacked against the wall, dark brown, heavy as anything. Later I tried to lift a smaller piece and nearly threw my back out.

A young man called him "Uncle Bing." He'd been making dragon boats twenty or thirty years. His father did, his grandfather did.

"You just asked how we repair. Look over there—" He pointed to a corner. On the ground lay the wreck of an old dragon boat—not the whole boat, a section of the hull, the planks already rotted, you could crumble them with a pinch. "This. Last year during the boat parade it hit a rock, the bottom split. They hauled it over for me to fix. I said it can't be fixed, the planks have to be replaced. Replacing the planks means taking it apart. Taken apart, it's not as good as building a new one."

"So did they replace it?"

"They replaced it. Built a new one. The planks from this old boat, the usable ones I kept, the rest burned."

He walked over to the old hull, squatted, ran his hand along the wood grain. His fingers rubbed back and forth in the cracks of the wood. That motion reminded me of Uncle He choosing pork liver, pressing the meat—not looking, but touching. Wood doesn't speak, but if you touch it enough, you can feel where it's still "alive" and where it's already "dead."

"Uncle Bing, how long does it take you to build a dragon boat?"

"Depends how many people. Me alone, two months. With an apprentice helping, forty days."

"Do you still have apprentices?"

He stood up, patted the sawdust off his hands. "That one. The Hunan kid. Called Xiao Duan. Lives in the back."

He pointed toward the rear of the workshop. A tin shed had been put up back there, functioning as a dormitory.

Xiao Duan came out. Twenty-four or twenty-five, thin, wearing black-framed glasses and an old T-shirt printed with "Hongxing Erke." He and Uncle Bing were nothing alike—Uncle Bing the kind of solid who could kill a bull with one punch, Xiao Duan the kind of thin a gust of wind could blow away.

"Xiao Duan is my daughter-in-law's cousin," Uncle Bing said. "He's been in Dongguan three years, first at an electronics factory in Liaobu. Last year he came to me and said he wanted to learn to build dragon boats. He said he can draw. I said building dragon boats doesn't need drawing. He said he wanted to try."

"How's he learning?"

Uncle Bing gave an "hmph." "Clumsy. But he'll take the hardship."

Xiao Duan scratched his head beside him, didn't argue.

***

I stayed at the workshop all afternoon. Uncle Bing worked, I watched.

He was repairing an old boat—the hull had split, he needed to fit a strip of wood into it to reinforce it. First he used a chisel to trim both sides of the crack even, measured the size, hammered a glued wooden strip into the crack. Every hammer blow very light, but the position extremely precise. After the strip was fitted in, the seam was almost invisible.

"Uncle Bing, is it just the two of you here?"

His hand paused. Then he went on hammering.

"My son drives a taxi in the Dongguan city area. He says building dragon boats doesn't make money, after costs it's less than what he makes driving a taxi."

"Are you angry?"

"Angry about what? He grew up, made his own choice."

That sentence again. I'd heard it from Uncle He—"He grew up. Growing up is not eating the same breakfast as you." From Uncle Liu—"I hung it, that's enough." Now Uncle Bing said "He grew up, made his own choice." Dongguan's fathers all seem to have a capacity—to press disappointment into a very flat sentence.

But Uncle Bing was different from Uncle He. Uncle He's "not angry" was truly acceptance. Uncle Bing's "not angry" had underneath it something deeper, something he didn't want anyone to see.

That evening, Xiao Duan went to buy food—portions for two. Uncle Bing pulled a pack of cigarettes from his trouser pocket, offered me one. I waved it off, don't smoke. He lit one himself, drew deeply.

"When he was little I brought him here. After school he'd come to the workshop to do his homework. I'd be planing wood, he'd be writing characters."

He threw the cigarette butt on the ground, crushed it with his foot.

***

Uncle Bing took me to see the canal where the "hidden dragons" lie. Just a narrow waterway in the village, not deep, but muddy at the bottom. Every year after the dragon boat race, the dragon boats are sunk into the mud of this canal—not thrown in at random, but covered with mud, only a bit of the bow showing. This way the wood doesn't dry and crack, doesn't get eaten by insects. The next year around Qingming they "raise the dragon"—dig the boat out of the mud, wash it clean, apply tung oil, test the water.

"On the day of raising the dragon, the whole village comes. The drum sounds, the mud is dug, the boat floats—that scene, once you see it you never forget it." Uncle Bing said, "But my son never saw it. The year he left, he didn't come for the raising. He said he had to study. After that he never came back."

He stood by the canal, nothing on the water surface—the boats still sleeping under the mud. June sunlight fell on the water, fragmented. His shadow cast in the water, twisted crooked by the ripples.

Xiao Duan came back with two boxed meals, one in each hand, handed one to Uncle Bing. Uncle Bing took it, didn't open it, first asked: "What did you buy?"

"Green pepper shredded pork, and a steamed egg."

"Mm." Uncle Bing opened the box, squatted by the pile of wood shavings and ate.

Xiao Duan stood behind Uncle Bing, as if ready at any moment to pick up the conversation, or as if accustomed to not interrupting.

As Uncle Bing ate, he suddenly said, "That new boat for Upper Village, when are they coming to get it?"

"Next month, I think," Xiao Duan said. "They said they want dragon scales painted. I said I can't. They said find that Hunan kid. I said what he paints is cartoon—"

Uncle Bing cut him off: "He said he paints traditional dragon scales. One by one, not cartoon. Isn't that right?"

Xiao Duan nodded. "Yes. One overlapping the next, like fish scales. I looked up pictures online—"

"Online?" Uncle Bing glanced at him.

"...I found books to look at." Xiao Duan corrected himself. "Photocopied from the library."

Uncle Bing didn't ask further. He shoveled in a mouthful of rice, chewed very slowly, as if chewing on that word—cartoon, internet, photocopy, library—these things were too far from his boat, so far he couldn't be bothered to understand.

Xiao Duan said no more.

***

I think the dragon boat, in the relationship between Uncle Bing and Xiao Duan, and in the relationship between Uncle Bing and his son, has a subtle mirroring.

Uncle Bing's son thought building dragon boats "doesn't make money," and ran. Xiao Duan came, with the confidence of "I can draw," but Uncle Bing told him—your way won't do, mine will. Xiao Duan didn't run. He stayed.

This is a connection harder than blood. Uncle Bing's son had blood, but not this connection. Xiao Duan had no blood, but had it.

I thought of the old woman in the hall silently moving her lips. Of the "good" Uncle Liu said when hanging the lamp. Of Uncle He pushing the bowl away after finishing a wrong bowl of jidi congee. Of Xiao Yang laying a dish of her own ai ban before the grave.

Uncle Bing stood by the canal, doing nothing. But the way he stood—back slightly stooped, hands in his trouser pockets, the scar on his forehead glinting white in the sun—was itself a kind of "being there."

When I left, Xiao Duan saw me to the workshop door. Uncle Bing didn't come out—he was inside planing wood.

At the village entrance I looked back. The iron door was still half closed. A thread of light showed under the gap—Uncle Bing inside.

The astringency of wood, the sting of tung oil, the sweetness of wood shavings, the salt of sweat—mixed together, leaking from the gap in the door, drifting to the canal at the village entrance, drifting to the dragon boats sunk in the mud.

The dragon boats wait in the mud for a year. They remember this smell. They remember it every year.

## Section Twenty-Two: The Qixi Offering Tables

***

The dragon boats were waiting in the mud for their drums, and the people in the city had already looked up at the sky. On an afternoon in July, a thin dampness hung heavy in the air—as if something were about to fall, yet reluctant to fall.

I was downstairs buying water. Xiao Yang suddenly said, "Tomorrow is Qixi."

I was taken aback. "Oh."

"There are offering tables in Wangniudun. Have you been?"

"No."

"When I was little my mom took me. The whole table was full of things carved from melons and fruit. Very pretty."

I smiled and said, "Will you take me to see?"

She nodded, didn't say much.

The next morning we got there and discovered—it seemed to be the first Folk Qiqiao Skills Competition. A red paper was pasted at the entrance to the hall, written in brush: "Wangniudun Folk Qiqiao Skills Competition." The characters weren't especially fine, but pasted right in the middle of the doorframe, very formal.

I stood at the entrance of the hall, the sun on the back of my neck. Thought for a moment. Then changed into a gray T-shirt and followed Xiao Yang inside.

***

Quite a few people inside. A smell arrived first—the raw rice fishiness of paste, the green bitterness of winter melon pulp, mixed together. Not sharp, but thick. Like the smell of that piece of radish at the bottom of Chen Ah Po's basin cuisine—the first sniff you think nothing of it, the second sniff and it won't go away.

A dozen or so long tables were set up inside. Each table was piled with things—not gold or silver, but melons and fruit, grains of rice, beans, paper flowers. On the most conspicuous table, a winter melon had been hollowed out, a pagoda carved into its outer skin—eaves, windows, spire, layer upon layer. A dish of mung beans had been piled into the shape of a magpie bridge.

A woman stood behind that table. In her fifties, short hair, wearing a faded floral short-sleeved blouse, an apron tied at her waist—the sewing kind, with several pockets in front, filled with scissors, tweezers, toothpicks. She was gluing grains of rice onto a rockery carved from pomelo peel. The grains were tiny; she picked them up with tweezers, dipped them in a bit of paste, pressed them on. Each grain pressed, pause one second, then pick up the next.

Her fingers were very steady. But her nails were cut very short, and on the sides of her index and middle fingers was a layer of yellowed hard callus, worn from holding tweezers—thin, but shiny.

I stood beside her watching. She didn't look up. After gluing the last grain, she stepped back, tilted her head and looked for a moment. Then picked up a pair of scissors and snipped off a stray thread of paste hanging below the rockery.

"Is it pretty?" she suddenly asked.

"Pretty," I said, only then realizing she wasn't asking us.

Only then did she turn to look at me. Her eyes weren't big, the corners drooping a little; when she looked at people it was like sizing them up—not sizing up you as a person, but sizing up whether this piece of clothing could be altered.

"Are you reporters?"

"No."

"Then what are you here for?"

"To look."

She gave an "oh." Turned back, began packing up the tools on the table. Scissors into the apron pocket, tweezers into another pocket, toothpicks back into the plastic box. Her movements were brisk, just like when she'd been gluing rice grains—every motion had its place.

"How long have you been doing this?" I asked.

She didn't stop. "This? Or something else?"

"The offering tables."

"A long time."

She put the last thing away, took off the apron, draped it over the back of the chair. Then she pulled a cardboard box out from under the table and began loading things into it—several bottles of paste, a pack of toothpicks, an old toothbrush, a few sponges. An arrow was drawn on the side of the box in marker, pointing down.

She lifted the box. Not heavy, but bulky. I reached out to steady the bottom.

"Thanks."

"Can we come watch how you make them?"

She glanced at me, then at Xiao Yang.

"If you want to watch, watch."

***

Walking out of the hall, the sun was fierce. She walked ahead, the box blocking her face; all I could see was the back of her floral blouse, soaked in one patch. The sweat stain was shaped like a leaf.

"What's your surname?"

"Ah Ying."

Just two words. Didn't say the surname.

Her home was in an old alley in Wangniudun town. Not a tourist-district old alley—a real old alley where people actually lived. The alley was narrow; two people walking side by side had to turn sideways. The ground was concrete, cracked in places, grass growing in the seams. The ground-floor doors of the houses on both sides were closed, the paint peeling in patches.

She stopped at an iron gate. No paint, just welded iron pipes, rusted. She took out keys and opened it, pushed the box in first, then squeezed in sideways herself.

I followed. Inside was cooler than outside. The living room wasn't big: an old sofa, a television, a very old New Year print on the wall—a chubby baby holding a carp. Across from the sofa was a folding table, piled with things: half a winter melon, a few pieces of pomelo peel, a handful of mung beans, a handful of red beans, a few toothpicks, a bottle of paste, a pair of tweezers. A small desk lamp sat at the corner of the table, its plastic shade yellowed.

"Sit." She pointed to the sofa. Clothes were piled on it; she pushed them aside, revealing a place to sit. I sat down. The springs had collapsed; my bottom sank.

She set the box on the floor, pulled her phone from her apron pocket, checked the time. "Four-thirty. Want water?"

"No thanks."

"You do this every year?"

"Done it twenty or thirty years."

She didn't say more. The room was very quiet. The lamplight fell on the mung beans and red beans; the surface of the beans had a thin sheen, like a road just after rain.

After a while, the door to the inner room opened. A man came out. Sixty or so, thin, wearing a gray vest, his ribs visible one by one. He didn't look at me, walked to the water dispenser and poured a cup, stood there drinking. After drinking, he glanced at me.

"Who are you?"

"Here to see the offering tables."

He gave an "oh." Carried the cup back into the inner room.

Ah Ying didn't look up. Her hand stirred the mung beans on the table.

"My husband," she said.

Just three words. Not an explanation, a statement. Like saying "this is a table," "that is a chair."

She picked up a mung bean, looked at it in her palm, put it back.

"Did you learn this yourself?"

"My grandmother. She used to do it in the village. Every year half a month before Qixi, she'd carry the family dinner table out to the courtyard and set it up. I squatted beside her watching. She wouldn't let me touch. Said 'your hands are clumsy, don't ruin it.' So I watched. Watched for many years."

She pulled off the last strand of pith from the orange peel, laid it beside the small pile of pith. White threads, almost invisible under the lamp.

Then she stood up, threw the orange peel in the trash. Walked back to the folding table, looked once more at that winter melon pagoda.

***

Toward evening, we returned to the hall.

The offering tables were all set up now. A dozen tables, each lit. The small bulbs inside the winter melon pagoda had been changed; light came through the carved gaps in the tower's body, square by square, like a castle that glowed. The mung beans on the magpie bridge were packed tight, one against another, no gaps between. The sesame seeds of the heavenly river were spread very evenly, like a real river.

Ah Ying stood beside her table. She had changed her floral blouse—still floral, but a darker color. Her hair was combed, pinned behind her ears with black hair clips. She looked at the winter melon pagoda, and there was light in her eyes. Not the light of the pagoda. Her own light.

There were some people in the hall. Old people from the town, and outsiders like us, a few young people holding cameras, probably reporters.

Ah Ying stood up. Patted the dust off her knees—there wasn't any dust, she just made the motion.

She reached out and touched the tip of the spire with her finger. Very lightly. Like touching a baby who had just fallen asleep.

We looked around the hall a while longer. Those lights, those beans, those paper flowers, those hollowed winter melons and pomelo peels—they weren't anything remarkable. But they had been glued grain by grain with tweezers on folding tables. They had existed. Once a year at Qixi they existed. After Qixi they were dismantled. Next year, made again.

Walking out of the hall, the sky was completely dark. Wangniudun's night wasn't very dark. Streetlights in the distance, car lights, light leaking from the windows of the houses.

Xiao Yang said, "Pretty, isn't it?"

I said, "Very exquisite."

"When I was little and came with my mom, it was bigger than this. The whole eight-immortals table was covered. Now..." She didn't finish.

It was almost ten when we got back to the rented room.

The crack in the ceiling was invisible in the dark. But I knew it was there.

The offering tables were dismantled, and Qixi's bit of exquisite care was put away. All autumn, the days flowed like the East River—silently, but always flowing. By the time I looked up again, the sky had gone gray, and the wind blew from the river, damp. Before the winter solstice, I went to Qishi.

## Section Twenty-Three: Winter Balls

Before the winter solstice, work took me to Qishi.

By the time the work was done it was almost noon. After lunch, I strolled along the East River.

The sky was gray—not the gray of coming rain, but the gray of winter pressing everything down a notch. The lychee leaves were still green, but a dark green, as if draped with a cloth. The wind blew from the river, damp; no matter how thick your clothes, it seeped in.

On a village road by the East River I smelled something.

Not fragrant—cooked. The sweetness of glutinous rice mixed with the aroma of peanut and sesame, with a bit of astringency—banana leaf. When winter balls are steamed, banana leaves are laid underneath; steamed hot, the banana leaf gives off this smell: not sharp, but thick, as if someone had stirred the words "New Year is coming" directly into the steam.

I stopped and sniffed. The wind passed, the smell thinned; the wind stopped, the smell thickened again. The wind by the East River has a direction; I followed it and turned north.

Turned into an alley, walked about twenty meters, a courtyard on the right. Iron gate, green paint, badly peeling, revealing the rust beneath. The iron gate was ajar; on it was pasted a sheet of A3 paper, with two crooked characters drawn in colored markers—"Winter Balls." I pushed the door open.

The hinge creaked—not loud, but in the cold air it carried far.

The courtyard wasn't big, concrete floor, swept very clean, several bundles of firewood stacked against the corner, an axe leaning beside the woodpile. In the middle stood a large iron pot, big—must have been eighty or ninety centimeters across—under it a brick stove, red brick, laid very neatly, you could tell an expert had built it. Lychee wood burned in the stove. Lychee wood gives off a sweet smell when it burns, mixed into the steam. The fire wasn't big, but the steam from the pot kept rising, white, condensing into puffs in the cold air, like someone blowing smoke rings at the sky.

"Ka." A sound came from the east side of the courtyard. Very crisp, like biting through a stick of sugarcane. Two seconds later, another.

A woman stood before the pot. In her forties, round face, sweat on her face—not from heat, but steamed by the moisture. A layer of fine beads on her forehead, her temples wet, stuck to her face. She wore a dark red down jacket, the zipper only half up, a gray sweatshirt underneath, sleeves pushed to the elbows, flour on her arms.

"Buying winter balls?"

"Just looking."

"Look all you like."

She turned and grabbed a handful of glutinous rice flour from a basin beside her, added a little water, began kneading. The water was added very precisely, just that little bit—one drop more and it would be too thin. Her movements were quick, both hands turning the dough over and over, pressing down, folding over, turning direction, pressing down again. The dough went from loose to formed, from white to translucent, like a ball of mist gathering in her hands.

Winter balls. I'd heard of them the first year I came to Dongguan—many places in Dongguan eat winter balls at the winter solstice. Not tangyuan, but balls made of glutinous rice flour, filled with peanut-sesame-sugar, rolled in something on the outside.

"Is this coconut?" I asked.

"No. Coconut mixed with a bit of roasted glutinous rice flour. Pure coconut is too expensive; mix a little in, tastes about the same."

She set the kneaded dough on the work board, pinched off small portions, each about the size of a ping-pong ball. Picked one up, pressed a hollow in the middle with her thumb, pressed down, turned a circle, pressed the hollow deep, pressed the wall thin. Then a spoonful of filling—crushed peanut, sesame, white sugar, the three mixed in an enamel bowl, scooped a spoonful, pressed firm, filled it in. Pinched closed, gathered the seam with the web of the hand, pinched off the extra bit. Rolled round, palms together, turned a few times. Then rolled it in the plate of "coconut flour." One winter ball done, surface white, as if coated in frost.

Her hands were fast. From pinching the portion to setting it on the plate, I counted—about ten seconds.

"How long have you been doing this?"

"This? Been doing—" She thought for a moment. "Sixteen years."

"Before that?"

"Before that I worked at a toy factory in Shilong, making dolls, eight years."

"Making dolls?"

"Those little plastic figures. I was on the assembly line attaching arms, three thousand a day." She raised her hand and looked at it. "Later the factory moved to Vietnam, so I came back and learned to make winter balls from my mother. My mother did it thirty years."

She took off her gloves; I saw her hands. Fingers not long, knuckles a little thick, especially the pad between thumb and index finger, bulging out. Several cracks on the back of her hand, split by winter, some scabbed, some still open.

"Where's your mother?"

"At home. She's seventy-eight this year. Hands still work, but her back doesn't, hurts if she stands too long. So this year I do most of it, she helps behind, roasting the peanuts."

She pointed toward the house. Through the glass door I could see an old woman sitting behind the stove, turning peanuts in an iron wok. The glass door was the old sliding kind, aluminum frame, a faded "Fortune" character pasted on the glass. The old woman wore a navy padded jacket, an apron washed pale over it, sitting on a low stool, a spatula in her hand. The fire was small; the peanuts rustled in the wok. The old woman moved very slowly, pausing after each turn. Not hesitation—waiting. Waiting for what was under this turn to come up, then turning again. Every turn reached everything; not one was wasted.

"You do this every year?"

"Mm. Start end of November, work till the day before the winter solstice, about ten days, several hundred a day."

"Sell well?"

"So-so. Give them away too. Every year I send a box to the old folks' home in the next village, thirty. Their teeth aren't good, so I make theirs with thinner skin, less sugar in the filling, they can manage."

"Is that your husband?"

I looked toward the back of the courtyard. A man squatted by the woodpile splitting wood, in his fifties, wearing an old khaki jacket, cuffs worn shiny. His legs spread, a thick block of wood before him, axe in hand. The axe rose, fell; each split, the wood went "ka," a crisp sound, like biting through sugarcane. He stacked the split wood beside him, one layer crosswise, one lengthwise, stacked like a wall.

"Splitting wood, tending the fire, moving things. He doesn't touch the dough—says his hands are clumsy, what he makes looks like rocks."

"What did he do before?"

"Worked at the same factory as my mom in Shilong." She said, "Same toy factory. I was on the assembly line attaching arms, he was in the injection molding workshop running machines. That's where we met. After the factory moved, he went to a logistics company driving trucks, ten years. Long haul, Dongguan to Hunan, Dongguan to Jiangxi, sometimes to Jiangsu."

"And now?"

"Quit last year. Herniated disc, can't drive long haul, can't sit two hours, legs go numb."

"And now?"

"Helps me at home. Splits wood, tends the fire, moves things. Says there's nothing else he can do, but this is all right."

"All right" again. But this time from a man's mouth—not perfunctory, but a kind of acceptance. Accepting that his body can't do it anymore, accepting that he can't drive long haul. But he can still split wood, still tend the fire, still move things. That's enough.

She smiled a little, the corners of her mouth lifting, her eyes curving. It was very brief, about a second. When she smiled, deep fine lines appeared at the corners of her eyes, radiating outward—not lines you get in your forties, but lines accumulated from years of squinting and smiling, years of being steamed, years of staring at a pot of white balls.

She carried out a large iron basin, heaped with roasted peanuts, still giving off a little heat. She grabbed a handful, rubbed them between her palms, blew. The peanut skins flew up, fell to the ground, scattered everywhere.

She picked up a roasted peanut and handed it to me. I took it, shelled it, the skin rubbed right off. The peanut was pale yellow—not the golden of frying, but the pale yellow of roasting, a little matte. I bit in: crisp, fragrant, a little sweet.

Ah Fang—her husband had just called her name, that's how I learned it—walked back, picked a winter ball from the stove and handed it to me.

"Try it."

I took it. Hot, burning my hand; I tossed it from palm to palm. White, the "coconut flour" on the surface sticking to my fingers, a little tacky. I bit in: the skin very glutinous, not sticking to the teeth, soft, breaks at a bite, doesn't string, doesn't paste to the roof of the mouth. The filling was hot, the aroma of peanut and sesame mixed with the sweetness of sugar, melting in the mouth. The sugar had melted into syrup, not granules; the peanuts still crisp, the sesame crushed between the teeth, the aroma of the oil coming out last. Not cloyingly sweet—just right, sweet to the third bite and then it stops, after that the fragrance of peanut and the bitterness of sesame.

"Sweet?"

"Just right."

She gave an "mm," as if she'd gotten the answer she wanted.

She went on kneading the dough. The flour on her hands grew less as she kneaded, the dough smoother and smoother.

She put the last winter ball on the plate. The plate was full now, a white plate, one against another, like a light snow had just fallen.

She didn't say much, but after the word "do" came a motion—she picked up the plate, walked into the house, set it on the table beside the old woman. The old woman glanced at it, reached out and took one, bit into it.

"Mom, it's hot."

The old woman ignored her, chewed, her eyes narrowing a little. Not closing, but narrowing, the lids drawing toward the middle, the brows lifting at the sides. I knew that expression—it meant "that's right."

In the courtyard, the man finished splitting the last bundle of wood, leaned the axe against the wall, patted the wood dust off his hands, patted his trouser legs, walked to the stove, added a stick of wood to the fire. The flame leaped up with a "hu." The steam grew thicker, white, condensing into puffs in the cold air.

I stood at the courtyard gate. The wind from the East River blew over, carrying the smell of water, cool, but not piercing. It was winter's cold, but with the sweetness of glutinous rice in it.

I bought ten; Ah Fang packaged them, and I carried them toward the town center.

I had just gotten into the car the company had arranged when my phone buzzed. Xiao Yang: "My mom says come home for dinner on the winter solstice."

I thought for a moment, replied one word: "Okay."

## Section Twenty-Four: Lighting the Lamp

Before the sweetness of the winter balls had faded, the year's end arrived. After finishing Ah Fang's box of winter balls, Dongguan's New Year flavor took another step toward density—it seemed the footsteps of the New Year were drawing nearer, and all the waiting began to gather in the ancestral halls.

After coming back from Zhangmutou past the winter solstice, with the year's end approaching, the company had a lot of business, and I mostly didn't go out.

On the twenty-eighth of the twelfth lunar month I went back to Zhanjiang for the New Year. On the fourth day of the first month I was back in Dongguan.

From 2003 to now, the fifth year.

That crack in the ceiling was especially clear in the morning light—from the light fixture to the corner, winding, like a river. Where exactly did this river end?

The firecrackers outside were still going. The scattered kind, not one string after another, occasionally rolling over from the distance, like thunder.

I got up, dressed, opened the window. The sound was far away.

On the twelfth day of the first month, I saw a small colored lamp hanging on the balcony of a neighboring household.

Not the string of festival lights, but a single one, palm-sized, covered in gauze paper, a small candle lit inside. In daylight you couldn't see the light, but I recognized the shape—it was a lantern. The kind hung in ancestral halls.

I went downstairs and happened to run into Chen Ah Po, and only then learned that the family had moved here from Tangxia years ago, Hakka. That little lamp was not New Year decoration; it was "lighting the lamp."

One lamp, and behind it a whole set of things—clan, ancestral hall, incense, names.

Walking to the convenience store, Xiao Yang was behind the counter gnawing a steamed bun.

I asked her, "Free tomorrow?"

She looked at me like I was a stranger. True enough—I rarely initiated plans, barely said much. She was probably wondering if this guy had taken the wrong medicine.

"If you're free, let's go to Shipai tomorrow to see the lamp lighting."

"Other people's ancestral hall—will they let us in?"

"I'll find someone to take us in."

"Then I'll take tomorrow off."

The next morning, we parked at the entrance to Tangwei Village. The car was borrowed from Uncle Zhang, an old black Jetta, the AC not working well; we shrank our necks the whole way from the cold.

The firecrackers were loud. Under the old banyan at the village entrance, red paper scraps had already piled up; when the wind blew, the scraps swirled on the ground. The tree was very old, aerial roots hanging down like an old man's beard. A circle of red cloth was wrapped around the trunk, no telling when it had been tied, the edges already frayed.

A friend waited for us under the tree. He took us into the village. The alley was narrow, oyster-shell walls on both sides—those semicircular shell faces reflecting in the sun, some already broken, revealing the gray plaster inside. At the time I didn't know these walls were left from the Ming and Qing dynasties; I just thought: how did this wall get so many shells embedded in it?

The ancestral hall was in the middle of the village. A couplet pasted at the entrance, red paper, black characters. I didn't know what the couplet said, only that the characters were well written.

Walking in, quite a few people inside, gathered in twos and threes chatting about something. A smell in the air—not fragrant, but mixed: the wax of candles, the smoke of incense, and someone had brought roast goose, the oil aroma mixed in. These smells layered together, not exactly pleasant, not unpleasant either, just the particular smell of an ancestral hall.

Not much different from Uncle Liu's ancestral hall before—same layout, same smell. Ancestral halls, these things, probably look the same all over the country. Because what they hold is the same.

The lamps were framed with bamboo strips, covered with gauze paper, paper cuts pasted on—peonies, pomegranates, bats. Some lamps had paintings, painted with brush and pigment, the colors very pale, as if washed by water. I looked up; the lamps swayed slightly, candlelight shining through the gauze paper, like ripples on water.

Those hanging on the beams, and those not yet hung—each lamp was the arrival of a new life. Look at the old people in the ancestral hall, then look at the lamps on the beams—this seemed to be a transfer station of life.

An old man squatted beside the eight-immortals table. Red paper, scissors, paste spread on the table. He was cutting something, the scissors black, large. He cut very slowly, looking after each cut, like playing chess.

We stood nearby watching.

"What's he cutting?" I asked.

The old man looked up at me. Gray hair, thin, high cheekbones.

My friend said, "Double-happiness characters. Pasted on the lamps, for looks."

My friend had something to attend to and left first; we just stood there watching. Watched for about ten minutes; the old man finished one, picked up the brush, wrote a name on a red strip. The characters were small, but the strokes very steady.

"You cut every year?" I asked.

"Every year. My grandson's just been hung up; now I'm cutting for others."

Outside the ancestral hall the firecrackers went off again. A bigger string, the sound bouncing between the brick walls. We covered our ears; the old man didn't—he went on writing names, his hand didn't tremble.

I stared blankly for quite a while, not sure whether I felt joy or sorrow, probably both.

Xiao Yang tugged at me; only then did I come back to myself and follow her out of the ancestral hall.

Red was everywhere in the village. The couplets were red, the lanterns were red, the firecracker scraps were red, the children's clothes were red.

A child of two or three ran in the alley, a woman chasing behind, probably his mother. The woman shouted, "Slow down, don't fall!"

The child didn't slow, ran faster. The red clothes flashed in the alley, like a fish darting in water.

I followed the child for a stretch. He ran to a pomelo tree and stopped. A table was set under the tree, several pomelos on it. An old woman was picking pomelo leaves, and when she'd picked a handful, handed them to the person beside her. Each person took a few leaves, rubbed them between their palms.

"Ah Po, what's this for?" I asked.

"Washing away bad luck. Pomelo leaves soaked in water, wash your hands, clean for the year."

I was taken aback. The old woman glanced at us, handed over a few pomelo leaves.

"You rub too."

I took them, rubbed them between my palms. The leaves were dry, but rubbing released a smell—not fragrant, but clean, like freshly peeled pomelo skin, but fainter.

I tossed the leaves back on the table, said "thank you." She didn't respond, turned to help others rub leaves.

I walked to the old banyan at the village entrance. The firecrackers had finished; the bamboo pole was still there, bare. The ground was all red; stepping on it went "gezhi gezhi."

An old woman sat on the stone bench under the tree, a sweater before her. Dark blue. Just sitting there, watching the people on the road.

"Ah Po, who are you waiting for?"

"My grandson. He went to buy firecrackers."

"You come every year?"

"Mm. Since he could walk, every year on the thirteenth of the first month we come. Lively, the child likes it."

She didn't look at me, looked toward the road.

When we got in the car, I looked back. The old woman was still sitting there, the dark blue sweater on her lap. Two pomelo leaves lay on the ground beside the stone bench.

Looking back through the rearview mirror, the banyan grew smaller, the red grew fainter.

Back in Nancheng. At night, I stood on the balcony. The streetlight downstairs was broken; the whole neighborhood was dark. Firecrackers in the distance, very far, but constant.

I turned off my phone and lay down. The ceiling was invisible, but there seemed to be a touch of red, glowing all night.

## Section Twenty-Five: Bamboo Weaving

After coming back from Shipai, I was in a daze for a long time. The world runs fast; I follow slowly.

The propaganda boards by the road, the television—"new Dongguan people" was everywhere, blotting out the sky. "Migrant workers" had a new name—was that recognition? Shouldn't recognition mean the same *name* and the same meaning? I couldn't understand it.

My heart was in a mess, so I thought of the guanxiang incense slices. After Qingming, I went to Chen Ji's incense shop again, spent three hundred yuan, and it hurt.

Coming out of the incense shop, I wandered. In a side alley behind Yaxiang Street, I smelled bamboo.

Not fragrant—raw. Freshly split bamboo has a green, bitter smell, like the skin of a raw cucumber, but harder, sharper. Mixed with a bit of bamboo-dust powderiness, it itched my nose, made me want to sneeze.

At the end of the alley was a room with its door wide open. A pile of bamboo at the doorway—not whole poles, but already-split strips, of varying lengths, stacked against the wall. The green side facing out, some already turning yellow. A few finished pieces hung at the door: a bamboo basket, two winnowing baskets, a birdcage. The birdcage door was open; no bird inside.

An old man sat on a low stool at the door, splitting strips. In his hand was a bamboo splitting knife—not a kitchen knife, but a proper splitting knife, the blade narrow and long, thin as paper. He stood a strip of bamboo on his leg, the blade set in the middle of the strip, one hand holding the bamboo, the other pressing the back of the blade. "Ka"—the strip split down the middle. The sound was crisp, like biting through a stick of celery.

I stood nearby watching. He didn't look up, split another. Another "ka."

"Boss, your knife is very fast," I said.

"Sharpened it all morning."

He finally looked up at me. Sixty or so, hair half gray, combed neatly, swept back. Wearing a white vest, his shoulders tanned dark, a shade apart from his arms. His fingers were long, the knuckles thick, a thick callus on the web between thumb and index finger—not a thin callus, but the decades-old kind, like stone.

"Come in. Standing at the door you're blocking the light."

***

I walked in. Inside was darker than outside. A row of shelves against the wall, holding all sorts of bamboo ware: winnowing baskets, rice sieves, bamboo chairs, summer mats, lantern frames. Some finished, some half done. Bamboo shavings piled on the floor, a thick layer, soft underfoot, like walking on dry leaves.

The old man went on splitting strips. Each one split, he put the two halves on the left and right respectively. Green on the left, yellow on the right, clearly separated.

"Green bamboo is tough, used for basket handles, lantern frames. Yellow bamboo is brittle, used for bottoms, for dividers." He spoke as he split. Not explaining to me—habit. When someone watched, he talked; he'd been talking for decades.

"How long have you been doing this?"

"This? Since my twenties. Used to be at a bamboo factory, then the factory closed, so I do it at home. Make as many as sell. Now—" He paused. "Now fewer sell."

He set down the splitting knife, picked up a green strip and began scraping. The scraper was a curved iron piece mounted on a wooden handle. He held the strip in his left hand, the scraper in his right, drawing from one end to the other. A thin layer of green skin peeled off, curling into a fine strip, falling on his leg. After one pass, the strip's color went from deep green to pale yellow, smooth.

"Your craft—won't you take an apprentice?" I asked.

"I have."

"Where is he? Gone out?"

"Doesn't come anymore."

He didn't say why, but his hands slowed by a beat.

"Then family transmission—family learning is good too."

"My son would rather go to work; the income is higher than doing this."

"He doesn't know how?"

"He does. I taught him when he was little; he could weave a basket. But slowly. He said: 'Dad, I take three hours to weave one, you sell one for fifteen yuan.' I said: 'Then don't weave.'"

I laughed. His hands stopped.

"My granddaughter learned for a while too. Young, school comes first."

He went on: "She wove a coaster, palm-sized, took her two days. When it was done she took it home to show her mother."

"Will you teach her in the future?"

He didn't answer right away, picked up a strip and turned it over and over. The bamboo fibers gave off a very fine sheen in the dim light.

"No." He said it with certainty—not pessimism, but knowing. "Because her mother already told me: 'Dad, don't teach her this, she has to study, get into university.'"

"You didn't argue?"

"Argue about what. She's right." He set down that strip, picked up the next. Split. "Ka."

"Then what happens when you can't do it anymore? These—" I pointed at the bamboo ware on the shelves, "the things."

"These things?" He glanced at the shelves. "Nobody wants them. Who still uses a bamboo basket for groceries, everyone uses plastic bags. Who still uses a bamboo sieve for rice, everyone buys supermarket rice. Who still uses a bamboo chair? Everyone buys plastic."

"Then why do you still make them?"

He finished splitting that strip, separated the two halves, left green, right yellow, neatly arranged. "Because—" He thought for a long time. "Don't know what else to do. Idle hands don't know where to go."

***

He stood up, his knees went "ka." Sixty-year-old knees. He walked to the shelf, took down from the top a small bamboo-woven box, palm-sized, hexagonal, a pattern on the lid: a plum blossom. The petals woven in one by one, not painted on.

"This took me three days," he said. "The plum blossom needs four colors of strips—green, yellow, dyed red, dyed black. When changing strips you have to hit the position exactly; one wrong strip and it's crooked."

I picked up the hexagonal box. Very light, but sturdy. Opened the lid, empty inside, the inner wall smooth—the yellow bamboo side scraped very thin, polished again and again.

I set down the box. He handed me the scraper, then a strip, green side up.

"You scrape."

I took the scraper. My hand was a size bigger than his; the handle was worn shiny by his hand, a little slippery in mine.

I tried, drawing the scraper from one end of the strip to the other, not much came off, the green skin still there.

"Force is wrong." He said, "Not pushing, pressing. The blade edge against the strip surface, use your body weight to press down, not the strength of your hand."

I tried again, put the weight of my arm into it, pressed the blade down, a thin layer of green curled up, fell on my leg.

"Right. Like that."

I scraped one through, the strip turned pale yellow, smooth.

"Not bad." He said, "The one you scraped, enough for the edge of a coaster. Not bad."

Those two words again. But this time not perfunctory. When he said "not bad," his eyes were on the plum blossom box on the top shelf.

***

The ice cream from the corner shop didn't come back; what came back was a bottle of AD calcium milk. She—Lin Xiaoxiao, twelve, two ponytails, a long-sleeved school uniform.

She squatted beside me watching me scrape, watched for a few minutes, then pulled from her pocket a half-woven coaster.

"Grandpa said he'd teach me to change color today."

"Change to what color?"

"Red. Weave a plum blossom."

"In middle school now?"

"Mm, first year, in Songshan Lake. Our class has very few Dongguan people, lots of outsiders—oh no, lots of new Dongguan people."

The old man took down a small bundle of red-dyed strips from the shelf, very fine, a little thicker than a hair, and handed it to his granddaughter. Then he held her hand—a small hand wrapped in a big one—guiding her fingers, pressing the red strip into the half-woven coaster.

"Thread it through here. Press one, lift one. Right. Slowly."

Her fingers moved in his hand, very slowly, but every motion precise.

The sun moved in from the alley mouth, shone on the three of us. The old man's white hair was almost transparent in the light, the granddaughter's ponytails tied with red elastics. The birdcage with the open door hung on the wall, empty, as if waiting for a bird that hadn't come back.

I thought of the lamps in the Shipai ancestral hall—each one the arrival of a new life. Here there was no lamp, only a half-woven coaster, and a twelve-year-old girl, in her grandfather's hands, pressing a red strip into the right place.

When changing strips you have to hit the position exactly. One wrong strip, and it's crooked.

I don't know whether Lin Xiaoxiao will keep weaving in the future. Her mother says she has to get into university, her grandfather says "she's right." But at this moment, in this dim room, the sunlight moved in an inch, and she moved with it an inch. Her small hand wrapped in the big hand, the red strip threaded through, press one, lift one.

This is probably all of it: not transmission, but one afternoon.

Grandfather taught her for a day, she wove a coaster, took it home to show her mother. Her mother didn't say it was bad. Her mother just said, you have to study.

That afternoon stayed in the coaster. Palm-sized, press one lift one, with an unfinished plum blossom. It won't be hung in an ancestral hall, won't go on an "intangible heritage" list, no one will photograph it. It will be put in some drawer, or on some afternoon be picked up again by a twelve-year-old girl, woven a couple more stitches, put down again.

But it existed. On one afternoon, the sunlight moved in an inch, and she moved with it an inch.

I've been in Dongguan five years, seen too many things like this—Aunt Feng's winnowing baskets, Uncle He's jidi congee, Uncle Bing's dragon boats, Ah Ying's offering tables, Ah Fang's winter balls, and this plum blossom coaster. None will go into a museum. They live in a person's kitchen, workshop, drawer. Then the person grows old, or leaves, and the thing is gone.

But that afternoon is still there. Big hand holding small hand, red strip threaded through. This image won't disappear, because it's not an object—it's time itself.

I set down the scraper and took my leave. The old man didn't keep me, only said, "Come scrape again when you're free."

Walking out of the alley, the sunlight was harsh. People came and went on Yaxiang Street, the three words "new Dongguan people" reflecting light on some propaganda board.

I felt my pocket. No bamboo leaves, no strips, only a ring of keys and a few yuan. But my fingers seemed to still hold the feel of that strip—the green side cool, the yellow side rough.

***

Back in the rented room, I stood on the balcony. The streetlight downstairs was still broken, the whole neighborhood dark. Firecrackers in the distance, very far, but constant.

That crack in the ceiling was invisible in the dark, but I knew it was there. Five years, it hadn't changed, and I hadn't changed much either. Only in the crack there seemed to be a little more—not light, but red, yellow, green, strips of all colors, press one lift one, woven into the shape of a coaster.

A palm-sized, crooked, unfinished plum blossom.

I turned off the light and lay down. In the dark, I seemed to hear another "ka"—the old man splitting a strip, green on the left, yellow on the right, neatly arranged.

After that sound, a long silence.

## Section Twenty-Six: Meatball Congee

***

Before the "ka" of the bamboo strips had faded, the weather turned hot through and through. After a spell of quiet, the river sounded again—the Dragon Boat Festival had come, the drums driving people to the water. This time, not the lamps in the ancestral hall, not the strips in the hand, but the whole river surface moving.

On the day of the Dragon Boat Festival, by the time I reached Daojiao it was already past nine.

Both banks were packed with people. In slippers, in vests, in pajamas, in culture shirts printed with "Rural Credit Cooperative." Children on their fathers' shoulders, holding cotton candy. Old people on their own folding stools, a thermos and a folding fan before them. The thermos probably held goji berries or chrysanthemum; the fan printed with the character for "fortune."

The dragon boats hadn't come yet. But there was already something on the water—not dragon boats, but trash: plastic bottles, foam boxes, a slipper. The water was still yellow. Every year it's yellow, but this year it was a different yellow, a muddier yellow, like flour had been mixed in. I watched the surface, the silt swirling in the water, not in a hurry to sink, as if waiting for something.

A tall old man beside me said, "This year there seem to be a lot fewer outsiders, right?"

Another old man said, "Yeah, several factories in the village say they have no orders, all closed." As if talking about something that had nothing to do with him. In fact it probably really had nothing to do with him.

The tall old man said, "No wonder I see those community people running to the factories every day, afraid the outsiders will make trouble, right?"

The other old man didn't respond. He looked down, fished a pack of cigarettes from his trouser pocket, shook one out, lit it. It was Hongshuangxi. He took a drag, didn't exhale, let the smoke turn in his mouth before slowly letting it out. The smoke drifted onto the river, mixed with the water vapor.

I moved two steps to the side. Beside me was a middle-aged woman with two children.

The people standing by the river change every year. You think those faces will always be there, but they won't. They just suddenly don't come one year. Without a sound. Maybe they went back home, maybe they went to another city, maybe the children grew up and didn't need to ride on shoulders anymore. Faces change batch after batch, but the river doesn't change, the drums don't change, the river water doesn't change.

***

Ten o'clock. The drums sounded.

Not one drum, but a dozen drums at once, coming from far upstream. Dong. Dongdong. Dong—. The drumbeats ran across the water faster than the dragon boats; the sound arrived first, the boats after, like thunder walking ahead of rain. That low-frequency vibration came up through the soles of my feet, through my calves, my waist, my spine, finally stopping in the chest between my shoulder blades. Drumbeats aren't heard with the ears, but with the bones.

When the first dragon boat rounded the bend, the people on both banks began to shout. Not orderly cheering, but chaotic, each shouting their own: someone shouted "Daojiao—Daojiao—," someone shouted "Houde—Houde—," someone shouted "Ah Qiang—hurry up—," and someone was shouting a name I couldn't understand, Cantonese mixed with Hakka, and Mandarin too.

The dragon boat came. A man stood at the bow holding a long bamboo pole—not an oar, but the "bow pole," responsible for directing the rhythm at the bow. The paddlers on both sides were bare-chested, the muscles on their backs bulging with each stroke. When the oar blades entered the water they were as neat as a knife cutting down; the spray splashed up, hitting the faces of the first few rows of spectators. No one dodged.

The young man beside me—early twenties, wearing a black T-shirt with "Houde Village Dragon Boat Team" printed on the chest—was holding up his phone filming, the lens pointed at the water. Beside him a man in his forties—probably his father—was also filming, but with an old compact camera, a crack across the screen.

"Dad, yours comes out all blurry," the young man said.

"Blurry is fine. I'm filming for your mother to see."

"Mom can't see clearly on the camera."

"Transfer it to the computer, she can see clearly. She wears reading glasses."

Father and son didn't argue further, kept filming.

Dragon boat after dragon boat came. This wasn't a race, it was "chenjing"—the dragon boats of various villages visiting one another. You come to my village, I go to yours; each dragon boat arriving at the other village's dock must "kaobiao"—come ashore to eat dragon boat rice. After eating, row back. In one day, a boat visits three or four villages, the paddlers eat three dragon boat meals at noon. At the time I found it incredible; only later did I learn this was the proper thing: the race was secondary, the visiting was the core. You row to my dock, I treat you to a meal; you row to his dock, he treats you to a meal. One river strings together a dozen villages, one meal strings together several hundred people. This isn't a race, this is a way of living.

***

Two in the afternoon. Chenjing reached its liveliest. More people at the dock, someone setting off firecrackers—not the small kind, but the big red rolls, hung on bamboo poles, once lit going "piliplala" for a minute. The smoke was thick, white, mixed with the water vapor on the river; the whole dock seemed in fog. Someone choked on the smoke, coughed a few times, but didn't leave, eyes squinting, still looking at the river.

Over at the dock they began setting up tables: plastic tablecloths, disposable bowls and chopsticks, a dozen round tables. The food had been cooked ahead in the ancestral hall's big kitchen—basin cuisine, braised pork, white-cut chicken, fried rice noodles. And one thing every year: Daojiao meatball congee. The congee was gray, because the meatballs were hand-beaten, lots of starch, so the soup came out cloudy.

The paddlers sat on plastic stools, holding bowls, eating in big mouthfuls. Their shoulders were still red—not from sunburn, but from the oar handles rubbing while paddling. Some had band-aids on their shoulders, some had split the webbing of their hands, wrapped in white tape. But they ate with relish. Someone nearby was taking photos, the lens pointed at that bowl of gray congee.

I walked to the river. The sun was starting to slant west, the drums still sounding, another dragon boat rounding the bend upstream. The bow pole at the front raised high, the paddlers shouting—the shouts weren't lyrics, just "hey—yo—hey—yo—," but the rhythm matched the drums; when the drums slowed, the shouts slowed; when the drums sped up, the shouts sped up.

That slipper on the water was still floating, a plastic bottle now beside it. The water was still yellow, but in the afternoon the yellow was a shade lighter than at noon, the silt slowly sinking. The distance between the slipper and the bottle hadn't changed; they drifted downstream together, like two travelers who didn't know each other, happening to take the same road.

I turned and walked toward the street. By the road someone was clearing tables, the plastic tablecloth lifted at one corner by the wind, disposable bowls and chopsticks put into black garbage bags, tied tight. Tomorrow this river would go back to being an ordinary river, the dragon boats would sink back into the mud, the drums would be put away in the shed, the dock would be empty. But that gray congee, those shouts gone hoarse, those backs moving tables—they would stay in this fifth year's layer.

***

The sky darkened without my noticing; it seemed about to rain. A cool drop fell on my palm. It was rain, and also the water vapor off the river, swept up by the wind, carried to this street.

The rain came. Not the sky-blotting kind of dragon-boat rain, but as if someone had tipped a basin of water from the sky, the spray splashing everywhere, hitting the concrete and raising a layer of white mist, like someone setting off a string of dud firecrackers in the distance—only smoke, no sound.

By the time I ran to the little restaurant at the street corner, the water was already up to my ankles. Cool, with a muddy fishiness, like a riverbed just turned over.

A canopy was set up outside the shop, several round tables, close together. Under the canopy sat a large aluminum bucket of meatball congee, a layer of congee skin stuck to the inside, dried into white patches, like frost flowers on a winter window.

A woman stood by the table, in her forties, ladling congee into bowls. The aluminum ladle hit the bucket wall with a "kuangkuang" sound, like someone beating a cracked gong.

"How much a bowl?" I asked.

"Ten yuan."

I paid and sat down at a table to the side. Beside the congee bucket was a small piece of cardboard with "Meatball Congee" written in marker, the strokes very thick, like a child's writing.

She handed me a bowl of congee. Gray, steaming. Very hot; the first sip numbed my tongue, but the taste was right—the meatballs hand-beaten, lots of starch, the soup cloudy, but savory, salty. After the second sip, a faint sweetness rose from the base of my tongue, like the aftertaste left in the mouth after drinking meat broth as a child.

At the next table sat six or seven men who had just come in, bare-chested, their backs still wet—couldn't tell if sweat or rain. They didn't talk, heads down, eating hard. The sound of chopsticks hitting bowl rims, the sound of someone slurping congee, mixed together, like a symphony without a conductor, messy, but dense, every sound landing on the beat.

I asked the proprietress, "A festival like this, just you alone, can you manage?"

"My husband went to row the dragon boat. He rows the bow oar."

I gave an "oh," went on drinking the congee. The rain grew heavier, hitting the ground outside the canopy with a "papapa," like someone drumming a drumhead with their fingers. The banyan leaves were beaten down by the rain, each leaf hung with a string of water drops, as if someone had strung glass beads on the leaf tips.

In the rain, another dragon boat came to the dock. Green, the dragon head's teeth white plastic, the eyes two white plastic balls—the sun was hidden by clouds, the bulbs didn't reflect, but the dragon head still had its mouth open, as if shouting, only no one heard.

The paddlers came ashore, jumped off the boat, bare feet stepping into the water, spray splashing up. Someone handed towels, someone wiped their faces with their hands, water running down from their hair, down the groove of the spine, soaking a dark ring into the waistband at the waist, like a circle of ink blurring on rice paper.

The rain stopped. Not gradually, but suddenly, as if someone had turned off the tap. The air went quiet all at once, only the sound of water dripping from the eaves remained: one drop, two drops, three drops, like a clock.

***

I finished the last sip of congee, the bowl bottom up, a drop of congee clinging to the bowl wall, slowly sliding down.

Walking to the roadside, the water had receded some, but the ground was still wet, my soles going "patapat" on it, like stepping on a sponge full of water. The wind blew from the river, carrying the smell of water. Not the yellow of dragon-boat water, but the clear of after-rain, like someone had wrung out a dishcloth—but the fishiness of silt was still underneath, faint, like a memory that won't disperse.

My phone buzzed. Xiao Yang: "Was there chenjing today?"

"Mm. Ate dragon boat rice."

"Delicious?"

"Bowl bottom up. I'll pack a portion back for you."

She sent an emoji, a smiley face, a stick figure holding a little flag.

I laughed.

## Section Twenty-Seven: Mid-Autumn Lanterns

The drumbeats of the Dragon Boat Festival, the gray congee of dragon boat rice, steamed away by August's heat, seemed to pass. I couldn't remember how many festivals had passed in between; I only remember that on the fourteenth of the eighth month, the company dinner in Wanjiang broke up. At Haoxia Fang, someone at the table mentioned "new Dongguan people," someone mentioned "orders," I had nothing to say; after the dinner I didn't go back to the rented room, walked alone toward Gonglian.

Walking, I smelled wax oil. Not fragrant—warm, like someone had just blown out a row of candles, the smoke not yet dispersed.

Following the smell I turned into an old street. The street wasn't wide, old houses on both sides, ground-floor shops, second-floor residences. Most shops closed, roller shutters half down. An old man stood at a doorway, on a small low stool, using a bamboo pole to pick at lanterns. Not the LED plastic kind, but paper, covered in gauze paper—square, round, hexagonal, some painted with Chang'e, some with rabbits, some with nothing painted, just plain white paper. A small candle lit inside, the light showing through the paper, like a thin layer of moon.

I stopped to watch. A wire hook was tied to the end of his pole; he was hooking a lantern—not hanging it up, but taking it down. The candle inside had burned out, the paper surface blackened in a small patch. He took it down, set it by his foot, put up a new lantern, lit the candle, raised the pole to hang it back.

"Uncle, isn't Mid-Autumn tomorrow? Hanging lanterns this early?"

He glanced at me. Gray hair, wearing a pair of reading glasses, the arms tied with a black string hanging around his neck, the string a little frayed. Wearing a white undershirt, the collar loose, revealing the collarbone. Gray trousers, black cloth shoes, dust on the uppers.

"The fourteenth is when it starts." He said, "Shu zhongqiu—erecting the Mid-Autumn lanterns—not waiting till the fifteenth night. Starts the night of the fourteenth, hangs for three days, till the sixteenth."

"Shu zhongqiu"—first time I'd heard the phrase.

"What does it mean?"

"Shu means 'to erect.' The lanterns have to be stood up, hung high. In the old days they said the higher the better, the farther the light shines." He paused. "Can't do that now, not safe. All hung lower."

The pole raised the new lantern up; the candlelight flickered behind the gauze paper, as if coming alive.

He picked up the pole again. Three lanterns left at his feet. He hung them one by one, stepping back after each, looking up, adjusting the angle. After the last one, he leaned the pole against the wall, leaned himself against the chair at the doorway. A few drops of wax had fallen on the road, already dry, small patches, dark brown.

***

"You light candles every year? Not electric?"

"Electric doesn't look good. Candlelight moves; when the wind blows, the shadow wavers on the paper, like it's alive."

He turned and went inside, came out with something in his hand—a very small lantern, palm-sized, hexagonal, a rabbit painted on the paper. Painted very roughly, the lines crooked, like a child's drawing.

"My granddaughter painted it." He said, "She's six, in kindergarten in Liaobu. Came to stay a month in the summer, painted this, told me to hang it up for Mid-Autumn."

He set the little lantern on the railing, didn't hang it high, just set it there. A small candle lit inside too, the light very weak, but the rabbit on the paper was visible, ears long, crooked.

"They're coming for Mid-Autumn?"

"Coming. Son and daughter-in-law both coming for dinner."

The wind blew from the street entrance; the lantern swayed, the candle shadow wavered on the gauze paper, like water ripples. That crooked-eared rabbit swayed in the light too, as if nodding.

"You're from elsewhere?"

"Mm, been in Dongguan five years."

"Are you leaving too? I hear lots of factories, lots of companies are closing."

"Not leaving for now." I looked up at the lanterns—second-floor balconies, ground-floor doorways, trees by the road, utility poles, dozens of them, yellow, white, orange, red, some swaying, some still. The paintings on the paper—Chang'e, rabbits, flowers and birds, the character for "fortune"—faintly visible in the candlelight. The nearest lantern had a bird painted on it, couldn't tell what kind, but the lines of the wings were fluid, in the wavering light seeming to flap. "Anyway, I'm still here this year."

"Half the tenants in my building upstairs have left."

"The building I rent in, fewer people too."

He didn't respond, looked down and lit a cigarette, the tip glowing once in the dark.

I leaned against the utility pole. First Mid-Autumn, I ate lotus-seed-paste mooncake alone; the eve of the fifth Mid-Autumn, I stood beside an old man, watched him hang a few lanterns, watched his granddaughter's painted rabbit tilt its ears in the candlelight.

The candle in the lantern flickered, the shadow on the paper wavered with it, as if alive.

***

A firecracker sounded in the distance. Not the big rolls of the Dragon Boat Festival, but small, one string, maybe a dozen shots, "piliplala" for a few seconds, then quiet.

"Whose family sets off firecrackers this early?" I asked.

"Someone on the next street had a baby boy, celebrating Mid-Autumn early—counts as added joy."

He pointed into the distance. There were lanterns over there too, but fewer than here, only a few, each one big. The biggest hung right in the middle of the doorway, bigger than a basketball, characters written on the paper, too far to read.

"That's the lamp for lighting the lamp." He said, "Families that had a baby boy this year also hang a lamp at Mid-Autumn—double joy."

I suddenly thought of the flower lanterns in the Tangwei ancestral hall during the first month, the dozen names on the beams. Those lamps should have been taken down by now, carried home by each family. At Mid-Autumn, hung again? From the ancestral hall to the balcony, from the first month to the eighth, one lamp had walked most of a year's road.

Someone walked past. A woman leading a child. The child held a very small lantern—not paper, but plastic, LED inside, blue light, very bright, but unchanging, not wavering.

The child looked up at the paper lanterns above, paused.

"Mom, those lanterns move."

"That's candlelight. The wind."

"Why doesn't ours move?"

"Because ours isn't a candle."

The child gave an "oh," walked on.

The old man watched them go, tossed his cigarette butt past the road. The butt glowed once, went out.

***

I walked a stretch. By the road someone was selling lanterns, not paper, but plastic, LED, five yuan each. The vendor was a young man, in his twenties, dozens laid out before him, all shapes: rabbits, stars, moons, little cars, the light very bright, blue, red, green.

A child squatted beside him, three or four, choosing. Finally picked a rabbit-shaped one, pressed the switch, the rabbit's belly lit up, red. He held it up, shone it at his own face, a layer of red light on his face.

I walked on. Candlelight wavered behind paper, LED light held steady behind plastic. Two things existing at once: one growing old, one growing up. Neither replaced the other. Only—tomorrow the fifteenth, there would be more lanterns.

The moon hadn't come out yet, today was overcast. But the lantern light was enough.

## Section Twenty-Eight: Turning Pinwheels

The lantern light wavered for half a month; once the autumn wind came, it was time for something else to turn. The ninth of the ninth month—Double Ninth Festival—at Machong. Half of the road along the Machong riverbank was closed—not for construction, but for stalls. Along one side of the entire road, one after another, all of them pinwheels.

Not the plastic kind children run with, but paper: bamboo frames, covered with colored crepe paper, round ones, bagua-shaped ones, some made into lotus-petal shapes. The smallest were palm-sized, the largest taller than a person—the tall ones aren't for holding, but for sticking in the ground, or tying to the back of a bicycle.

When the wind blew, several hundred pinwheels turned at once. The sound wasn't loud—the "shasha" of paper surfaces rubbing against each other, like wind blowing through a bamboo grove when spring has just sprouted.

Today was the Double Ninth Festival. Machong people don't climb heights—there are no mountains here—they observe "turning the pinwheels."

I asked an old woman by the road. She said: "To turn your luck."

I thought she was joking. Only later did I learn that when the pinwheels turn, they turn away bad luck and turn in good luck. Not superstition, but a sense of ritual, like pasting the character for "fortune" upside down at New Year—you know it might not work, but you still do it.

I stood by the road watching the pinwheels turn.

The vendors were mostly people my father and mother's age. Before them were laid out dozens of traditional bagua pinwheels, thick bamboo frames, thick paper surfaces, the colors bright red and green, going "huhu" in the wind. Those techniques, those color schemes, probably hadn't changed in a dozen or several dozen years.

But there was one exception—the stall closest to the street entrance.

***

A young woman in her twenties, ponytail, the ends of her hair a little frizzy, wearing a denim jacket washed pale, the cuffs frayed to white threads. The pinwheels before her were different from the others: not the traditional round bagua, but shapes she had designed herself—a butterfly, the wings of translucent tracing paper, patterns printed on them. When the wind blew, the butterfly's wings turned, the patterns overlapped, becoming new colors.

"How much for this?"

"Thirty-five."

"Why more expensive than the others?"

"Because theirs are machine-pasted, mine are hand-pasted. Takes over half an hour each."

She picked up a butterfly, turned it. The pattern on the wings went from red overlapping into purple, then from purple overlapping into blue. Her fingers were very slender, nails cut very short, on the pads of her fingers some fine red marks, paper cuts—from cutting the tracing paper carelessly. When she turned the butterfly her wrist was very steady, as if turning something very precious.

She set down the butterfly, pointed at it. "Started making them this year. Before I made the traditional ones, bagua-shaped. My dad told me to make traditional ones. I said 'Dad, yours don't sell anymore.' He said 'what do you know.' I said 'I know the times.'"

She lowered her head to adjust the angle of the butterfly's wings. The tracing paper was very thin; behind the paper her finger outlines showed, like through a layer of mist. After adjusting, she set the butterfly back on the stall, lined it up with the others. Each wing faced the same direction, like a row of troops awaiting inspection.

I looked behind the stall. A man in his sixties sat in a folding chair—the old green canvas kind, the frame already rusted. A thin blanket covered his knees, plaid, the edges pilled. In his hand a small knife, shaving bamboo strips.

The knife was the paper-cutting kind, the blade very thin, pushed along the surface of the strip, shaving off a thin layer of bamboo skin. His hand was very steady, but much slower, resting a second after each shave. The strip turned an angle in his hand, another shave. The shaved bamboo skin was pale yellow, curling, falling on the blanket.

"My dad," the young woman said. "Rest a bit, you've shaved twenty, that's enough."

He handed the strip over. She took it, the cut end rough; she scraped it with her fingernail, removing the burr, the motion very practiced, as if done many times.

"He fell on a construction site a few years ago, hurt his knee, now he can't even walk smoothly."

She pulled a small tube of ointment from her pocket, a white plastic tube, the three characters for "analgesic balm" printed on it, the characters already worn a little blurry. Twisted off the cap, squeezed some onto her finger, bent over, pressed it on his knee through the blanket. Her fingers circled around the knee, the pressure neither light nor heavy.

The man didn't dodge, didn't say "no need." Just sat there, hands on his knees, the veins on the back of his hands thick, like one river after another.

"What's your surname?"

"Ouyang."

"The craft of making pinwheels, did you teach her?"

"Mm." The young woman said, "Learned from childhood—bagua-shaped, round, lotus-shaped, he knows them all. The butterfly I figured out myself."

She stood up, walked back to the stall, picked up a butterfly and turned it. The red and blue on the wings overlapped, becoming purple, the purple very faint in the light, like a small patch of mist.

"My dad says butterflies don't look like pinwheels. I say a pinwheel just turns, and a butterfly turning is also turning. He says 'yours is too flashy.' I say 'yours is too old-fashioned.' He didn't argue further—"

He was still shaving bamboo strips, the knife pushing with a "sisi" sound, a thin layer of bamboo skin curling up, falling on the blanket. Head down looking at the work in his hands, didn't look up, but the corner of his mouth seemed to move. Not a smile, but the feeling of being talked back to by his daughter and not wanting to admit it.

"How are your sales this year?"

She glanced at the stall. Behind her were still a dozen butterflies, unsold. The wind turned the nearest one two circles, then it stopped.

"Not bad."

"Not bad" again. But this time not perfunctory. When she said those two words, her eyes were on her father—on the knife in his hand pushing the strip bit by bit. The speed very slow, but every cut shaved evenly. The strip in his hand grew finer and finer, tougher and tougher, wouldn't break when bent into a circle. That was decades of skill.

She pointed under the stall. A cardboard box, filled with bubble wrap and courier bags, a row of tape already cut, each about fifteen centimeters, neatly stuck to the edge of the box, like a row of little tongues.

"Online orders, I pack them when I get home at night."

"People buy them online too?"

"Some. Not many. All from other provinces, say they saw them as children, want to show their kids what real pinwheels look like."

She picked up a butterfly, handed it to me.

"Aren't you going to buy one?"

***

I handed over the money, took the butterfly. The paper very light, the pattern red and blue. I turned the handle—the handle was bamboo, shaved very smooth, warm in the hand. The wings turned, red and blue overlapped, became purple.

The wind blew from the Machong riverbank, carrying the smell of water. Not the yellow of dragon-boat water, but autumn water, cool, clean. When the wind passed several hundred pinwheels, the paper surfaces sounded at once—shashasha, shashasha, like several hundred people turning the pages of a very old, very old book at the same time.

I walked on. By the road someone was packing up.

That day the pinwheel turned all day at the convenience store entrance. Was the bad luck turned away? Don't know. But new things did turn in—a butterfly made by Ouyang, the fine strips her father shaved, the tape cut on the courier bags. The old was growing old, the new was growing.

The sun began to slant west. The pinwheels' shadows stretched long on the ground. A butterfly's shadow fell on my shoe. Purple.

After Double Ninth, the pinwheels were put away, and the days grew colder day by day. Winter didn't come fast, but it came. The New Year passed, the Lantern Festival passed too. Xiao Yang said a couple days ago that sour preserved plums were good; I thought I'd bring some back on the way. Happened to be handling business near Shilong today, walked toward the old street after it was done.

## Section Twenty-Nine: Dried Fruits

After the Lantern Festival, 2009 · Shilong

The pinwheels had turned away the bad luck, and the new year began again. My seventh year in Dongguan.

Shilong's old street is by the river. Arcades next to arcades, the gray on the pillars layered year after year, rain can't wash it off, sun can't crack it. Below, all sorts of small shops—shoe repair, general goods, tailor, herbal tea. And one selling preserved fruits. There were still traces of the New Year on the street.

The shop had no signboard. Not that it wasn't hung, but it was hung and the characters had fallen off. Originally it should have been "×× Preserved Fruits," four characters, now only one "fruit" remained, white background red character, plastic board, corners curled up. I stood at the door looking for a while, didn't go in.

Inside was dark, darker than under the arcade. My eyes took a few seconds to adjust—a row of glass jars against the wall, big and small, the tall ones to the waist, the short ones to the knees, filled with dark-colored things: black-brown olive kernels, dark yellow tangerine peel, brown-red dried star fruit, dark green licorice lemon. A layer of condensation on the outside of the jars, like beer bottles just out of the fridge.

A woman sat behind the counter, in her forties, short hair, the ends flipping outward a little, as if not combed. Wearing a loose navy T-shirt, the collar loose, revealing a small mole beside her collarbone. She didn't look at me, head down shelling something—only later did I learn it was olive kernels, the kernel inside the black olive pit. She tapped the pit lightly with a small iron hammer, tapped out a crack, then pried it open with her fingernail, took out the kernel. The kernel was white, like a small almond.

She tapped very slowly, pausing a second after each tap, listening for the "feel." Tap too hard and the kernel breaks, tap too light and the shell won't open. She tapped about seven or eight times before opening one. The kernel taken out, placed in a small dish. A dozen or so already in the dish, white, arranged neatly.

"What are you buying?"

She didn't look up, hand still tapping the next pit.

I said, "Just looking. Haven't had much appetite lately."

"Then look all you like."

I walked to the glass jars, looked one by one. The jar at the very edge was preserved olives—black, wrinkled, like dried plums. Next was tangerine peel steeped in fritillary-loqat paste, the color so dark it was almost black, a sticky sugar frost on the surface. Next was preserved plums, very small, not much bigger than a thumbnail, salt frost on the surface, a white layer.

"How much are these preserved plums?"

"Just in." She finally looked up at me. "Made last winter, marinated half a year, just right now. Twenty-five per liang."

"Weigh me two liang."

She grabbed a handful from the jar, put them on a piece of oil paper, weighed, wrapped, handed over. Her hand was very dry, the knuckles a little thick—not the thin callus from holding tweezers like Ah Ying, but from years soaked in sugar-salt water: the finger pads white, the lines shallow, like paper soaked too long.

"How long has this shop been open?"

"This shop? Fifteen years."

"Is the shop passed down in the family?"

"Was at a factory. Making shoes. Eight years."

She looked at the opened pit in her palm, threw it into the small bamboo basket by her foot. Dozens of pits already piled in the basket, all empty.

"Hands hurt. Making shoes you bend over all day, use glue, strong smell. Later the finger joints swelled, in the morning couldn't make a fist. Quit."

"Then opened this shop?"

"Mm."

She sat back behind the counter, picked up the next pit, tapped lightly with the small hammer. One tap. Too hard. "Pa," the pit split in two, the kernel shattered too, white fragments falling on the tabletop.

She stopped, looked at the fragments, looked for a few seconds. Then put down her hand, picked up the next pit. This time tapped very lightly—one, pause; two, pause; three, the shell opened, the kernel whole.

"Preserved plums take half a year to marinate, tangerine peel three years, preserved olives a month, dried star fruit three days to dry—depends on the weather. Bad weather and it molds."

I said, "The weather's been very good this year, not much rain."

"Yeah, after the dragon-boat rains the weather's been good, good for drying star fruit."

She stood up, pulled a bamboo sieve from under the counter. A layer of tangerine peel just taken from the jar to air spread on the sieve, the surface still damp. She carried the sieve to the shop entrance, set it between the arcade pillars—where there was wind, but no sun.

"Tangerine peel needs to dry in the shade, can't be sunned directly, direct sun cracks it."

After moving the sieve, she patted the fragments off her hands. Her hands were still dry, white.

I stood at the shop entrance. People walked by on the street. An old woman carrying a vegetable basket—a bunch of water spinach, a block of tofu, a few eggs. She glanced at the preserved fruit shop as she passed, didn't stop. Took a few steps, looked back again. Then walked on.

"She used to come often." The proprietress sat back behind the counter. "Every time bought two liang of preserved olives, said 'to make soup for my son.' Her son is in Shenzhen, she doesn't eat them herself, says too salty."

She was probably talking about the old woman who had just walked past.

She lowered her head, began tapping pits again. This time didn't count the taps, her hand knew on its own.

I stood there watching for a while. The shop was very quiet, the sounds from outside came in—car horns, someone's television in the distance—none of it noisy, mixed with the "shasha" of tapping pits, like a very slow beat.

Walking out of the shop. The wind under the arcade was cooler now, the tangerine peel in the bamboo sieve slowly drying, the edges curling up a little, like paper scorched by fire.

I walked a stretch of the old street. Passed a tailor's shop—door closed, "Out for lunch, back at two" pasted on the glass. Passed a herbal tea shop—three people in line at the door, all waiting for "bansha." Passed a general store—a few plastic stools at the door, two old men sitting playing chess, the pieces slapping the board "papa."

At the end of the street was the river. The East River bends here, the water a little clearer than upstream—but still yellow. A few boats moored on the river, not dragon boats, but sand barges. The engine sound was far, but constant, like a heartbeat that won't stop.

My seventh year in Dongguan. The New Year had just passed, the weather still cold, the river wind coming through under the arcade, carrying a bit of damp. The bag of preserved plums in my hand was already open, the sour smell drifting out, mixed with the fishiness of the river wind. I bit into one—sour, then sweet, then astringent, finally no taste at all, only the sides of my tongue surging with saliva again and again.

I spat out the pit. The pit was brown, small, fell on the concrete, didn't bounce, just stopped there.

Walking back, the preserved fruit shop was still there. Whether the door was closed I couldn't see clearly. But I knew she was still inside, the sound of tapping pits still going.

That sound was like rain. Like the rain after the dragon-boat rains stop—not raining, but the ground still wet.

## Section Thirty: Sweet Soup

That preserved plum pit still had a bit of astringency on the tip of my tongue, and the days slid to April 2009. Xiao Yang said there was a sweet soup shop in Chashan that made something delicious, and dragged me to Nanshe again.

Nanshe is an ancient village from the Ming and Qing dynasties. I only learned that later—when I got off the bus all I saw was a cluster of ancestral halls and old houses, gray brick walls, dark tile roofs, stone drums squatting at the doorways. A paifang at the village entrance, carved with four characters: "Nanshe Ancient Village." The characters were new, the stone old. I hadn't noticed last time.

She pulled me through a narrow alley. Old houses on both sides, some half collapsed, some still lived in. A washing machine set at a doorway, the hose running straight to the drainage ditch in the alley. The water ran "huahua," frothy white.

At the end of the alley was a room. The door was wooden, old, a patina on the surface, should be smooth to the touch—but I didn't touch it. The door was half open, light coming from inside. Not lamp light, but stove-fire light.

I pushed the door open.

***

The stove was on the left. Brick-built, two large iron woks on top. One wok sent up steam, the other had its lid on, steam coming from the seam too. The firewood in the stove burned bright, orange-red tongues licking the bottom of the woks. A woman squatted before the stove, adding a stick of wood. The wood was lychee, gives off a sweet smell when burned.

She heard footsteps, didn't look up.

"Sit."

Only one table in the room, four plastic stools. The table was folding, stainless steel tube frame, white plastic top, a crack at the corner, taped with clear tape. The tape had yellowed, the edges curling, a bit of dust stuck to it. I pulled out a stool and sat. The legs were uneven, wobbled. I shifted position, put my weight on three legs, then it was steady.

"What are you eating?"

I asked, "What do you have?"

"Sweet potato soup, mung bean paste, mung bean with kelp, bean-curd skin with egg, sesame paste. Today also mango pomelo sago."

Her tone was very flat when she said these, like reading a menu, or reciting a poem she'd read many times. Each name she said, her hand moved unconsciously—not gesturing, but her fingers rubbing on the apron, or pinching the corner of her clothes.

Xiao Yang said, "Two sweet potato soups."

She gave an "mm," stood up, walked to the stove, stirred the steaming wok with a long-handled ladle. The sweet potato chunks tumbled in the syrup, orange-red, already cooked very soft.

She filled a bowl. Not a small bowl—a large sea bowl, white porcelain, a ring of blue pattern around the rim, a few chips revealing the white body inside. The syrup filled it to the brim, a drop hanging on the rim, about to fall but not falling.

She carried it over, set it before me. Then from the stove she took a small dish with a few slices of ginger. She picked up a slice and put it in my bowl.

"Better with ginger. Drives out the cold."

***

She didn't fill one for herself, leaned against the stove standing. Forty or so, round face, skin on the dark side, sweat on her forehead—from the stove fire. Wearing a dark blue apron, a few drops of syrup stains on the front, dried into sticky marks.

"How long have you been open?"

"This stove?" She pointed at the stove. "Built last year. The place I rented in the village before was demolished, moved here, cheaper here."

"I mean the sweet soup shop."

"Twelve years."

"You do it alone?"

"Alone. Buy ingredients in the morning, come back, wash, cut, cook. Open in the afternoon, sell till eight or nine at night."

She walked to the wok with the lid, lifted it. Inside was mung bean paste, deep green, already cooked to a paste, a layer of bean skin floating on the surface. She stirred in the middle with the ladle, the bean paste turned up like mud.

The skin on the back of her hand was very rough—not the thin callus of Ah Ying, not the white soaked hand of the dried fruit shop proprietress, but the kind that comes from years dealing with fire: large pores, a layer of fine cracks on the surface, like a dried riverbed.

She walked back to the stove, added a stick of wood. The lychee wood went "pipi" once.

"Sister Xie, where are your sweet potatoes from?"

"From Zengcheng. Every week I go to Shijie wholesale market to get them, have to pick the red-hearted kind, yellow-hearted isn't sweet enough."

She set down the ladle, walked to the sweet potato soup wok, stirred again. The sweet potato chunks turned over, revealing the orange-red core inside.

"Don't your kids come back to help?"

"One in Guangzhou, one in Shenzhen, where would they find the time."

"Is business all right?"

"Barely enough for living expenses. No storefront, in an old house, no one finds it."

"And in the future?"

Her stirring hand paused for a second.

"Finish this year, next year won't open."

"Then income?"

"Daughter and son say they'll give me money every month."

***

I finished that bowl of sweet soup. The bottom of the bowl was indeed clean—not a grain of mung bean, not a piece of sweet potato skin, not a drop of syrup clinging to the bowl wall. I set the bowl on the table, bottom up.

She saw it, said nothing, the corner of her mouth lifted again.

We walked out of that room. The alley was dark, the sky about to go dark. The orange-red light of the stove fire leaked from the half-open door, fell on the gray brick wall, like a piece of red-hot iron pressed against the wall.

We walked toward the alley entrance. Passing the washing machine, the water still running, the white foam already turned gray, the water in the drainage ditch going "gugu."

From deep in the alley came the sound of television, someone playing Cantonese opera—couldn't hear clearly what, but the tune was familiar, the kind only in old operas: slow, low, carrying a kind of resigned gentleness.

Out of the alley, the Nanshe Ancient Village paifang had become a dark silhouette in the dusk.

Xiao Yang suddenly said, "Next year we won't be able to eat it."

I didn't say anything.

The wind at the alley mouth was a little stronger, carrying a smell—was it the sweetness of the lychee wood drifting from Sister Xie's stove, or the ginger of that bowl of sweet potato soup? Couldn't tell. That smell didn't stay long in the wind, was covered by other smells—car exhaust, the oil smoke of the roadside barbecue stall, and the incense smell drifting from afar.

***

Back at the rented room, I learned that Chen Ah Po had passed away a few days ago, and the landlady now was her daughter.

People come and go—what thing will always wait for you in the same place? Time grows old, things disappear. What remains is obsession, or unwillingness to let go?

The days seem to be one day copying another, one person copying another. This is what life looks like.

I can't speak grand truths, can only watch time flow away, slowly recording one ordinary day after another.

***

But I'm moving too—found a new job in Dongcheng, Xiao Yang found a job too.

Lying in bed, looking at the crack in the ceiling, saying one sentence: thank you for keeping me company for six years, old friend.

My life goes on, the road must still be walked. Changing places, might be another Dongguan.

Dongguan, you are a fast city that makes people "slow down." Your fastness, the whole world has seen; your slowness, I want to record on your behalf.

You keep being fast. But please—also keep being slow. Because it's precisely those who don't rush who let those who rush know: only when you arrive somewhere, is it called home.

